{"id":"biobuzz-sweeper-intake","name":"BIOBUZZ sweeper roller intake (POLLEN and NECTAR)","aliases":["wide front sweeper","front roller intake","single-roller POLLEN intake","dual-roller POLLEN intake","over-under roller intake","full-width intake"],"category":"game","tags":["intake","pollen","nectar","floor pickup"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"roller-intake","parent":"roller-intake","type":"roller-intake","short":"A wide spinning roller across the robot's front that sweeps POLLEN and NECTAR balls off the floor and into the robot.","how":"A motor spins one or two horizontal rollers with flexible flaps or tubing about one ball-height off the tiles. Each roller pinches a ball against the floor (one roller) or against the second roller (two rollers) and pulls it inward. From there the ball goes to storage, a conveyor or a hopper, then an indexer or gate, then the shooter. The gap must fit both 2.8 in. POLLEN and 3.6 in. NECTAR, so flexible flaps are usual.","uses":["collecting scattered POLLEN","collecting NECTAR the drive team enters in the LOADING ZONE","picking up POLLEN that falls out of a FLOWER's bottom"],"pros":["touch-it-own-it over a wide front","simple: one motor","flexible flaps forgive the two ball sizes"],"cons":["can pull in a 5th element and break G407 (4 max)","can pull in opponent NECTAR (G408) unless you sort it out or drive carefully","wide rollers stick out and get hit","Do not catch or deflect elements falling from a tipped HIVE before they touch the floor (G409)."],"failures":["flaps wear or tear","two balls jam side by side at the entrance","roller too fast, so balls bounce off","the gap that works for NECTAR is too loose for POLLEN"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"roller rotation that pulls balls inward"},"params":[{"key":"rollerDiameter","label":"Roller diameter (with flaps)","unit":"mm","min":25,"max":100,"default":50,"estimate":true},{"key":"gapToFloor","label":"Roller center height above tiles","unit":"mm","min":40,"max":110,"default":75,"estimate":true,"note":"POLLEN is about 71 mm and NECTAR about 91 mm across"},{"key":"width","label":"Intake width","unit":"in","min":6,"max":18,"default":14,"estimate":true},{"key":"rollerRpm","label":"Roller speed","unit":"rpm","min":200,"max":1600,"default":900,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R105","G407","G408","G409"],"note":"Checked against the TU08 manual for size, the 4-element limit and opponent NECTAR. Legality still depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Intakes (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/types-of-intakes.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Principles of an Intake (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/principles-of-an-intake.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML) TU08","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/types-of-intakes.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/11115-intake1.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), FTC 11115 Gluten Free, Rover Ruckus surgical tubing intake (not a BIOBUZZ robot)"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":0},
{"id":"biobuzz-side-roller-intake","name":"BIOBUZZ opposed side-roller intake","aliases":["side-roller intake","opposed vertical rollers","vertical wheel intake","compliant wheel side intake","independent left/right rollers"],"category":"game","tags":["intake","pollen","nectar","centering"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"roller-intake","parent":"roller-intake","type":"roller-intake","short":"Two rows of vertical spinning wheels, one on each side of a mouth, that grab a ball between them and pull it in.","how":"Two vertical shafts of compliant or grip wheels spin in opposite directions and squeeze a ball between them. They pull it into the robot and center it for a single-file conveyor or indexer that leads to the shooter. Each side can have its own motor or servo so the robot can steer a ball or spit one out. The gap sets compression: about 71 mm POLLEN and 91 mm NECTAR need sprung arms or a gap that suits both.","uses":["single-file feeding into a conveyor or spindexer","taking in balls one at a time to respect the 4-element limit","pulling POLLEN out of the FLOWER's bottom Retrieval Opening (goBILDA's StarterBot uses a wheel intake for this)"],"pros":["centers balls for a single-lane transfer","one ball at a time is easy to count","compliant wheels grip well"],"cons":["narrow mouth, so you must drive onto the ball","the two ball sizes need spring-loaded arms","two shafts to support"],"failures":["the gap is too wide and POLLEN slips","the gap is too tight and NECTAR stalls the motor","wheels glazed with wear","one side slips and the ball rotates out"],"dof":1,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"one or two motors or CR servos","output":"opposed wheel rotation that pulls a ball inward"},"params":[{"key":"wheelDiameter","label":"Wheel diameter","unit":"mm","min":50,"max":100,"default":72,"estimate":true},{"key":"gap","label":"Gap between wheel rows","unit":"mm","min":55,"max":90,"default":66,"estimate":true,"note":"less than POLLEN's 71 mm for grip. Use spring-loaded arms if NECTAR must also pass"},{"key":"wheelRpm","label":"Wheel speed","unit":"rpm","min":150,"max":1200,"default":600,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["G407","G408","G418"],"note":"Removing POLLEN from a FLOWER is allowed only from the bottom (G418). Check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Intakes (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/types-of-intakes.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"FTC StarterBot Resource Guide (2026-2027) (goBILDA)","url":"https://www.gobilda.com/ftc-starter-bot-resource-guide-2026-2027-season/","kind":"vendor"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/types-of-intakes.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/9971-intake.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), FTC 9971 LanBros, Relic Recovery spring-loaded compliant wheel intake (not BIOBUZZ)"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":0},
{"id":"biobuzz-ramp-intake","name":"BIOBUZZ deployable ramp intake with rollers","aliases":["pivoting drop-down intake","deployable intake ramp","dual-stage intake"],"category":"game","tags":["intake","deploy","pollen","nectar"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"deployable-intake","parent":"deployable-intake","type":"deploy-intake","short":"An intake that starts folded inside the 18 in. cube, then swings down to make a ramp whose rollers push balls up into the robot.","how":"A servo or small motor pivots the intake frame from stowed (up) to deployed (down), so the robot fits the 18 x 18 x 18 in. starting cube (R102) and then reaches past the bumper. A spinning roller at the ramp's tip grabs balls. A second roller or belt stage carries them up the ramp into storage, then an indexer, then the shooter. Tune the deployed angle so the tip clears the floor by less than a ball height.","uses":["reaching POLLEN in front of the bumper","a two-stage intake that lifts balls to a high conveyor","stowing to protect the intake when not in use"],"pros":["reach after the start without breaking the start-size rule","a ramp lifts balls without a separate conveyor","stowing protects it from hits"],"cons":["extra joint and actuator","the deployed reach still has to fit the 18 x 24 in. expansion footprint (R105; the starting cube is R102)","rollers on a pivot need a belt or chain through the pivot"],"failures":["the servo is overloaded holding the ramp down against the floor","the pivot slops so the tip height drifts","the belt through the pivot jumps teeth"],"dof":2,"actuation":"motor+servo","motion":{"input":"servo pivot plus roller motor","output":"ramp rotation (deploy) and roller spin (pull)"},"preset":{"states":{"up":0,"down":100},"upper":110,"lower":0,"speed":180},"params":[{"key":"deployAngle","label":"Deploy angle from stowed","unit":"deg","min":60,"max":130,"default":100,"estimate":true},{"key":"rampLength","label":"Ramp length","unit":"in","min":4,"max":12,"default":7,"estimate":true},{"key":"rollerRpm","label":"Roller speed","unit":"rpm","min":200,"max":1400,"default":800,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R102","R105","G416"],"note":"The deployed ramp must stay within the expansion limits during the match. Legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Transfers: flip-up transfer (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Principles of an Intake (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/principles-of-an-intake.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML) TU08","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":1},
{"id":"biobuzz-kicker-intake","name":"BIOBUZZ roller intake with servo kicker","aliases":["kicker intake","flicker intake","pusher intake","paddle-assisted intake"],"category":"game","tags":["intake","inside","transfer","kicker"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"roller-intake","parent":"roller-intake","type":"kicker","short":"A roller intake plus a servo paddle that flicks each caught ball up into the robot's storage or shooter path.","how":"The roller pulls a ball into a pocket. A servo-driven paddle then swings from rest to kick and pushes the ball up or sideways into a hopper, conveyor or spindexer slot, then returns. This replaces a second conveyor stage with one servo stroke. Downstream the path is storage, then gate, then flywheel.","uses":["lifting a ball from floor level into a higher hopper","pushing a ball into a spindexer slot","clearing a jam at the intake exit"],"pros":["cheap and compact second stage","one ball per stroke, so counting is easy","no long belt to tension"],"cons":["slower than a continuous conveyor","timing needs a sensor or a fixed delay","the paddle must clear the next ball"],"failures":["the kick fires before the ball is seated","the servo horn strips","the paddle catches a second ball"],"dof":2,"actuation":"motor+servo","motion":{"input":"roller motor plus servo rotation","output":"ball pulled in, then pushed into storage"},"preset":{"states":{"rest":0,"kick":70},"upper":90,"lower":0,"speed":400},"params":[{"key":"kickAngle","label":"Kick travel","unit":"deg","min":30,"max":120,"default":70,"estimate":true},{"key":"kickTime","label":"Kick and return time","unit":"ms","min":150,"max":800,"default":350,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R502","R503"],"note":"Uses an approved servo under the R502 limits. Legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Transfers (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML) TU08","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":1,"sensors":["color or distance sensor to see the ball (optional)"]},
{"id":"biobuzz-size-adaptive-intake","name":"BIOBUZZ adjustable-gap intake for POLLEN and NECTAR","aliases":["adjustable-gap intake","spring-loaded compliant intake","sprung roller intake","size-adaptive feed channel","guided funnel intake"],"category":"game","tags":["intake","compliance","pollen","nectar","funnel"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"roller-intake","parent":"roller-intake","type":"roller-intake","short":"A roller intake whose roller or side wall rides on springs so it grips both the 2.8 in. POLLEN and the 3.6 in. NECTAR.","how":"The driven roller (or one side of a side-roller pair) is mounted on a pivoting arm held down by surgical tubing or a spring. A small POLLEN gets pinched at the rest gap. A bigger NECTAR pushes the arm up and still gets compressed. Angled guide walls (a funnel) in front center balls into the mouth. Downstream: conveyor, then indexer, then shooter.","uses":["one intake for both BIOBUZZ ball sizes","absorbing hits from other robots"],"pros":["consistent grip on both sizes","forgives bumps"],"cons":["spring preload needs tuning","more parts than a fixed roller","Do not catch or deflect elements falling from a tipped HIVE before they touch the floor (G409)."],"failures":["the spring is too weak and POLLEN slips","the spring is too strong and NECTAR stalls the roller","the pivot binds"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation (the spring arm is passive)","output":"roller spin with a floating gap"},"params":[{"key":"restGap","label":"Gap at rest","unit":"mm","min":50,"max":75,"default":62,"estimate":true},{"key":"maxGap","label":"Gap fully open","unit":"mm","min":80,"max":100,"default":88,"estimate":true,"note":"just below NECTAR's ~91 mm so it is still compressed"},{"key":"springForce","label":"Spring preload","unit":"N","min":2,"max":30,"default":10,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R105","G407","G409"],"note":"Springs are not numerically limited in the R rules I read. Check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Intakes (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/types-of-intakes.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Principles of an Intake (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/principles-of-an-intake.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/types-of-intakes.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/9971-intake.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), FTC 9971 LanBros, Relic Recovery spring-loaded intake (not a BIOBUZZ robot)"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":0},
{"id":"biobuzz-flower-pollen-retrieval","name":"BIOBUZZ FLOWER POLLEN retrieval (bottom opening)","aliases":["FLOWER comb","pollen poker","servo poker","retrieval opening intake","flower picker"],"category":"game","tags":["intake","flower","pollen"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"flower-retrieval","type":"sweeper-arm","short":"A servo arm or wheel that reaches into a FLOWER's bottom Retrieval Opening and combs POLLEN out so the robot can collect it.","how":"G418 lets a robot remove only POLLEN, and only from the bottom of a FLOWER. The Retrieval Opening is about 3.55 in. tall and 3.57 in. deep. REV's StarterBot uses a servo-powered poker on its side to comb POLLEN out. goBILDA's StarterBot pulls POLLEN out with its wheel intake. Freed POLLEN then goes through the normal intake, storage and shooter path.","uses":["refilling with POLLEN from the 4 staged in each FLOWER","feeding the shooter during TELEOP"],"pros":["a predictable POLLEN source next to the wall","a servo poker is cheap"],"cons":["G418 forbids removing NECTAR or using the sides, and forcing NECTAR out of the middle ring is listed as likely strategic","you must align against the wall"],"failures":["the poker pushes POLLEN deeper instead of out","the arm snags the FLOWER (watch G415)","you reach the opening at the wrong height"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation","output":"arm sweep through the bottom opening"},"preset":{"states":{"stowed":0,"sweep":90},"upper":120,"lower":0,"speed":300},"params":[{"key":"reach","label":"Reach into opening","unit":"in","min":1,"max":3.5,"default":2.5,"estimate":true,"note":"the opening is ~3.57 in. deep"},{"key":"sweepAngle","label":"Sweep angle","unit":"deg","min":45,"max":150,"default":90,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["G418","G415"],"note":"Only POLLEN, only through the bottom (G418). Do not grab or attach to the FLOWER (G415). Legality depends on the design and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"2026-27 REV DUO FTC Starter Bot","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/ftc-kickoff-concepts/home/2026-27-rev-duo-ftc-starter-bot","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"FTC StarterBot Resource Guide (2026-2027) (goBILDA)","url":"https://www.gobilda.com/ftc-starter-bot-resource-guide-2026-2027-season/","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML) TU08","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"medium","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":0,"servos":1},
{"id":"biobuzz-four-ball-hopper","name":"BIOBUZZ 4-element storage hopper","aliases":["multi-ball hopper","gravity-fed storage channel","multi-ball intake with staging","ball magazine","staging channel"],"category":"game","tags":["inside","storage","pollen"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"gravity-hopper","parent":"gravity-hopper","type":"hopper","short":"A sloped bin inside the robot that holds up to four balls and lets gravity roll them toward the shooter feeder.","how":"Balls from the intake drop into a channel sloped toward the feeder. A gate or feeder wheel at the low end lets one ball at a time into the flywheel. A tilting version uses a servo to raise the back and push the last balls forward. Size it for 4 elements, because G407 caps CONTROL at 4 SCORING ELEMENTS.","uses":["holding the 4 preloaded POLLEN at the start","buffering balls between the intake and the shooter"],"pros":["no motor needed if gravity does the work","simple and light"],"cons":["slow feed on shallow slopes","balls of two sizes can wedge","Do not catch or deflect elements falling from a tipped HIVE before they touch the floor (G409)."],"failures":["two balls jam side by side","a ball rests on a flat spot and never rolls","the hopper is too big, so the robot holds 5 or more"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"gravity, optionally a tilt servo","output":"balls roll to the feeder"},"preset":{"states":{"load":0,"feed":20},"upper":30,"lower":0,"speed":120},"params":[{"key":"capacity","label":"Capacity","unit":"balls","min":1,"max":4,"default":4,"estimate":false,"note":"G407: no more than 4 controlled at once"},{"key":"slope","label":"Floor slope","unit":"deg","min":5,"max":35,"default":15,"estimate":true},{"key":"laneWidth","label":"Lane width","unit":"mm","min":75,"max":110,"default":98,"estimate":true,"note":"a single lane wider than NECTAR (~91 mm)"}],"seasons":["biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["G407","R105","G409"],"note":"Capacity is limited by G407, not by any construction rule. Legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML) TU08","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"},{"title":"Types of Transfers (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":0,"servos":0},
{"id":"biobuzz-shooter-conveyor","name":"BIOBUZZ ball conveyor to the shooter","aliases":["motorized ball conveyor","intake-to-shooter transfer","side-belt ball feed"],"category":"game","tags":["inside","conveyor","transfer"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"belt-conveyor","parent":"belt-conveyor","type":"conveyor","short":"Belts or rollers that carry balls from the intake up to the shooter's feed point and hold them there until it is time to shoot.","how":"One motor drives belts or roller stacks that press each ball against a back wall or an opposing belt and carry it up an incline. The top of the conveyor stops just short of the flywheel, and a gate or the conveyor itself feeds one ball when the flywheel is at speed. The path is intake, then conveyor (storage), then flywheel. REV's Ultimate Goal guide notes that two short belts in series hold normal force better than one long belt.","uses":["lifting balls from floor intake height to a launcher","storing up to 4 elements in line"],"pros":["moves balls up steep angles","doubles as storage","continuous and fast"],"cons":["compression must suit two ball sizes","more motors and belts to tension"],"failures":["slip with too little compression","stall with too much","balls jam at the entrance or exit"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"ball translation along the path"},"params":[{"key":"length","label":"Conveyor length","unit":"in","min":6,"max":24,"default":12,"estimate":true},{"key":"angle","label":"Incline","unit":"deg","min":0,"max":90,"default":60,"estimate":true},{"key":"compression","label":"Squeeze on the ball","unit":"mm","min":2,"max":15,"default":6,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["G407","R503"],"note":"Store no more than 4 elements. Count the motor toward R503. Legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Conveyor (REV FTC Kickoff Concepts, Ultimate Goal)","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/ftc-kickoff-concepts/ultimate-goal-2020-2021/conveyor.md","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"Types of Transfers: conveyor transfer (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":0},
{"id":"biobuzz-shot-gate","name":"BIOBUZZ one-at-a-time shot gate","aliases":["ball singulator","one-at-a-time shooter feeder","single-ball indexing","escapement"],"category":"game","tags":["inside","gate","feeder","indexing"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"servo-gate","parent":"servo-gate","type":"gate","short":"A servo flap that holds the ball queue back and opens just long enough to let one ball reach the flywheel.","how":"The gate blocks the end of the hopper or conveyor. When the flywheel is at speed, the servo swings it open, one ball rolls or is pushed into the flywheel, and the gate closes before the next ball. A two-flap escapement keeps the second ball back while the first goes. The path is storage, then gate, then flywheel.","uses":["spacing shots so the flywheel recovers its speed","holding preloads during AUTO driving"],"pros":["one servo","gives a consistent shot interval"],"cons":["a single flap can let two balls through if the timing is off","slower than continuous feeding"],"failures":["the gate closes on a ball","servo creep lets balls leak","the flywheel has not recovered when the next ball arrives"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation","output":"flap opens or closes"},"preset":{"states":{"closed":0,"open":60},"upper":90,"lower":0,"speed":500},"params":[{"key":"openTime","label":"Open time per ball","unit":"ms","min":80,"max":600,"default":200,"estimate":true},{"key":"openAngle","label":"Open angle","unit":"deg","min":30,"max":90,"default":60,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R502","R506"],"note":"Use a servo. Solenoid actuators are prohibited (R506). Legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Programming Flywheel (REV 2026-27 Starter Bot)","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/ftc-kickoff-concepts/home/programming-teleop-and-auto/programming-flywheel.md","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML) TU08","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"medium","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":0,"servos":1},
{"id":"biobuzz-spindexer","name":"BIOBUZZ spindexer (rotary ball indexer)","aliases":["rotary indexer"],"category":"game","tags":["inside","indexer","storage","sorting"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"rotary-indexer","parent":"rotary-indexer","type":"spindexer","short":"A rotating wheel of pockets that stores balls and turns each one in turn to the shooter's feed point.","how":"A servo or motor with an encoder rotates a disc with 3-4 pockets. The intake drops a ball into the pocket at the load position. A rotation then brings a chosen pocket to the feed position, where a kicker or feeder wheel pushes the ball into the flywheel. A color sensor at the load pocket can tell POLLEN from NECTAR and red from blue, so the robot can choose the shot order or spit out opponent NECTAR (G408). The path is intake, then spindexer, then kicker, then flywheel.","uses":["holding up to 4 elements and choosing which to shoot","sorting NECTAR from POLLEN"],"pros":["picks which ball to shoot","compact storage"],"cons":["needs accurate position control","pockets sized for both balls waste space","more software"],"failures":["the position drifts and a pocket misaligns","a ball sits half in a pocket and jams the rotation","the sensor misreads color under field light"],"dof":1,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"servo or motor rotation","output":"stepwise disc rotation between pockets"},"preset":{"states":{"slot1":0,"slot2":120,"slot3":240},"upper":360,"lower":0,"speed":360},"params":[{"key":"slots","label":"Pockets","unit":"count","min":2,"max":4,"default":3,"estimate":true,"note":"never store more than 4 elements (G407)"},{"key":"pocketDiameter","label":"Pocket size","unit":"mm","min":75,"max":105,"default":96,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["G407","G408"],"note":"Sorting does not excuse repeatedly controlling opponent NECTAR (G408). Legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML) TU08","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"},{"title":"Color Sensor V3 (REV)","url":"https://www.revrobotics.com/rev-31-1557/","kind":"vendor"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":0,"servos":1,"sensors":["color sensor","encoder or absolute servo position"]},
{"id":"biobuzz-nectar-sorter","name":"BIOBUZZ POLLEN/NECTAR and color sorter","aliases":["ball diverter","color sorter","reject flap"],"category":"game","tags":["inside","sorting","diverter"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"diverter","parent":"diverter","type":"diverter","short":"A servo flap inside the robot that sends each ball one way or another, for example opponent NECTAR back out and everything else to the shooter.","how":"A sensor (color, or a size check) reads each ball right after the intake. A servo flap then routes it to the shooter path, a FLOWER-scoring path, or back out of the robot. Opponent NECTAR should leave quickly, because repeatedly controlling it is likely strategic under G408. The path is intake, then sensor, then diverter, then a hopper or the exit.","uses":["rejecting opponent NECTAR","separating own NECTAR (for FLOWERS late in the match) from POLLEN (for the HIVE)"],"pros":["lets one intake serve two scoring paths","handles mixed piles"],"cons":["adds a sensor, a servo and code","balls can stall at the flap"],"failures":["the flap moves while a ball is under it","misreads color","the reject path throws balls out of the field (G405)"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation","output":"flap switches the ball path"},"preset":{"states":{"pass":0,"divert":45},"upper":60,"lower":0,"speed":500},"params":[{"key":"switchAngle","label":"Flap travel","unit":"deg","min":20,"max":70,"default":45,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["G408","G405"],"note":"A sorter does not excuse controlling opponent NECTAR. Do not eject elements from the field. Legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML) TU08","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"},{"title":"Color Sensor V3 (REV)","url":"https://www.revrobotics.com/rev-31-1557/","kind":"vendor"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":0,"servos":1,"sensors":["color sensor"]},
{"id":"biobuzz-fixed-shooter","name":"BIOBUZZ fixed-angle flywheel shooter","aliases":["fixed-angle flywheel","single flywheel launcher"],"category":"game","tags":["score","shooter","hive"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"flywheel-shooter","parent":"flywheel-shooter","type":"flywheel","short":"A fast spinning wheel that throws POLLEN and NECTAR up into the HIVE's upward-facing CELL at a fixed angle.","how":"A motor spins one wheel against a fixed backplate, or two wheels against each other, so a ball fed in is squeezed and thrown. The exit angle is fixed, so range is set by wheel speed and robot position. Teams often shoot from a few marked spots. The HIVE pivot is about 44 in. up and the robot is at most 29 in. tall, so balls must arc up into the 20 x 14 in. CELL opening. Feed: hopper or conveyor, then gate, then flywheel.","uses":["scoring HIVE TIPS by launching into the upward-facing CELL","AUTO preload shots"],"pros":["simple and robust","one motor (REV's 2026-27 StarterBot uses one UltraPlanetary HD Hex flywheel)"],"cons":["accurate only from known spots","the two ball sizes leave at different speeds"],"failures":["speed sags between shots","compression changes as wheels wear","launching into a non-top CELL face (G417)"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"ball launched on an arc"},"params":[{"key":"wheelDiameter","label":"Flywheel diameter","unit":"mm","min":72,"max":125,"default":96,"estimate":true},{"key":"wheelRpm","label":"Flywheel speed","unit":"rpm","min":1500,"max":6000,"default":3500,"estimate":true},{"key":"exitAngle","label":"Exit angle above horizontal","unit":"deg","min":30,"max":75,"default":55,"estimate":true},{"key":"compression","label":"Ball compression","unit":"mm","min":3,"max":20,"default":8,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R105","G417","T405"],"note":"The R rules I read have no launch speed limit. Launch only into the upward-facing CELL. Legality depends on the whole robot. Do not launch during field measurement or calibration time (T405)."},"refs":[{"title":"2026-27 REV DUO FTC Starter Bot: programming TeleOp and Auto","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/ftc-kickoff-concepts/home/programming-teleop-and-auto.md","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"Flywheel Launcher (REV, Ultimate Goal)","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/ftc-kickoff-concepts/ultimate-goal-2020-2021/shooter","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML) TU08","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":1},
{"id":"biobuzz-hooded-shooter","name":"BIOBUZZ hooded flywheel with adjustable hood","aliases":["hooded flywheel","deflector"],"category":"game","tags":["score","shooter","hood","hive"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"hooded-flywheel","parent":"adjustable-hood","type":"hood","short":"A flywheel that rolls the ball along a curved hood. A servo moves the hood to change the exit angle for different distances.","how":"The ball is squeezed between the spinning flywheel and a curved hood and leaves where the hood ends. A servo pivots or moves the hood so the exit angle changes: lower for close shots, higher for far ones. REV found about 115 mm from flywheel center to hood center gave the best compression on its DECODE launcher, and says to keep compression the same at the entry and exit of the hood. Feed: storage, then gate, then flywheel and hood.","uses":["hitting the HIVE CELL from many distances","pairing with distance-based auto aim"],"pros":["one shooter for many ranges","a hood gives a consistent spin and arc"],"cons":["the hood servo must hold under load","there is a lookup table to tune"],"failures":["hood backlash","compression drift","worn hood surface"],"dof":2,"actuation":"motor+servo","motion":{"input":"flywheel motor plus hood servo","output":"launch speed and exit angle"},"preset":{"states":{"low":0,"high":25},"upper":30,"lower":0,"speed":200},"params":[{"key":"hoodTravel","label":"Hood angle range","unit":"deg","min":10,"max":40,"default":25,"estimate":true},{"key":"hoodRadius","label":"Flywheel center to hood","unit":"mm","min":80,"max":140,"default":115,"estimate":false,"note":"REV DECODE build tips (for DECODE Artifacts). Re-tune for POLLEN and NECTAR"},{"key":"wheelRpm","label":"Flywheel speed","unit":"rpm","min":1500,"max":6000,"default":3500,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["G417","R502"],"note":"Aim only at the upward-facing CELL. Legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Build Tips & Tricks (REV DECODE 2025-26)","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/ftc-kickoff-concepts/decode-2025-26/build-tips-and-tricks","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML) TU08","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":1},
{"id":"biobuzz-pivot-shooter","name":"BIOBUZZ pivoting shooter","aliases":["pivot-adjustable shooter","angle-adjustable launcher"],"category":"game","tags":["score","shooter","hive"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"pivoting-shooter","parent":"pivoting-shooter","type":"shooter-pivot","short":"A whole flywheel shooter on a hinge, tilted by a motor or servo to change how steeply it shoots.","how":"The flywheel assembly pivots about a horizontal axis. A motor with an encoder, or a strong servo through gearing, sets the pitch. Unlike a hood, the whole feed path tilts with the shooter, so the feed handoff must work at every angle. Feed: storage, then gate, then flywheel.","uses":["long and short HIVE shots","stowing the shooter low for driving"],"pros":["large angle range"],"cons":["a heavy moving mass","the feed must follow the pivot"],"failures":["the pivot sags under the shooter's weight","the feed misaligns at extreme angles"],"dof":2,"actuation":"motor+motor","motion":{"input":"pivot actuator plus flywheel motor","output":"launch pitch and speed"},"preset":{"states":{"low":30,"high":65},"upper":75,"lower":20,"speed":60},"params":[{"key":"pitchRange","label":"Pitch range","unit":"deg","min":20,"max":80,"default":45,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R105","G417"],"note":"Stay within the 29 in. height at every pitch. Legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML) TU08","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"},{"title":"Arms (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/arms.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":2,"servos":0},
{"id":"biobuzz-turret-shooter","name":"BIOBUZZ turret shooter","aliases":["rotating turret shooter","turreted launcher","closed-loop turret aiming"],"category":"game","tags":["score","shooter","turret","hive"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"turret","parent":"turret","type":"turret","short":"A shooter on a rotating base, so it can aim at the HIVE while the robot drives in another direction.","how":"The shooter sits on a lazy Susan, a bearing stack or a center bearing. A motor turns it through a belt or gear on the turret ring. An encoder or a homing sensor sets angle zero. gm0 notes that lazy Susans handle lateral loads poorly, so shooter recoil can damage them. Cables cross the rotation, so limit travel (for example ±180°) or use a slip ring. Feed must enter through or near the axis: storage, then gate, then flywheel on the turret.","uses":["shooting while driving","keeping the CELL in view of a camera that rides the turret"],"pros":["fast aim without turning the chassis","good with auto aim"],"cons":["complex","heavy up top","the feed path across the rotation is hard"],"failures":["wires wind up","backlash","the turret loses its zero","bearing damage from recoil"],"dof":2,"actuation":"motor+motor","motion":{"input":"turret motor plus flywheel motor","output":"yaw aim plus launch"},"preset":{"states":{"front":0,"left":90,"right":-90},"upper":180,"lower":-180,"speed":180},"params":[{"key":"yawRange","label":"Yaw travel each side","unit":"deg","min":45,"max":180,"default":180,"estimate":true},{"key":"turretRatio","label":"Motor to turret reduction","unit":"ratio","min":3,"max":20,"default":8,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R303"],"note":"A single-DoF COTS part under R303 (not one of its listed exceptions); legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Full Turrets (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/turrets/full-turret.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML) TU08","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/turrets/full-turret.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/19818-turret.jpg","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), FTC 19818 Jade Innovations, Power Play lazy Susan turret rotating slides (a turret, but not a shooter turret)"},"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":2,"servos":1,"sensors":["turret encoder","homing limit switch or magnet sensor"]},
{"id":"biobuzz-flower-placer","name":"BIOBUZZ FLOWER top placer (lift bucket or tilting chute)","aliases":["elevated FLOWER top placement","lift-mounted scoring bucket","tilting scoring chute","flower dumper","top-entry placer"],"category":"game","tags":["score","flower","lift","bucket","nectar"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"tilting-bucket","parent":"tilting-bucket","type":"bucket","short":"A bucket or chute raised above a FLOWER's ~21.5 in. top opening that tips a ball in from above; elements may only enter through the top of a FLOWER (G418).","how":"A lift or arm raises a small bucket or chute above the FLOWER's roughly 4 in. top opening. A servo then tips it so the ball drops in from above. Entering anywhere else is not allowed (G418). NECTAR may go in only during the last 60 s (G410), and it sets FLOWER ownership. Feed: storage or diverter, then bucket, then FLOWER top.","uses":["placing own NECTAR to own a FLOWER late in the match","placing POLLEN for 2 points each in an owned FLOWER"],"pros":["precise single-ball placement"],"cons":["needs height (about 21.5 in.) inside the 29 in. limit","must line up with a 4 in. opening"],"failures":["the ball bounces off the rim","NECTAR enters before the last 60 s (MAJOR FOUL per NECTAR)","the bucket or lift shakes the FLOWER (G418)"],"dof":2,"actuation":"motor+servo","motion":{"input":"lift motor plus tip servo","output":"raise, then tip"},"preset":{"states":{"hold":0,"dump":110},"upper":130,"lower":0,"speed":300},"params":[{"key":"dropHeight","label":"Bucket lip height","unit":"in","min":22,"max":28,"default":24,"estimate":true,"note":"the FLOWER top is ~21.5 in. and the robot max is 29 in."},{"key":"tipAngle","label":"Tip angle","unit":"deg","min":60,"max":150,"default":110,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["G410","G418","G415","R105"],"note":"Top entry only, NECTAR only in the last 60 s, no grabbing the FLOWER. A concave alignment shape that partly wraps it is allowed. Legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML) TU08","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":1},
{"id":"biobuzz-garden-release","name":"BIOBUZZ GARDEN release chute","aliases":["controlled GARDEN release chute","garden dropper","floor release gate"],"category":"game","tags":["score","garden","gate"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"servo-gate","parent":"servo-gate","type":"gate","short":"A servo gate at a low exit that sets balls down in your alliance's 23 in. x 2 in. GARDEN strip for 1 point each.","how":"The robot drives along its GARDEN tape. A servo opens a rear or side gate so stored balls roll out and come to rest at least partly in the GARDEN volume. The gate and the ramp lip control the release so balls do not roll away. Feed: storage, then gate, then GARDEN.","uses":["quick 1-point scores","unloading leftover elements to respect the 4-element limit"],"pros":["simple","no aiming"],"cons":["low points","GARDENS are not protected, so opponents may remove elements"],"failures":["balls roll out of the 2 in. strip","releasing on the wrong alliance's GARDEN"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation","output":"gate opens and balls roll out"},"preset":{"states":{"closed":0,"open":80},"upper":90,"lower":0,"speed":400},"params":[{"key":"exitHeight","label":"Exit lip height","unit":"mm","min":10,"max":80,"default":30,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["G411","G405"],"note":"Corralling elements to deny opponents is prohibited (G411). Legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML) TU08","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"medium","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":0,"servos":1},
{"id":"biobuzz-hive-camera","name":"BIOBUZZ up-tilted camera for HIVE AprilTags","aliases":["camera-assisted HIVE targeting","AprilTag camera mount","upward camera","targeting camera"],"category":"game","tags":["sense","vision","apriltag","hive"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"camera-mount","parent":"camera-pan","type":"camera-mount","short":"A camera tilted upward, because BIOBUZZ AprilTags are on the bottoms of the HIVE CELLs, facing down at the field.","how":"The 36h11 tag clusters (IDs 30-45, 3.25 in.) sit under each CELL and face down. A camera pitched up (or mounted on a servo tilt or on the turret) can see them from below and give range, bearing and yaw to the CELL for aiming. Mount it rigidly and measure its offset from the robot center for localization.","uses":["aiming a turret or chassis at the HIVE","measuring distance for hood or flywheel speed"],"pros":["targets mark the CELLs directly","works with the FTC SDK AprilTag processor"],"cons":["the steep look-up angle and the moving HIVE complicate pose","no tags on FLOWERS or walls (per the manual text I read)"],"failures":["the camera loosens and calibration is lost","motion blur","the HIVE tips mid-detection, moving the tags"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"optional tilt servo","output":"camera pitch"},"preset":{"states":{"level":0,"up":-35},"lower":-60,"upper":0,"speed":120},"params":[{"key":"pitch","label":"Camera pitch up","unit":"deg","min":10,"max":60,"default":30,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R707","R708","R105"],"note":"Use a single-sensor UVC webcam or an R702-listed coprocessor (R707/R708); the mount must stay within R105. Legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML) TU08, Section 9.9 AprilTags","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"},{"title":"AprilTag Introduction (FTC Docs)","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/apriltag/vision_portal/apriltag_intro/apriltag-intro.html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":0,"servos":0,"sensors":["webcam"]},
{"id":"biobuzz-auto-aim","name":"BIOBUZZ auto aim and launch-speed control (software)","aliases":["AprilTag targeting","distance-assisted alignment","automatic launch-speed control","shot lookup table","parking alignment aid"],"category":"game","tags":["software","vision","shooter","turret","localization"],"entry":"software","family":"auto-aim","type":null,"short":"Code that uses the HIVE AprilTags or odometry to point the shooter at the CELL and pick wheel speed and hood angle from the distance.","how":"The AprilTag processor (or odometry) gives the robot's pose relative to the target CELL. A controller turns the turret or chassis to zero the bearing. A lookup table maps distance to flywheel speed and hood angle, with interpolation between tuned points. The same pose lets AUTO drive into the LOADING ZONE to PARK.","uses":["consistent HIVE TIPS from anywhere","PARK in the LOADING ZONE at the end of AUTO or TELEOP"],"pros":["cuts driver workload","repeatable"],"cons":["tuning time","depends on a camera or odometry"],"failures":["a stale tag pose","the table was tuned for a different ball (POLLEN vs NECTAR)","controller oscillation"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","motion":{"input":"camera pose or odometry","output":"turret, hood and flywheel setpoints"},"params":[{"key":"yawTolerance","label":"Aim tolerance","unit":"deg","min":0.5,"max":5,"default":2,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["G417"],"note":"Software is not a mechanism, but its shots must still follow G417."},"refs":[{"title":"AprilTag Localization (FTC Docs)","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/apriltag/vision_portal/apriltag_localization/apriltag-localization.html","kind":"official"},{"title":"Control Loops (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/software/concepts/control-loops.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["webcam","odometry"],"parent":"vision-assisted-aiming"},
{"id":"biobuzz-shot-sequence","name":"BIOBUZZ automated shooting sequence (software)","aliases":["encoder-controlled shooter","sensor-triggered shot feeder","flywheel velocity control","auto launch sequence","shoot-when-ready"],"category":"game","tags":["software","shooter","feeder","automation"],"entry":"software","family":"shot-sequence","type":null,"short":"Code that spins the flywheel to a target velocity and opens the feeder only when it is up to speed, one ball at a time.","how":"Use velocity control (built-in PIDF or a Kv feedforward plus PID) on the flywheel encoder. When the measured speed is within a tolerance, open the gate or run the feeder for one ball, then wait for recovery. REV's 2026-27 StarterBot TeleOp has exactly this flywheel sequence: the feeder waits until velocity is within range. A beam break or color sensor can confirm each ball left and count stored balls toward the 4-element limit.","uses":["AUTO preload shots","one-button TeleOp shooting"],"pros":["consistent range","less driver timing"],"cons":["tolerance tuning","battery sag changes recovery time"],"failures":["the tolerance is so tight it never fires","feeding a ball during recovery leaves it short"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","motion":{"input":"flywheel encoder, ball sensor","output":"feeder or gate commands"},"params":[{"key":"speedTolerance","label":"Ready tolerance","unit":"%","min":1,"max":10,"default":3,"estimate":true},{"key":"recoveryDelay","label":"Minimum delay between shots","unit":"ms","min":100,"max":800,"default":300,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["T405"],"note":"Do not launch during field calibration time. Software legality also depends on how it is used in the match."},"refs":[{"title":"Programming Flywheel (REV 2026-27 Starter Bot)","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/ftc-kickoff-concepts/home/programming-teleop-and-auto/programming-flywheel.md","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"Control Loops (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/software/concepts/control-loops.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Finite State Machines (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/software/concepts/finite-state-machines.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["flywheel encoder","beam break or color sensor (optional)"]},
{"id":"belt-conveyor","name":"Belt conveyor","aliases":["single-belt conveyor","dual-belt conveyor","vertical conveyor","inclined conveyor","chain-driven conveyor","flighted belt","continuous conveyor","timing belt conveyor","Belt (conveyor) intake","belt intake","conveyor intake","continuous-loop tread intake","tread intake","o-ring belt conveyor","band conveyor"],"category":"inside","tags":["transfer","storage","conveyor"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"belt-conveyor","type":"conveyor","short":"A moving belt or chain loop that carries game pieces through the robot, usually from the intake up to a scorer.","how":"A motor drives one pulley. The belt, chain or O-ring loop presses the piece against a back wall or a second belt (dual-belt) and carries it along a straight or inclined path. Upstream is the intake. Downstream is a gate, feeder or shooter, or a bucket. Chain with flights or paddles moves loose pieces up steep angles. gm0 counts this among continuous conveyors: constant contact reduces jams, but low grip can slip.","uses":["intake-to-shooter transfer","vertical elevation of balls or rings","in-line storage"],"pros":["buffers several pieces","handles complex paths","continuous flow"],"cons":["jams at the entry and exit","tensioning","significant mechanical complexity"],"failures":["belt slip","jumped teeth","pieces stall where normal force drops in the middle of a long belt"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"piece translation"},"params":[{"key":"length","label":"Belt run","unit":"in","min":4,"max":30,"default":12,"estimate":true},{"key":"surfaceSpeed","label":"Belt surface speed","unit":"in/s","min":5,"max":60,"default":25,"estimate":true},{"key":"compression","label":"Piece compression","unit":"mm","min":1,"max":15,"default":5,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:ultimate-goal"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R503"],"note":"A generic mechanism. Check the motor count and the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Transfers (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Timing Belt (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/power-transmission/belt.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Conveyor (REV Ultimate Goal)","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/ftc-kickoff-concepts/ultimate-goal-2020-2021/conveyor.md","kind":"vendor"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/8644-conveyor.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), FTC 8644 Brainstormers, Ultimate Goal O-ring belt conveyor"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":0},
{"id":"side-belt-conveyor","name":"Side-belt (sandwich) conveyor","aliases":["side belting","sandwich conveyor","twin-belt pinch conveyor","opposed belts"],"category":"inside","tags":["transfer","conveyor"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"belt-conveyor","parent":"belt-conveyor","type":"conveyor","short":"Two belts on either side of the piece that pinch it and carry it up, even nearly vertically.","how":"Two belt runs face each other with a gap a little smaller than the piece and are driven together, often from one motor. The piece is pinched from both sides, so it climbs steep paths. REV's Ultimate Goal design used 3 mm GT2 belts with 30 mm traction wheels beside the pulleys, and found two shorter belts in series held normal force better than one long one. Upstream is the intake. Downstream is the shooter feed.","uses":["vertical ring or ball elevator","compact feed inside the 18 in. cube"],"pros":["climbs up to about 90°","positive grip"],"cons":["gap tuning","two belt runs to support"],"failures":["the gap opens and slips","flanges rub the piece"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"piece translation"},"params":[{"key":"gap","label":"Belt-to-belt gap","unit":"mm","min":20,"max":110,"default":60,"estimate":true,"note":"slightly less than the piece size"}],"seasons":["general","historical:ultimate-goal"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R503"],"note":"A generic mechanism. Check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Conveyor (REV Ultimate Goal)","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/ftc-kickoff-concepts/ultimate-goal-2020-2021/conveyor.md","kind":"vendor"}],"image":{"kind":"illustration","page":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/ftc-kickoff-concepts/ultimate-goal-2020-2021/conveyor.md","src":"https://268621232-files.gitbook.io/~/files/v0/b/gitbook-legacy-files/o/assets%2F-MHCAE012xNfg1h3SM9v%2F-MHNaNDeHRMWJbUULoF6%2F-MHNbSrTsSQC2FJ16eW7%2FSide%20roller.jpg?alt=media&token=e30aa512-961c-4dab-be39-ea135aa1aa26","credit":"REV Robotics FTC Kickoff Concepts, Ultimate Goal conveyor (hand sketch of a side-belt concept)"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":0},
{"id":"band-conveyor","name":"Rubber band or tubing conveyor","aliases":["flexible-belt conveyor","rubber band conveyor","O-ring conveyor","polycord belt","surgical tubing conveyor"],"category":"inside","tags":["transfer","conveyor"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"belt-conveyor","parent":"belt-conveyor","type":"conveyor","short":"A conveyor made from stretched rubber bands, O-rings or tubing, so it gives a little and grips odd-shaped pieces.","how":"Elastic loops run between pulleys or spools and act as a soft belt that conforms to the piece. Their stretch provides the compression. Upstream is the intake. Downstream is a hopper or shooter. Round polybelt can slip on long runs (REV tested it first and moved to GT2).","uses":["ball transfers","light pieces at moderate angles"],"pros":["cheap","self-tensioning","forgiving"],"cons":["less grip than toothed belt","bands wear and snap"],"failures":["band breaks","slip on long runs"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"piece translation"},"params":[{"key":"bandCount","label":"Number of bands","unit":"count","min":2,"max":12,"default":4,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:ultimate-goal","historical:rover-ruckus"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"A generic mechanism. Check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Transfers (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/11115-conveyor.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), FTC 11115 Gluten Free, ball transfer using rubber bands"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":0},
{"id":"roller-conveyor","name":"Roller conveyor","aliases":["roller stack","wheel conveyor","omni wheel conveyor","roller-to-belt transfer","powered rollers"],"category":"inside","tags":["transfer","conveyor"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"roller-conveyor","type":"conveyor","short":"A series of spinning rollers or wheels, chained or belted together, that push a piece along a path.","how":"Several shafts of rollers or omni or compliant wheels, linked by belt or chain to one motor, push the piece along a guide wall. gm0 notes that rollers grip odd shapes well, but pieces can catch in the gaps between rollers. Upstream is the intake. Downstream is the shooter or deposit.","uses":["steep ring or ball paths","short transfers"],"pros":["good grip","handles odd shapes"],"cons":["gaps between rollers catch pieces","many shafts"],"failures":["a piece wedges between rollers","a broken link stops one roller"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"piece translation"},"params":[{"key":"rollerPitch","label":"Roller spacing","unit":"mm","min":30,"max":120,"default":60,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:ultimate-goal"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"A generic mechanism. Check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Transfers (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/7244-roller.jpg","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), FTC 7244 OUT of the BOX Robotics, Ultimate Goal roller conveyor with omni wheels"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":0},
{"id":"hybrid-conveyor","name":"Hybrid roller and belt conveyor","aliases":["hybrid conveyor","rollers with tubing between","roller-belt combo"],"category":"inside","tags":["transfer","conveyor"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"roller-conveyor","parent":"roller-conveyor","type":"conveyor","short":"Rollers with belt or tubing stretched between them, combining the grip of rollers with the constant contact of a belt.","how":"gm0 describes hybrid conveyors as rollers with continuous material in between. The belt covers the gaps where pieces would catch, and the rollers add grip. Design it so both materials touch the piece. Upstream is the intake. Downstream is the shooter or bucket.","uses":["long transfers of balls"],"pros":["fewer catch points than rollers alone","more grip than a belt alone"],"cons":["harder to design"],"failures":["only one material touches the piece, so you get the weaknesses of both"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"piece translation"},"params":[],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"A generic mechanism. Check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Transfers (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":0},
{"id":"guide-channel","name":"Passive guide channel or gravity chute","aliases":["curved guide","gravity chute","ramp","funnel","guide rails","lexan channel"],"category":"inside","tags":["transfer","passive"],"entry":"component","family":"guide-channel","type":null,"short":"Fixed walls or a sloped chute that steer pieces to the next stage with no motor.","how":"Polycarbonate or printed walls and sloped floors use gravity and the push from upstream stages to route pieces. A curved guide turns a piece's direction between the intake and a conveyor. A gravity chute drops pieces from a hopper to a feeder. gm0 stresses funneling for a consistent path.","uses":["linking intake to conveyor","feeding a hopper to a gate"],"pros":["no actuator","light and reliable"],"cons":["only works downhill or with push","can jam at bends"],"failures":["the chute is too flat, so pieces stop","the bend radius is too tight"],"dof":0,"actuation":"passive","motion":{"input":"gravity or upstream push","output":"guided piece motion"},"params":[{"key":"slope","label":"Chute slope","unit":"deg","min":5,"max":60,"default":20,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"A generic part. Check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Principles of an Intake (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/principles-of-an-intake.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Transfer Design Fundamentals (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-design.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":0,"servos":0},
{"id":"direct-transfer","name":"Direct (transferless) transfer","aliases":["transferless","direct transfer","intake into bucket"],"category":"inside","tags":["transfer","architecture"],"entry":"architecture","family":"transfer","type":null,"short":"A layout where the scoring holder sits right behind the intake, so a piece is captured the moment it is collected.","how":"The intake pushes the piece straight into the bucket, claw or hopper that will score it. No internal mover is needed. The scoring mechanism must reach both the spot behind the intake and its scoring position.","uses":["bucket robots","simple ball robots feeding a shooter directly"],"pros":["lowest complexity","compact","fastest"],"cons":["the scorer must travel to the intake"],"failures":["the scorer is not home when the intake feeds"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","motion":{"input":"intake push","output":"piece into the holder"},"params":[],"seasons":["general","historical:freight-frenzy"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"A design concept. Check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Transfers (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"transfer-arm","name":"Transfer arm or rotating transfer bucket","aliases":["rotating transfer bucket","intake-to-claw transfer","intake-to-elevator transfer","pass-through arm","handoff arm"],"category":"inside","tags":["transfer","arm"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"transfer","type":"transfer","short":"A small pivoting arm or bucket that takes a piece from the intake and swings it over to the lift, claw or scorer.","how":"A servo rotates a bucket or arm between an intake pose and a transfer pose. At the intake pose it receives the piece. It swings over and drops or hands the piece to the next mechanism, such as a claw on a lift or an outtake bucket. Upstream is the intake. Downstream is the elevator, claw or deposit.","uses":["intake-to-claw handoff on slide robots","moving pieces past the frame to a lift"],"pros":["decouples the intake from the scorer","one servo"],"cons":["the order of moves matters","needs alignment"],"failures":["the handoff misses","the servo cannot hold the loaded arm"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation","output":"arm swing"},"preset":{"states":{"intake":0,"transfer":160},"upper":180,"lower":0,"speed":300},"params":[{"key":"swing","label":"Swing angle","unit":"deg","min":60,"max":200,"default":160,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"A generic mechanism. Check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Transfers (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Transfer Design Fundamentals (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-design.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":0,"servos":1},
{"id":"flip-up-transfer","name":"Flip-up transfer","aliases":["flip-up intake","pivoting transfer tray","flip intake","coaxial flip"],"category":"inside","tags":["transfer","intake"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"transfer","parent":"transfer-arm","type":"transfer","short":"The intake or its holder flips up around a pivot to line up with the next mechanism and hands off the piece.","how":"A holding mechanism rotates around a pivot until it lines up with the collector. Running the intake in reverse then ejects the piece into the scorer. The intake can spin coaxially with the flip pivot, and it can start flipped up to fit the 18 in. cube. Upstream is the floor. Downstream is the deposit or bucket.","uses":["intakes on extending slides","gaining height for a handoff"],"pros":["compact","fits the sizing cube when stowed"],"cons":["limited motion","careful alignment","slower than grate or direct transfers","exposed to hits"],"failures":["misaligned handoff","the pivot gets bent by contact"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo or motor rotation","output":"intake flip"},"preset":{"states":{"intake":0,"transfer":150},"upper":170,"lower":0,"speed":240},"params":[{"key":"flipAngle","label":"Flip angle","unit":"deg","min":90,"max":180,"default":150,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:freight-frenzy","historical:rover-ruckus"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R105"],"note":"A generic mechanism. Check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Transfers (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":0,"servos":1},
{"id":"grate-transfer","name":"Grate (fork) transfer","aliases":["grate transfer","fork transfer","comb transfer","interleaved forks","Horizontal-to-vertical slide transfer","horizontal-to-vertical transfer lift","slide transfer"],"category":"inside","tags":["transfer"],"entry":"concept","family":"transfer","type":null,"short":"Offset forks on two mechanisms pass through each other, so a piece is lifted from one onto the other almost instantly.","how":"The intake bucket and the deposit each have comb-like forks offset so they mesh without colliding. When one moves through the other, the piece is left on the second. It usually pairs with two angled linear slides. Upstream is the intake. Downstream is the deposit.","uses":["slide-on-slide robots","fast handoffs"],"pros":["low complexity","nearly instant","suits linear mechanisms"],"cons":["needs precise alignment","a required order of moves","hard to use with rotary mechanisms"],"failures":["forks collide","the piece slips between tines"],"dof":null,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"motion of the mechanisms carrying the forks","output":"piece transfer"},"params":[],"seasons":["general","historical:rover-ruckus"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"A design concept. Check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Transfers (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/6929-grate.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), FTC 6929 Data Force, Rover Ruckus grate transfer deposit"},"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"high","status":"historical","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"tilting-ramp","name":"Tilting ramp or chute","aliases":["pivoting chute","tilting tray","dump ramp","switchable ramp"],"category":"inside","tags":["transfer","gate"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"ramp","type":"ramp","short":"A chute that a servo tilts to start, stop or redirect pieces rolling through the robot.","how":"A sloped tray pivots on one edge. At one angle pieces stay put. Tilted, they roll to the next stage, such as a feeder, bucket or exit. Upstream is the intake or storage. Downstream is the feeder or exit.","uses":["holding then releasing balls","switching between two exits"],"pros":["simple","gravity does the work"],"cons":["slow for many pieces","needs space to tilt"],"failures":["the servo stalls under the load","pieces bounce out"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation","output":"tray tilt"},"preset":{"states":{"down":0,"up":25},"upper":40,"lower":0,"speed":200},"params":[{"key":"tilt","label":"Tilt range","unit":"deg","min":10,"max":45,"default":25,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"A generic mechanism. Check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Transfer Design Fundamentals (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-design.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"low","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":0,"servos":1},
{"id":"gravity-hopper","name":"Gravity hopper","aliases":["gravity-fed hopper","storage bin","tilting hopper","ball hopper"],"category":"inside","tags":["storage"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"gravity-hopper","type":"hopper","short":"A sloped bin that stores several pieces and lets gravity roll them to the feeder one after another.","how":"Pieces from the intake drop in. The floor slopes toward an exit gate or feeder. A servo can tilt the hopper to push the last pieces out. Upstream is the intake or conveyor. Downstream is a gate, feeder or shooter.","uses":["ball storage before a shooter","holding preloads"],"pros":["passive","light"],"cons":["slow on shallow slopes","pieces can bridge"],"failures":["pieces bridge or jam","the hopper holds more than the game allows"],"dof":1,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"gravity, optional tilt servo","output":"pieces to the exit"},"preset":{"states":{"load":0,"feed":20},"upper":30,"lower":0,"speed":120},"params":[{"key":"capacity","label":"Capacity","unit":"pieces","min":1,"max":10,"default":3,"estimate":true,"note":"limited by game rules (BIOBUZZ G407: 4)"},{"key":"slope","label":"Floor slope","unit":"deg","min":5,"max":40,"default":15,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"A generic mechanism. Game control limits apply. Check the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Transfers (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":0,"servos":0},
{"id":"stack-magazine","name":"Stacked magazine","aliases":["stacked magazine","vertical magazine","spring-assisted magazine","tube magazine","ring stack"],"category":"inside","tags":["storage"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"magazine","parent":"gravity-hopper","type":"hopper","short":"A tube or stack that holds pieces one above another and feeds from the bottom or the top.","how":"Pieces stack in a vertical or tilted column. Gravity or a spring follower pushes the stack toward a feed point, where a kicker or feeder pulls off one piece at a time. A servo-lifted magazine can raise the stack to the shooter, like many Ultimate Goal ring magazines. Upstream is the intake or conveyor. Downstream is the kicker and then the shooter.","uses":["rings or discs","balls in a single column"],"pros":["compact footprint","predictable order"],"cons":["one feed point","the follower spring can jam"],"failures":["the stack tilts and binds","double feed"],"dof":1,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"gravity, spring or servo lift","output":"the stack advances to the feed point"},"preset":{"states":{"load":0,"feed":20},"upper":30,"lower":0,"speed":120},"params":[{"key":"capacity","label":"Capacity","unit":"pieces","min":2,"max":6,"default":3,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:ultimate-goal"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R801"],"note":"Gas springs are allowed only as sealed pre-charged COTS items (R801). Check the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Transfers (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"low","status":"historical","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":0,"servos":1},
{"id":"multi-lane-magazine","name":"Side-by-side multi-lane magazine","aliases":["side-by-side magazine","multi-channel storage","dual-lane hopper","sorted lanes"],"category":"inside","tags":["storage","sorting"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"magazine","parent":"gravity-hopper","type":"hopper","short":"Parallel channels that store different kinds of pieces separately, each with its own exit.","how":"A diverter upstream sends each piece into a lane (for example by color or size). Each lane slopes to its own gate, so the robot can release a chosen type. Upstream is the intake and the diverter. Downstream are the gates and then the shooter or placer.","uses":["keeping two piece types apart for different goals"],"pros":["choose what to score","sorted storage"],"cons":["needs a diverter and more gates","wider"],"failures":["the wrong lane is chosen","a lane jams"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"gate servos","output":"selected lane releases"},"params":[{"key":"lanes","label":"Lanes","unit":"count","min":2,"max":3,"default":2,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"A generic mechanism. Game control limits apply. Check the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Transfer Design Fundamentals (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-design.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":0,"servos":2},
{"id":"rotary-indexer","name":"Rotary indexer (spindexer or carousel)","aliases":["spindexer","carousel","carousel indexer","rotary carousel storage","encoder-indexed storage","revolver","rotating indexing wheel","servo-gated indexer"],"category":"inside","tags":["storage","indexer","sorting"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"rotary-indexer","type":"spindexer","short":"A rotating disc of pockets that stores pieces and turns each one to a load or fire position on command.","how":"A servo, or a motor with an encoder, rotates a pocketed disc. Pieces enter at the load position from the intake, and the disc steps to bring a pocket to the feed position, where a kicker or feeder sends it on. Encoder or absolute feedback keeps the pockets aligned. Upstream is the intake. Downstream is the kicker and then the shooter or deposit.","uses":["ball storage with choice of order","color sorting"],"pros":["selective feeding","compact"],"cons":["needs position control","software heavy"],"failures":["the position drifts","a half-seated piece jams the rotation"],"dof":1,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"servo or motor rotation","output":"indexed disc rotation"},"preset":{"states":{"slot1":0,"slot2":120,"slot3":240},"upper":360,"lower":0,"speed":360},"params":[{"key":"slots","label":"Pockets","unit":"count","min":2,"max":6,"default":3,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"A generic mechanism. Game control limits apply. Check the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Transfers (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Color Sensor V3 (REV)","url":"https://www.revrobotics.com/rev-31-1557/","kind":"vendor"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"low","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":0,"servos":1,"sensors":["encoder or absolute position","color sensor (optional)"]},
{"id":"star-wheel-indexer","name":"Star-wheel singulator","aliases":["star-wheel indexer","rotary singulator"],"category":"inside","tags":["indexer","feeder"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"rotary-indexer","parent":"geneva-drive","type":"geneva-indexer","short":"A notched wheel that turns a fixed step at a time to pass exactly one piece per step from storage to the next stage.","how":"A star wheel with pockets sits at the exit of a hopper. Each step (servo position, or a continuously driven Geneva drive that turns intermittently) lets one piece through and blocks the next. Unlike a spindexer it does not store a set of pieces, it meters them. Upstream is the hopper. Downstream is the feeder or shooter.","uses":["metering balls into a flywheel"],"pros":["mechanically one piece at a time"],"cons":["Geneva geometry needs care","limited to round or regular pieces"],"failures":["a piece is pinched between the star tip and the wall"],"dof":1,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"servo or motor","output":"stepwise wheel rotation"},"params":[{"key":"pockets","label":"Pockets","unit":"count","min":3,"max":6,"default":4,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","non-ftc"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"A generic mechanism. Check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Transfer Design Fundamentals (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-design.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":0,"servos":1},
{"id":"servo-gate","name":"Servo gate","aliases":["servo release gate","servo hopper gate","door","flap","stopper","release gate"],"category":"inside","tags":["gate","storage"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"servo-gate","type":"gate","short":"A servo-driven flap that blocks or opens a path, holding pieces until you want them to move on.","how":"A servo swings a flap or pin into the piece path. Closed, it holds the queue. Opened, pieces pass by gravity or by a conveyor's push. Upstream is a hopper or conveyor. Downstream is a feeder, shooter or exit.","uses":["holding a hopper","releasing into a shooter","dropping pieces for scoring"],"pros":["cheap","simple","fast"],"cons":["a single flap may pass two pieces"],"failures":["closing on a piece","servo creep"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation","output":"flap open or closed"},"preset":{"states":{"closed":0,"open":70},"upper":90,"lower":0,"speed":500},"params":[{"key":"openAngle","label":"Open angle","unit":"deg","min":20,"max":120,"default":70,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R506"],"note":"Use a servo, not a solenoid (R506 in BIOBUZZ). Check the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Transfer Design Fundamentals (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-design.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":0,"servos":1},
{"id":"escapement","name":"Escapement (two-stop single-piece feeder)","aliases":["escapement feeder","single-element feeder","singulator","double gate","separator gate"],"category":"inside","tags":["gate","feeder"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"servo-gate","parent":"servo-gate","type":"gate","short":"Two stops that open alternately, so exactly one piece is let through each cycle.","how":"A rocker or two linked servos put a front stop and a rear stop in the path. When the front opens, the rear closes behind the first piece, so only that piece leaves. Then they swap. Upstream is a hopper or magazine. Downstream is the shooter or deposit.","uses":["one-at-a-time feeding without sensors"],"pros":["mechanical singulation","robust timing"],"cons":["the stop spacing must match the piece size","two pieces of different sizes complicate it"],"failures":["the spacing is wrong for one piece type"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rocking","output":"alternating stops"},"preset":{"states":{"closed":0,"open":40},"upper":60,"lower":0,"speed":500},"params":[{"key":"stopSpacing","label":"Stop spacing","unit":"mm","min":50,"max":120,"default":95,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","non-ftc"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"A generic mechanism. Check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Transfer Design Fundamentals (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-design.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":0,"servos":1},
{"id":"kicker","name":"Kicker or flicker","aliases":["flicker","pusher","ring pusher","ball kicker","paddle"],"category":"inside","tags":["feeder","transfer"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"kicker","type":"kicker","short":"A servo paddle that pushes one piece out of storage into the shooter or the next stage.","how":"A servo swings a paddle or arm from rest to kick, pushing the piece at the feed position into the flywheel or the next mechanism, then returns. Upstream is a magazine, hopper or spindexer. Downstream is the flywheel or deposit.","uses":["Ultimate Goal ring pushers","feeding a ball from a spindexer pocket"],"pros":["one piece per stroke","cheap"],"cons":["slower than a continuous feed"],"failures":["kicks before the piece is seated","horn strip"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation","output":"paddle push"},"preset":{"states":{"rest":0,"kick":60},"upper":90,"lower":0,"speed":500},"params":[{"key":"kickAngle","label":"Kick travel","unit":"deg","min":20,"max":120,"default":60,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:ultimate-goal"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"A generic mechanism. Check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Transfer Design Fundamentals (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-design.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":0,"servos":1},
{"id":"feeder-wheels","name":"Feeder wheels","aliases":["feeder","feed roller","multi-stage feeder","CR servo feeder","pre-accelerator"],"category":"inside","tags":["feeder","shooter"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"feeder-wheels","type":"feeder","short":"Small spinning wheels that push stored pieces into the shooter at a controlled moment.","how":"A CR servo or small motor spins wheels at the end of storage. Off, they hold pieces. On, they drive pieces into the flywheel. A second, faster stage can pre-accelerate. REV's 2026-27 StarterBot uses a CR servo feeder that runs only once the flywheel is within its velocity range. Upstream is a hopper or conveyor. Downstream is the flywheel.","uses":["feeding a flywheel shooter"],"pros":["simple","continuous or pulsed feeding"],"cons":["needs timing to avoid double feeds"],"failures":["feeding before the flywheel recovers","slipping on worn wheels"],"dof":1,"actuation":"crservo","motion":{"input":"CR servo or motor rotation","output":"pieces pushed forward"},"params":[{"key":"wheelDiameter","label":"Wheel diameter","unit":"mm","min":30,"max":90,"default":60,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"A generic mechanism. Check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"2026-27 REV DUO FTC Starter Bot: programming TeleOp and Auto","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/ftc-kickoff-concepts/home/programming-teleop-and-auto.md","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"Programming Flywheel (REV 2026-27)","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/ftc-kickoff-concepts/home/programming-teleop-and-auto/programming-flywheel.md","kind":"vendor"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":0,"servos":1},
{"id":"diverter","name":"Diverter or sorter flap","aliases":["servo diverter","sensor-controlled diverter","pivoting selection gate","sorter","multi-output routing chute","routing chute","Y-splitter"],"category":"inside","tags":["sorting","gate"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"diverter","type":"diverter","short":"A servo flap that switches which path a piece takes, for example to storage, to a second lane or back out.","how":"A flap at a fork in the path pivots between outputs. A color or distance sensor upstream (or the driver) decides the route. Multi-output chutes chain more flaps or use a rotating chute. Upstream is the intake and sensor. Downstream are storage lanes, the shooter or a reject exit.","uses":["color or size sorting","rejecting wrong pieces"],"pros":["one intake, many destinations"],"cons":["timing with the sensor","extra servo"],"failures":["switching while a piece is under the flap","misreads"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation","output":"path switch"},"preset":{"states":{"pass":0,"divert":45},"upper":60,"lower":0,"speed":500},"params":[{"key":"outputs","label":"Outputs","unit":"count","min":2,"max":3,"default":2,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"A generic mechanism. Game rules about controlling opponent elements may apply. Check the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Color Sensor V3 (REV)","url":"https://www.revrobotics.com/rev-31-1557/","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"Transfer Design Fundamentals (gm0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-design.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":0,"servos":1,"sensors":["color or distance sensor"]},
{"id":"size-selector","name":"Mechanical size selector","aliases":["mechanical size selector","size sorter","gap sorter","sieve","drop-through sorter"],"category":"inside","tags":["sorting","passive"],"entry":"component","family":"diverter","type":null,"short":"A fixed gap or bars that let small pieces drop through and carry big ones past, sorting by size without a sensor.","how":"Pieces roll over rails spaced between the two sizes. The smaller piece (for example 2.8 in. POLLEN) falls through into one lane, and the larger one (3.6 in. NECTAR) rolls on to another. It needs no actuator and works between the intake and a two-lane magazine.","uses":["separating POLLEN from NECTAR in BIOBUZZ"],"pros":["no sensor or servo","instant"],"cons":["sorts only by size, not color","needs room for two lanes"],"failures":["a worn or squashed ball goes to the wrong lane","pieces bounce over the gap"],"dof":0,"actuation":"passive","motion":{"input":"gravity or conveyor push","output":"pieces split by size"},"params":[{"key":"gap","label":"Rail gap","unit":"mm","min":72,"max":90,"default":80,"estimate":true,"note":"between POLLEN ~71 mm and NECTAR ~91 mm"}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"A generic part. Check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML) TU08 (ball sizes)","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":0,"servos":0},
{"id":"flywheel-shooter","name":"Flywheel shooter (single flywheel launcher)","aliases":["flywheel","flywheel launcher","single flywheel","shooter","launcher","wheel launcher","linear shooter","ball shooter","ring shooter","artifact launcher"],"category":"score","tags":["scoring","launcher","shooting","ranged scoring"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"flywheel","type":"flywheel","preset":{"states":{"stop":0,"run":3600},"speed":18000},"short":"A fast-spinning wheel squeezes a game piece against a hood or plate and throws it, so the robot can score from a distance.","how":"A motor spins one wheel, often with extra mass added (a steel flywheel or inset gears) so it keeps its speed when a piece passes through. The piece is pinched between the wheel and a fixed surface (a hood or backing plate) with a little compression, and the friction from the spinning wheel speeds it up and sends it out tangent to the wheel. A feeder (a servo pusher, a feeder wheel or a conveyor) pushes one piece in only once the wheel is back up to speed. If the next piece goes in too early, the wheel is still slow and that shot falls short. The wheel's surface speed is about rpm x pi x diameter / 60. The exit speed is only a fraction of that, depending on compression and slip, so treat the relation as a rough estimate and tune by testing.","uses":["Launching balls or rings into a raised goal from a distance","BIOBUZZ: launching POLLEN or NECTAR into the upward-facing CELL of the HIVE","DECODE and Ultimate Goal style ranged scoring"],"pros":["Scores from a distance without driving up to the goal","Fires again quickly once the wheel recovers its speed","Simple: one motor and one wheel"],"cons":["Shot distance changes with wheel speed, battery voltage and how worn the piece is","Needs a feeder and some way to control the wheel's speed for consistent shots","A single wheel can put spin on the piece, which curves its flight"],"failures":["Shots fall short because the feeder pushes the next piece in before the wheel recovers","The wheel tread wears or gets dirty, so grip and compression change","A flexible frame lets compression vary, so shots scatter","The wheel's set screw or hub slips on the shaft"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"game piece launched along the wheel tangent"},"params":[{"key":"wheelDiameter","label":"Flywheel diameter","unit":"mm","min":50,"max":130,"default":96,"estimate":true,"note":"96 mm matches goBILDA's Hogback wheel, which goBILDA sells a bolt-on steel flywheel for"},{"key":"wheelRpm","label":"Wheel speed","unit":"rpm","min":1000,"max":6000,"default":3000,"estimate":true,"note":"tune by testing; exit speed is only a fraction of surface speed"},{"key":"compression","label":"Compression on the piece","unit":"mm","min":0,"max":20,"default":8,"estimate":true,"note":"how far the gap is smaller than the piece; depends on how soft the piece is"},{"key":"flywheelInertia","label":"Added flywheel inertia","unit":"g·cm²","min":0,"max":5000,"default":1651,"estimate":false,"note":"goBILDA 82 mm steel flywheel, as listed by goBILDA"}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz","historical:decode","historical:ultimate-goal","historical:velocity-vortex"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["G417","G405","R503","R105"],"note":"In BIOBUZZ, launching into the upward-facing CELL is the only way to earn a HIVE TIP (G417), and pieces must not be deliberately ejected from the field (G405). Legality still depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Flywheel Launcher (REV Ultimate Goal kickoff concept)","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/ftc-kickoff-concepts/ultimate-goal-2020-2021/shooter","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"Programming Tips & Tricks (REV DECODE Starter Bot)","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/ftc-kickoff-concepts/decode-2025-26/programming-tips-and-tricks","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"Steel Flywheel (82mm Diameter, 152g, 1651 g·cm²)","url":"https://www.gobilda.com/steel-flywheel-82mm-diameter-152g-1651-g-cm/","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML)","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"}],"image":{"kind":"component-photo","page":"https://www.gobilda.com/steel-flywheel-82mm-diameter-152g-1651-g-cm/","src":"https://cdn11.bigcommerce.com/s-x56mtydx1w/images/stencil/original/products/2424/15239/Ang1__98257__00148__22658__78178.1761676109.jpg?c=1","credit":"goBILDA store listing","shows":"the steel flywheel mass that bolts onto a 96 mm Hogback wheel, not a complete shooter"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":1,"sensors":["motor encoder for velocity"]},
{"id":"dual-flywheel-shooter","name":"Dual-wheel flywheel shooter (opposed or side-by-side wheels)","aliases":["double flywheel","dual-opposed flywheel","dual-wheel compression shooter","side-by-side wheels","over-under wheels","pitching machine shooter","dual-flywheel","two-wheel shooter"],"category":"score","tags":["scoring","launcher","flywheel"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"flywheel","parent":"flywheel-shooter","type":"flywheel","preset":{"states":{"stop":0,"run":3600},"speed":18000},"short":"Two spinning wheels squeeze the game piece between them and push it out, like a baseball pitching machine.","how":"Two wheels spin in opposite directions, so their surfaces move the same way at the gap, and the piece is compressed between them instead of against a hood. They can sit over-under (stacked) or side-by-side (horizontal pinch). Each wheel usually has its own motor, but one motor geared to both also works. With equal surface speeds the piece leaves with little spin. Feed one piece at a time, and only once both wheels are back up to speed.","uses":["Flatter, straighter shots at a goal","Pieces that roll or skid badly against a fixed hood"],"pros":["Both sides of the piece are driven, so less slip and less unwanted spin","Changing the gap between the wheels sets the compression"],"cons":["Two motors use up more of the robot's motor limit","The two wheel speeds have to stay matched or the shot curves","Heavier and wider than a single wheel"],"failures":["One wheel slows more than the other, so shots curve to one side","The wheels move apart under load, so compression drops","A piece jams at the gap when it arrives off-center"],"dof":2,"actuation":"motor+motor","motion":{"input":"two motor rotations","output":"piece launched between the wheels"},"params":[{"key":"wheelDiameter","label":"Wheel diameter","unit":"mm","min":50,"max":130,"default":96,"estimate":true},{"key":"gap","label":"Gap between wheels","unit":"mm","min":20,"max":120,"default":60,"estimate":true,"note":"set a little smaller than the piece"},{"key":"layout","label":"Layout (0 over-under, 1 side-by-side)","unit":"count","min":0,"max":1,"default":0,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz","historical:ultimate-goal","historical:decode"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R503","G417"],"note":"The two motors count toward the 8-motor limit in R503. Legality still depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Flywheel Launcher (REV Ultimate Goal kickoff concept)","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/ftc-kickoff-concepts/ultimate-goal-2020-2021/shooter","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML)","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":2,"servos":1},
{"id":"differential-speed-dual-flywheel","name":"Differential-speed dual flywheel (backspin control)","aliases":["backspin shooter","differential speed shooter","spin-controlled flywheel","top/bottom wheel speed split"],"category":"score","tags":["scoring","launcher","flywheel","advanced"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"flywheel","parent":"dual-flywheel-shooter","type":"flywheel","short":"A two-wheel shooter whose wheels spin at different speeds on purpose, putting spin on the piece to change how it flies.","how":"It is built like an over-under dual flywheel, but each wheel has its own motor and speed setpoint. When one wheel's surface moves faster than the other's, the piece leaves spinning (backspin or topspin) as well as moving forward. Backspin can make a ball float or drop more steeply, depending on the piece. The speed split is found by testing. There is no reliable formula for a given FTC piece.","uses":["Tuning a ball's arc, for example a steeper drop into a cup-shaped goal","Correcting a curve that a single-wheel shooter puts on the piece"],"pros":["A second tuning knob besides wheel speed and hood angle","Can make shots less sensitive to distance"],"cons":["Needs two speed-controlled motors","Effects depend on the piece and are hard to predict; lots of testing","Small speed errors change the spin from shot to shot"],"failures":["The wheel speeds drift apart as the battery drains, changing the spin","One wheel's tread wears faster than the other's"],"dof":2,"actuation":"motor+motor","motion":{"input":"two independently controlled motor rotations","output":"piece launched with chosen spin"},"params":[{"key":"topRpm","label":"Top wheel speed","unit":"rpm","min":500,"max":6000,"default":3000,"estimate":true},{"key":"bottomRpm","label":"Bottom wheel speed","unit":"rpm","min":500,"max":6000,"default":2400,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R503"],"note":"It uses two motors from the R503 limit. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Flywheel Launcher (REV Ultimate Goal kickoff concept)","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/ftc-kickoff-concepts/ultimate-goal-2020-2021/shooter","kind":"vendor"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":2,"sensors":["motor encoders on both wheels"]},
{"id":"hooded-flywheel","name":"Hooded flywheel shooter (fixed hood)","aliases":["hooded shooter","FRC-style hooded flywheel","fixed hood","deflector shooter","curved hood launcher","hood shooter"],"category":"score","tags":["scoring","launcher","flywheel"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"flywheel","parent":"flywheel-shooter","type":"flywheel","preset":{"states":{"stop":0,"run":3600},"speed":18000},"short":"A flywheel with a curved hood over it: the piece rolls between wheel and hood and leaves at the angle the hood sets.","how":"The piece is fed into the gap between a spinning flywheel and a curved hood above it. The hood sets the compression, and its exit end sets the launch angle. The piece rolls along the hood as the wheel speeds it up, then leaves where the hood ends. Most of the compression that matters is at the exit point. Once built, a fixed hood always launches at the same angle, so shot distance is changed only with wheel speed or by moving the robot.","uses":["Ball shooters at a single, well-known shooting position","BIOBUZZ HIVE launching from a fixed spot"],"pros":["Consistent launch angle","Only one wheel needs to be driven","Steady compression along the arc"],"cons":["Fixed angle: distance is changed only through wheel speed","The hood adds height and must stay stiff"],"failures":["The hood flexes or its friction surface wears, so compression changes","The piece snags at the hood entry"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"piece launched at the hood's exit angle"},"params":[{"key":"hoodAngle","label":"Exit angle","unit":"deg","min":20,"max":75,"default":45,"estimate":true},{"key":"compression","label":"Compression","unit":"mm","min":0,"max":20,"default":8,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz","historical:decode"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["G417","G405"],"note":"BIOBUZZ allows launching into the upward-facing CELL (G417). Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Cypher (FTC 23511 Seattle Solvers, DECODE)","url":"https://roboftc.github.io/robots/cypher.html","kind":"team"},{"title":"Starter Bot DECODE (REV, adjustable deflector)","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/ftc-kickoff-concepts/decode-2025-26/starter-bot-decode","kind":"vendor"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1},
{"id":"adjustable-hood","name":"Adjustable hood (variable launch angle)","aliases":["servo hood","hood adjuster","adjustable deflector","angle-changing hood","variable hood","adjustable hood"],"category":"score","tags":["scoring","launcher","aiming"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"flywheel","parent":"hooded-flywheel","type":"hood","preset":{"states":{"low":0,"high":30},"lower":0,"upper":40,"speed":60},"short":"A hood that a servo can tilt, so the shooter changes its launch angle for near and far shots.","how":"The hood pivots about an axis near the flywheel, and a servo moves it through a linkage or gear. Tilting the hood changes where the piece leaves the wheel, and so the exit angle. It often changes compression as well, which should be checked at every setting. Teams usually store a few angle presets matched to wheel speeds for set shooting distances.","uses":["Shooting from several distances without moving the robot","Pairing an angle with a wheel speed from a distance lookup table"],"pros":["Extends range without changing wheel speed","Usually needs only one servo"],"cons":["Compression can change with angle","Takes a lot of tuning to map angle and wheel speed to distance"],"failures":["The servo is backdriven by the shot's reaction force, so the angle drifts","Slop in the linkage gives inconsistent angles"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation","output":"hood pivot (launch angle)"},"params":[{"key":"angleRange","label":"Hood travel","unit":"deg","min":5,"max":60,"default":30,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz","historical:decode"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R502"],"note":"The servo must meet the R502 servo requirements. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Cypher (FTC 23511 Seattle Solvers, DECODE)","url":"https://roboftc.github.io/robots/cypher.html","kind":"team"},{"title":"Starter Bot DECODE (REV)","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/ftc-kickoff-concepts/decode-2025-26/starter-bot-decode","kind":"vendor"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":1},
{"id":"adjustable-compression","name":"Adjustable compression (shooter tuning)","aliases":["compression adjustment","slotted axle","sliding hood mount","compression spacers"],"category":"score","tags":["scoring","flywheel","tuning","prototyping"],"entry":"concept","family":"flywheel","parent":"flywheel-shooter","type":null,"short":"Building a shooter so the squeeze on the game piece can be changed during testing, which is one of its most important tuning settings.","how":"The hood, backing plate or second wheel is mounted on slots, rails or spacers, so its distance from the flywheel can move in small steps. More compression gives more grip and energy transfer, up to a point. Too much slows the wheel and damages pieces. It is set during testing, not during a match. Once it is set the structure has to be stiff, or the compression changes from shot to shot.","uses":["Prototyping a new shooter","Fitting soft or worn game pieces"],"pros":["Lets you find a good setting quickly","Fixes inconsistency without a redesign"],"cons":["A slotted mount can slip if it isn't clamped well"],"failures":["Mount slips under impact and compression changes mid-event"],"dof":0,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"compression","label":"Compression","unit":"mm","min":0,"max":20,"default":8,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz","historical:ultimate-goal"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"This is a design practice, not a part. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Flywheel Launcher (REV Ultimate Goal kickoff concept)","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/ftc-kickoff-concepts/ultimate-goal-2020-2021/shooter","kind":"vendor"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"closed-loop-flywheel-control","name":"Closed-loop flywheel velocity control","aliases":["flywheel PIDF","velocity PID","setVelocity","feedforward flywheel","shoot when at speed","RUN_USING_ENCODER shooter"],"category":"score","tags":["software","flywheel","control"],"entry":"software","family":"flywheel","parent":"flywheel-shooter","type":null,"short":"Code that holds the flywheel at a target speed using its encoder, and lets the feeder fire only when the speed is right.","how":"The motor's encoder measures wheel speed in ticks per second. A feedforward term (F) supplies most of the power needed for the target speed, and a proportional term (P) corrects the error, which matters most after each shot. The feeder is allowed to run only when the measured speed is within a window around the target. A short settling delay after spin-up avoids a strong first shot from overshoot. REV's DECODE guide uses only P and F, and warns that too much fluctuation feeds balls too early.","uses":["Consistent shots as the battery drains","Fast multi-shot bursts that wait for the wheel to recover"],"pros":["Much more consistent than setting raw power","Recovers faster between shots"],"cons":["Needs tuning","Encoder noise at high speed needs filtering"],"failures":["P is set too high, so speed oscillates and the feeder fires at the wrong time","An unsupported motor or old firmware throws an error when setting PIDF coefficients"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"targetTicksPerSec","label":"Target velocity","unit":"ticks/s","min":200,"max":2800,"default":1500,"estimate":true},{"key":"readyWindow","label":"Fire window","unit":"ticks/s","min":10,"max":200,"default":50,"estimate":false,"note":"REV's DECODE tutorial starts the feeder at 50 ticks below target"}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz","historical:decode"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Software; legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Programming Tips & Tricks (REV DECODE Starter Bot)","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/ftc-kickoff-concepts/decode-2025-26/programming-tips-and-tricks","kind":"vendor"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["motor encoder"]},
{"id":"kicker-wheel","name":"Kicker / accelerator wheel","aliases":["kicker wheel","accelerator wheel","two-stage launcher","kicker-wheel ejector"],"category":"score","tags":["inside","transfer","flywheel","feeding"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"flywheel","parent":"feeder-wheels","type":"feeder","preset":{"states":{"stop":0,"run":3600},"speed":3600},"short":"A smaller powered wheel that grabs the piece and pushes it into the main flywheel at speed, or ejects it on its own.","how":"A motor- or servo-driven wheel sits just before the main flywheel. When it runs, it pulls the waiting piece in and hands it to the flywheel at a controlled moment, so it works as the feeder too. In REV's Ultimate Goal launcher, a first wheel brought the ring to one speed and a second motor's wheel took it to a higher final speed, which made the shots flatter. When it is stopped, it holds the next piece back.","uses":["Feeding pieces into a flywheel one at a time","Two-stage launching for higher exit speed"],"pros":["Repeatable feed timing","The extra stage can raise exit speed"],"cons":["Another motor or servo","The gap to the main wheel must be tuned"],"failures":["Feeds before the main wheel recovers","Two pieces get pulled in at once"],"dof":1,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"wheel rotation","output":"piece pushed into the main wheel"},"params":[{"key":"wheelDiameter","label":"Wheel diameter","unit":"mm","min":30,"max":100,"default":60,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz","historical:ultimate-goal","historical:decode"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R503"],"note":"It counts toward the motor and servo limits. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Flywheel Launcher (REV Ultimate Goal kickoff concept)","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/ftc-kickoff-concepts/ultimate-goal-2020-2021/shooter","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"Programming Tips & Tricks (REV DECODE Starter Bot)","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/ftc-kickoff-concepts/decode-2025-26/programming-tips-and-tricks","kind":"vendor"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1},
{"id":"fixed-angle-shooter","name":"Fixed-angle shooter (no aiming joint)","aliases":["static launcher","drive-to-aim shooter","fixed-angle launcher","fixed shooter"],"category":"score","tags":["scoring","aiming","beginner"],"entry":"concept","family":"flywheel","parent":"flywheel-shooter","type":"flywheel","short":"A shooter bolted to the robot at one angle: you aim by turning the robot and set distance with wheel speed.","how":"The launcher has no pivot or turret, so its direction is the robot's heading and its elevation is fixed. Distance is set by wheel speed or by shooting from known spots on the field. Teams often line up with AprilTags or field features, or use auto-heading on the drivetrain. It is the simplest architecture and the one most starter bots use.","uses":["Starter bots and first-year teams","Shooting from fixed field positions"],"pros":["Fewest parts and failure points","Easy to make rigid and consistent"],"cons":["The whole robot must turn to aim","Fewer ways to tune range"],"failures":["Drivetrain heading drift makes shots miss","Getting bumped while aiming"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","params":[{"key":"launchAngle","label":"Fixed launch angle","unit":"deg","min":15,"max":75,"default":45,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz","historical:decode"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Starter Bot DECODE (REV)","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/ftc-kickoff-concepts/decode-2025-26/starter-bot-decode","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"FTC Starter Bot Resource Guide (DECODE)","url":"https://www.gobilda.com/ftc-starter-bot-resource-guide-decode/","kind":"vendor"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1},
{"id":"pivoting-shooter","name":"Pivoting shooter (tilting launcher)","aliases":["shooter pivot","tilting shooter","elevation pivot","angled launcher","pitch-adjust shooter"],"category":"score","tags":["scoring","aiming","arm"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"pivot","type":"shooter-pivot","preset":{"states":{"low":20,"high":55},"lower":10,"upper":70,"speed":45},"short":"The whole launcher tilts on a pivot to change its shot angle, instead of moving only a hood.","how":"The flywheel, hood and feeder are mounted on a frame that pivots on a shaft, driven by a motor through a gear or belt reduction, or by a strong servo. Tilting the whole assembly changes launch angle without changing compression. The pivot has to carry the launcher's weight and the recoil from each shot, so it usually needs an encoder or absolute sensor and position hold.","uses":["Long and short shots from one robot","Also tilting down to meet an intake for a transfer"],"pros":["Compression doesn't change with angle","Large angle range"],"cons":["Moves a heavy mass","Changes the robot's center of gravity"],"failures":["Recoil backdrives the pivot","Slop in the gear train causes angle error"],"dof":2,"actuation":"motor+motor","motion":{"input":"pivot motor rotation","output":"launcher elevation angle"},"params":[{"key":"pivotRange","label":"Pivot travel","unit":"deg","min":10,"max":90,"default":45,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R105","R503"],"note":"Tilted positions must stay inside the R105 expansion volume. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML)","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"},{"title":"Arms (Game Manual 0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/arms.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":2,"sensors":["pivot encoder or absolute encoder"]},
{"id":"turret","name":"Turret (rotating base for a shooter, arm or slides)","aliases":["full turret","rotating turret","turret-mounted shooter","belt-driven turret","gear-driven turret","chain-driven turret","direct-drive turret","lazy susan turret","bearing stack turret","center-bearing turret","x-contact bearing turret"],"category":"score","tags":["aiming","scoring","arm","lift"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"turret","type":"turret","preset":{"states":{"front":0,"left":90,"right":-90},"lower":-180,"upper":180,"speed":90},"short":"A rotating platform that turns a shooter, arm or slides side to side, so the robot can aim without turning its drivetrain.","how":"A disc or plate rides on a lazy susan, a stack of bearings around its edge, or a large central (thrust or x-contact) bearing. A motor or servos drive it through a large gear, sprocket or pulley on the turret. A belt or gear reduction is most common, and direct drive works only for light loads. An encoder (motor or through-bore) gives the angle, with a limit switch or absolute encoder for homing. Wires that cross the joint need a service loop or a COTS slip ring.","uses":["Aiming a flywheel at the goal while driving","Rotating slides to score in different directions (Power Play)","Turning an arm to reach either side"],"pros":["Aims without moving the drivetrain","Faster, more precise aiming than turning the robot"],"cons":["Heavy and complex","Cables crossing the rotation","Side loads from a shooter can damage a cheap lazy susan"],"failures":["Belt skips or gear backlash cause aim error","Wire harness pulled out when over-rotated","Lazy susan racks under lateral load","Encoder loses home after a collision"],"dof":1,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"motor or servo rotation through gear, belt or chain","output":"yaw rotation of the platform"},"params":[{"key":"range","label":"Rotation range","unit":"deg","min":90,"max":360,"default":270,"estimate":true},{"key":"ratio","label":"Drive reduction to turret","unit":":1","min":1,"max":20,"default":5,"estimate":true},{"key":"drive","label":"Drive (0 belt, 1 gear, 2 chain, 3 direct)","unit":"count","min":0,"max":3,"default":0,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz","historical:decode","historical:power-play","historical:freight-frenzy","historical:ultimate-goal"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R303"],"note":"A single-DoF COTS part under R303 (not one of its listed exceptions); legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Turrets (Game Manual 0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/turrets/index.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Full Turrets (Game Manual 0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/turrets/full-turret.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Cypher (FTC 23511 Seattle Solvers, DECODE)","url":"https://roboftc.github.io/robots/cypher.html","kind":"team"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/turrets/full-turret.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/19818-turret.jpg","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo by FTC 19818 Jade Innovations (Power Play lazy susan turret)"},"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"sensors":["encoder","limit switch or absolute encoder"]},
{"id":"limited-angle-turret","name":"Limited-angle turret (hard-stopped, wire-wrap)","aliases":["limited turret","partial turret","soft-limited turret","180 degree turret","non-continuous turret"],"category":"score","tags":["aiming","turret"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"turret","parent":"turret","type":"turret","preset":{"states":{"front":0,"left":90,"right":-90},"lower":-135,"upper":135,"speed":90},"short":"A turret that rotates only within a set range, so its wires can simply flex instead of needing a slip ring.","how":"It is built like a full turret, but travel is limited by software limits and physical hard stops. Wires pass through or near the center with a service loop sized for the full range. To aim beyond the limit, the code wraps around the other way or turns the drivetrain. Homing at startup or an absolute encoder keeps the limits correct.","uses":["Most FTC turrets, which only need about 180 to 270 degrees"],"pros":["No slip ring","Simpler and more robust wiring"],"cons":["Must unwind to reach targets past the limit","The software limits must be correct"],"failures":["Lost homing lets it drive into the hard stop and pull wires","Cable fatigue from repeated flexing"],"dof":1,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"motor or servo rotation","output":"bounded yaw rotation"},"params":[{"key":"range","label":"Allowed range","unit":"deg","min":90,"max":330,"default":270,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R105"],"note":"Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Turrets (Game Manual 0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/turrets/index.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["encoder","limit switch"]},
{"id":"slip-ring-turret","name":"Continuous-rotation turret with slip ring (concept)","aliases":["slip ring turret","continuous turret","infinite turret","360 turret"],"category":"score","tags":["aiming","turret","electrical"],"entry":"concept","family":"turret","parent":"turret","type":"turret","short":"A turret that can spin endlessly in either direction because power and signals cross the joint through a slip ring.","how":"A slip ring has brushes that slide on rotating metal rings, carrying circuits across a joint that never stops turning. With it mounted on the turret axis, motors and sensors on the turret stay connected at any angle, so the aim code never has to unwind. The center of the turret must leave room for the slip ring, which conflicts with center-fed shooters.","uses":["Turrets that track a target continuously while the robot spins"],"pros":["No unwinding, always takes the shortest path"],"cons":["Brush wear and contact noise","Current and circuit limits","FTC allows only unmodified COTS slip rings on power and control paths"],"failures":["Intermittent contact causes motor or sensor dropouts","Brush wear debris"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","params":[{"key":"circuits","label":"Slip ring circuits","unit":"count","min":2,"max":12,"default":6,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R612"],"note":"R612 requires slip rings on critical power and control paths to be unmodified COTS items and bans custom ones. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Slip ring (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slip_ring","kind":"engineering"},{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML)","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"}],"image":{"kind":"schematic","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slip_ring","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Electric_Motor_with_Slip_Rings.svg/330px-Electric_Motor_with_Slip_Rings.svg.png","credit":"Wikimedia Commons, via the Wikipedia article Slip ring","shows":"cross-section sketch of slip rings on an electric motor, not a robot turret"},"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"vision-assisted-turret","name":"Vision-assisted turret aiming","aliases":["turret tracking","AprilTag aim","Limelight aim","tx aim"],"category":"score","tags":["software","sense","aiming","turret"],"entry":"software","family":"turret","parent":"vision-assisted-aiming","type":null,"short":"A camera finds the goal, often by its AprilTag, and code turns the turret to point the shooter at it.","how":"A camera mounted on the robot or on the turret detects a goal marker, such as an AprilTag, and reports its bearing (horizontal offset) and range. A control loop drives the turret to make the bearing zero, and range can pick the flywheel speed or hood angle from a table. With the camera on the turret the loop is direct. With the camera on the chassis, the code adds the turret's encoder angle. FTC's SDK reports AprilTag pose, and the Limelight 3A also offers built-in AprilTag tracking.","uses":["Aiming while driving","Picking shot speed from distance"],"pros":["Fast, repeatable aiming","Works from many positions"],"cons":["Camera latency and lighting sensitivity","Needs a calibrated camera for pose"],"failures":["Target lost or misdetected, so the turret swings away","Latency makes it overshoot and oscillate"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"aimTolerance","label":"Aim tolerance","unit":"deg","min":0.5,"max":5,"default":1.5,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz","historical:decode"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R708","R702","R202"],"note":"Vision devices must follow R708 (single image sensor) and the R702 coprocessor list (the Limelight 3A is listed). Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"AprilTag Introduction (FTC Docs)","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/apriltag/vision_portal/apriltag_intro/apriltag-intro.html","kind":"official"},{"title":"Limelight 3A Smart Camera (ServoCity)","url":"https://www.servocity.com/limelight-3a-smart-camera","kind":"vendor"}],"image":{"kind":"component-photo","page":"https://www.servocity.com/limelight-3a-smart-camera","src":"https://cdn11.bigcommerce.com/s-f6vfspkkjf/images/stencil/2400x2400/products/4945/22606/Ang1__39895.1727454484.jpg?c=1","credit":"ServoCity store listing","shows":"Limelight 3A camera only, not a turret"},"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["webcam or Limelight 3A","turret encoder"]},
{"id":"catapult","name":"Catapult (motor-wound launching arm)","aliases":["motor catapult","flinger","launching arm","rotating throwing arm","throwing arm","mangonel","onager"],"category":"score","tags":["launcher","scoring"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"catapult","type":"catapult","preset":{"states":{"cocked":0,"fired":90},"lower":0,"upper":110,"speed":360},"short":"An arm that swings fast and stops hard, throwing the game piece sitting in its cup.","how":"A motor drives the arm directly through a high-speed gear train, or winds it back against springs or surgical tubing and then releases it. The arm rotates through an arc, and when it hits a stop the piece leaves the cup tangent to the arc. Release angle and arm speed set the trajectory. Unlike a flywheel, it fires one piece per cycle and must reload or re-cock.","uses":["Lobbing balls into high goals","Historical ball games such as Velocity Vortex"],"pros":["Very repeatable when cocked to the same point","Simple once tuned; can throw high arcs"],"cons":["One piece per cycle, slow to reload","Shock loads at the stop","Stored-energy designs can be unsafe to handle"],"failures":["The stop or arm cracks from repeated impact","Springs or tubing weaken, so range drops","Gear teeth strip at the stop"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation (direct or winding)","output":"arm swing and piece release"},"params":[{"key":"armLength","label":"Arm length","unit":"mm","min":100,"max":350,"default":200,"estimate":true},{"key":"releaseAngle","label":"Stop/release angle","unit":"deg","min":45,"max":110,"default":80,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz","historical:velocity-vortex"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R301","R801","G417"],"note":"Stored spring energy is not specifically addressed in the rule text checked; R801 limits pneumatics. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Catapult (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catapult","kind":"engineering"},{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML)","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"}],"image":{"kind":"schematic","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catapult","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/18/Mang2.png","credit":"Wikimedia Commons, via the Wikipedia article Catapult","shows":"basic diagram of an onager, a historical catapult, not an FTC mechanism"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"historical","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1},
{"id":"servo-release-catapult","name":"Servo-release catapult (latched spring arm)","aliases":["latched catapult","spring catapult","surgical tubing catapult","elastic catapult","trigger catapult"],"category":"score","tags":["launcher","scoring"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"catapult","parent":"catapult","type":"catapult","preset":{"states":{"cocked":0,"fired":90},"lower":0,"upper":110,"speed":720},"short":"A catapult arm loaded by springs or elastic and held by a servo latch until the servo lets go.","how":"Elastic bands, surgical tubing or springs pull the arm forward. The arm is wound back by a motor or winch, or by the intake motion, and held by a latch hook. A servo pulls the latch, the stored energy swings the arm, and it stops against a bumper. The winding must then re-cock it before the next shot.","uses":["Lobbing pieces with few motors"],"pros":["The servo only triggers; the elastic supplies the power","High arm speed"],"cons":["Elastic weakens and changes with temperature","A stuck latch or accidental release is a safety risk","Slow to re-cock"],"failures":["Latch slips and fires early","Tubing breaks","Re-cock winch tangles"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor+servo","motion":{"input":"stored elastic energy, released by a servo","output":"arm swing"},"params":[{"key":"bandCount","label":"Elastic bands or tubes","unit":"count","min":1,"max":8,"default":2,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R801"],"note":"The rule text checked has no explicit spring/elastic rule; check the current manual and Q&A for stored-energy limits. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Catapult (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catapult","kind":"engineering"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":1},
{"id":"cam-driven-launcher","name":"Cam-driven launcher (snail or nautilus cam)","aliases":["snail cam","nautilus cam","cam catapult","cam puncher","cam-wound launcher"],"category":"score","tags":["launcher","scoring"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"catapult","parent":"catapult","type":"catapult","preset":{"states":{"cocked":0,"fired":90},"lower":0,"upper":110,"speed":720},"short":"A spiral-shaped cam turns slowly to wind up a spring-loaded arm, then drops off its edge to fire it, with no latch needed.","how":"A motor turns a snail-shaped (spiral) cam continuously in one direction. A follower on the arm rides up the cam's growing radius, stretching the springs or elastic. When the follower reaches the cam's step it drops off, and the arm fires. Turning further starts the next wind-up, so one motor running one way both cocks and fires, and the shot timing is set by the cam angle.","uses":["Repeated launching from one motor with no servo trigger"],"pros":["No separate latch or trigger","Repeatable energy per shot"],"cons":["The cam and follower see high contact loads","Fire timing depends on reading the cam angle"],"failures":["Follower wears or the cam step chips","Motor stalls at the peak of the wind-up"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"continuous motor rotation of the cam","output":"periodic arm firing"},"params":[{"key":"camLift","label":"Cam rise","unit":"mm","min":10,"max":60,"default":30,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:velocity-vortex"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R801"],"note":"Spring energy is not specifically addressed in the rules checked. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Catapult (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catapult","kind":"engineering"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"low","status":"historical","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1},
{"id":"roller-ejector","name":"Roller ejector (reverse-intake outtake)","aliases":["outtake rollers","roller outtake","spit-out","reverse intake","ejector rollers","roller deposit"],"category":"score","tags":["intake","scoring","transfer"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"roller-intake","type":"roller-intake","preset":{"states":{"stop":0,"run":-2160},"speed":2400},"short":"Spinning rollers push the piece out at low speed to drop or place it, often by running the intake in reverse.","how":"The same rollers or compliant wheels that pull a piece in are run backward to push it out in a controlled way. Roller speed and direction set how gently it leaves, so it places pieces rather than launching them. On flip-up transfers the intake reverses to hand a piece to the scorer.","uses":["Placing pieces in a low goal, garden or zone","Handing a piece to a scoring mechanism"],"pros":["No extra mechanism when it is the intake run backward","Gentle, controlled release"],"cons":["Short range only","Needs the robot positioned close to the target"],"failures":["Piece snags on the frame on the way out","Two pieces ejected at once"],"dof":1,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"roller rotation (reverse)","output":"piece pushed out"},"params":[{"key":"rollerDiameter","label":"Roller diameter","unit":"mm","min":20,"max":100,"default":48,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["G405"],"note":"Ejecting pieces off the field on purpose is a foul in BIOBUZZ (G405). Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Transfers (Game Manual 0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1},
{"id":"scoring-gate","name":"Servo scoring gate","aliases":["gate","trapdoor"],"category":"score","tags":["inside","scoring","transfer"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"gate","type":"gate","preset":{"states":{"closed":0,"open":90},"lower":0,"upper":120,"speed":300},"short":"A servo-driven flap that holds pieces in a bucket, chute or magazine and opens to let them out.","how":"A plate or finger on a servo horn blocks the exit of a holder. Turning the servo swings it out of the way, and gravity or a roller moves the pieces out. A quick open-close lets one piece through at a time. Because the gate itself takes impact from pieces, a strong servo and a stiff mount matter.","uses":["Releasing pieces from a deposit bucket","Metering pieces into a shooter","BIOBUZZ: dropping pieces into a FLOWER from above"],"pros":["Very simple and light","Fast"],"cons":["Only on/off control unless timed carefully","Pieces can wedge in the gap"],"failures":["Servo stalls against a jammed piece","Gate deflects and leaks pieces"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation","output":"gate pivot"},"params":[{"key":"openAngle","label":"Open angle","unit":"deg","min":30,"max":180,"default":90,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz","historical:freight-frenzy","historical:centerstage"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R502"],"note":"Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Transfers (Game Manual 0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Choosing a Servo (Game Manual 0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/power-and-electronics/servo-guide/choosing-servo.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":1,"parent":"servo-gate"},
{"id":"tilting-bucket","name":"Tilting deposit bucket (dumper)","aliases":["bucket","deposit bucket","deposit box","dumper","dump bucket","outtake box","scoring bucket","tipping bucket","freight box"],"category":"score","tags":["scoring","intake","transfer","lift"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"bucket","type":"bucket","preset":{"states":{"hold":0,"dump":120},"lower":0,"upper":150,"speed":240},"short":"An open box on a pivot that holds pieces and tips them out into the goal.","how":"A box or cup, often mounted on slides or an arm, catches pieces from the intake (directly behind it on transferless robots). A servo, or sometimes a motor, rotates it about a pivot near one edge until the pieces slide out over the lip into the target. Lip shape and tilt angle control where pieces land, and some buckets add a gate to release them one at a time.","uses":["Freight Frenzy and similar dumping deposits","Dropping pieces into a goal or FLOWER from above"],"pros":["Holds several pieces","Simple and forgiving about how pieces are aligned"],"cons":["Low precision about where pieces land","Shock loads on the servo"],"failures":["Servo gears strip from shock loads (gm0 warns brass gears suffer here)","Pieces bounce out while driving","Bucket hits the goal on tilt"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation","output":"bucket tilt"},"params":[{"key":"dumpAngle","label":"Dump angle","unit":"deg","min":60,"max":180,"default":120,"estimate":true},{"key":"capacity","label":"Pieces held","unit":"count","min":1,"max":6,"default":2,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz","historical:freight-frenzy","historical:rover-ruckus"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R105"],"note":"Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Transfers (Game Manual 0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Choosing a Servo (Game Manual 0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/power-and-electronics/servo-guide/choosing-servo.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":1},
{"id":"rotating-scoring-bucket","name":"Rotating (flip-over) scoring bucket","aliases":["flip bucket","flipping deposit","rotating bucket","flip-over outtake","180 bucket"],"category":"score","tags":["scoring","transfer"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"bucket","parent":"tilting-bucket","type":"bucket","preset":{"states":{"hold":0,"dump":180},"lower":0,"upper":200,"speed":240},"short":"A bucket that flips over far, often past vertical, to carry pieces up and over and dump them on the other side.","how":"Instead of tipping a little, the bucket pivots through a large arc, often around 180 degrees, usually on a servo or through a belt from a remote servo. It receives pieces facing the intake and flips over the top to release them toward the goal behind or above. Because the pieces ride through the flip, a lip or gate keeps them in until the end of the arc.","uses":["Receiving from an intake on one side and scoring on the other"],"pros":["Combines transfer and deposit in one motion"],"cons":["Pieces can fall out mid-flip","Large swing envelope"],"failures":["Pieces fly out early","Servo can't hold position against the load"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation","output":"large-angle bucket rotation"},"params":[{"key":"flipAngle","label":"Flip angle","unit":"deg","min":120,"max":220,"default":180,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:rover-ruckus","historical:freight-frenzy"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R105"],"note":"Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Transfers (Game Manual 0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"low","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":1},
{"id":"wrist-mounted-deposit","name":"Wrist-mounted deposit","aliases":["deposit wrist","outtake wrist","wrist deposit","claw on wrist"],"category":"score","tags":["arm","scoring","wrist"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"wrist","type":"wrist","preset":{"states":{"down":0,"up":90},"lower":-30,"upper":180,"speed":240},"short":"A deposit (bucket, box or gripper) on a servo wrist at the end of an arm or slide, so it can be angled to suit the goal.","how":"A servo at the end of the arm or slide rotates the deposit relative to the arm. The wrist keeps the deposit level while the arm moves, then tilts it to release pieces at the right angle. It is often paired with a virtual four-bar or slides, and sometimes with a roll servo (a two-servo or differential wrist).","uses":["Placing pieces at a precise angle","Keeping pieces level while lifting"],"pros":["Fine control of release angle","Works at many heights"],"cons":["Adds mass at the end of the arm","Extra servo"],"failures":["Wrist servo overloaded by a long deposit","Servo backlash makes the deposit wobble"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation","output":"deposit pitch relative to the arm"},"params":[{"key":"wristRange","label":"Wrist travel","unit":"deg","min":90,"max":270,"default":180,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz","historical:centerstage","historical:into-the-deep"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R502"],"note":"Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Linkages (Game Manual 0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linkages.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Choosing a Servo (Game Manual 0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/power-and-electronics/servo-guide/choosing-servo.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":1},
{"id":"gravity-drop-chute","name":"Gravity drop chute","aliases":["chute","drop chute","slide chute","gravity feed","drop tube"],"category":"score","tags":["inside","transfer","scoring"],"entry":"component","family":"chute","type":null,"short":"A sloped channel that lets pieces roll or slide down by gravity into a goal or another mechanism.","how":"A ramp or tube is angled steeply enough that pieces slide or roll without help. Pieces enter at the top from an intake or deposit and leave at the bottom into the target. It can be fixed, with zero joints, or tilted by a servo to aim or close it, and a gate at the bottom meters pieces.","uses":["Feeding pieces from intake to a shooter","Dropping pieces into a low goal"],"pros":["No power needed","Very simple and light"],"cons":["Only moves pieces downhill","Slow for sticky or irregular pieces"],"failures":["Pieces jam where the chute narrows or turns","Too shallow an angle stalls pieces"],"dof":0,"actuation":"passive","motion":{"input":"gravity","output":"piece slides down the chute"},"params":[{"key":"slope","label":"Chute angle","unit":"deg","min":15,"max":80,"default":35,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Transfers (Game Manual 0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","parent":"guide-channel"},
{"id":"controlled-release-funnel","name":"Controlled-release funnel","aliases":["hopper funnel","metering funnel","single-file release","funnel with gate"],"category":"score","tags":["inside","scoring","transfer"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"gate","type":"gate","preset":{"states":{"closed":0,"open":90},"lower":0,"upper":120,"speed":300},"short":"A funnel that lines pieces up single file, with a gate at the narrow end to release them one at a time.","how":"Sloped walls guide loose pieces into a single-file exit sized just over one piece. A servo gate, or a pin that pops in and out, blocks the exit. Opening briefly lets one piece drop, which keeps release order and count predictable. The funnel's walls also center pieces over a target below.","uses":["Dropping one piece at a time into a small target","BIOBUZZ: placing pieces into a FLOWER from the top","Metering pieces into a launcher"],"pros":["Counts out pieces reliably","Self-centers pieces"],"cons":["Pieces can bridge (arch) across the funnel and stop","Takes vertical space"],"failures":["Two pieces jam side by side at the throat","Gate closes on a piece"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo opens the gate","output":"one piece released"},"params":[{"key":"throat","label":"Throat width","unit":"mm","min":50,"max":120,"default":100,"estimate":true,"note":"just over the largest piece; BIOBUZZ NECTAR is about 3.6 in (91 mm)"}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["G410"],"note":"In BIOBUZZ, FLOWER scoring can begin only with one minute left (G410). Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML)","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"low","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":1},
{"id":"multi-level-deposit","name":"Multi-level deposit (preset scoring heights)","aliases":["multi-height scoring","preset heights","level scoring","tiered deposit","junction heights"],"category":"score","tags":["lift","scoring","software"],"entry":"concept","family":"linear-slide","type":"lift","preset":{"states":{"down":0,"low":10,"mid":20,"high":30},"lower":0,"upper":32,"speed":30},"short":"A deposit carried by a lift or arm that stops at set heights to score at low, middle or high goals.","how":"A lift (slides, elevator or arm) carries the deposit, and a motor encoder drives it to stored positions for each goal level. One button per level makes scoring repeatable. Accurate stops need a run-to-position or PID controller, and gravity compensation for vertical lifts.","uses":["Goals at several heights (Power Play junctions, CENTERSTAGE backdrop rows)"],"pros":["Fast, repeatable driver controls","One mechanism serves every goal height"],"cons":["Needs encoder control and homing","Tall lifts sway"],"failures":["Encoder drift after a missed home","Slides sag under load and land low"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"deposit height"},"params":[{"key":"maxHeight","label":"Maximum height","unit":"in","min":6,"max":29,"default":24,"estimate":true,"note":"R105 limits robot height to 29 in during the match"}],"seasons":["general","historical:power-play","historical:centerstage","historical:into-the-deep"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R105"],"note":"The robot must stay within the 18 x 24 x 29 in. expansion volume (R105.A). Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Linear Motion Guide (Game Manual 0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/index.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML)","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":2,"sensors":["motor encoder","limit switch for homing"]},
{"id":"slide-mounted-scoring","name":"Slide- or elevator-mounted scoring assembly","aliases":["elevator-mounted scoring","slide outtake","lift with deposit","vertical slides with bucket","outtake slides","elevator deposit"],"category":"score","tags":["lift","scoring","architecture"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"linear-slide","type":"linear-slide","preset":{"states":{"in":0,"out":20},"lower":0,"upper":28,"speed":30},"short":"A bucket, gate or gripper riding on the carriage of linear slides, so it can be raised or reached out to the goal.","how":"Linear slides (drawer, extrusion or bearing slides), rigged with string or belt, are driven by a motor spool or pulley. The deposit mounts on the last stage's carriage. Vertical slides make an elevator, and angled or horizontal slides add reach. A servo deposit (bucket, gate or wrist) at the end does the release.","uses":["High goals and baskets","Reaching over or into field elements"],"pros":["Long, straight, predictable travel","The deposit stays at a consistent angle"],"cons":["Rigging and tension upkeep","Tall slides sway and can tip the robot"],"failures":["String snaps or comes off the spool","Slides bind when one side lags"],"dof":2,"actuation":"motor+servo","motion":{"input":"motor spool rotation, plus a servo for the deposit","output":"carriage translation and deposit release"},"params":[{"key":"travel","label":"Slide travel","unit":"in","min":6,"max":28,"default":20,"estimate":true},{"key":"angle","label":"Slide angle from horizontal","unit":"deg","min":0,"max":90,"default":90,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz","historical:freight-frenzy","historical:centerstage","historical:into-the-deep"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R105","R301"],"note":"Must stay within the R105.A expansion volume; R301 bars COTS major mechanisms designed for the game task. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Linear Motion Guide (Game Manual 0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/index.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Types of Transfers (Game Manual 0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":2,"servos":1,"sensors":["motor encoder"]},
{"id":"index-and-release","name":"Index-and-release scoring system","aliases":["indexer","servo pusher","index and fire","magazine indexer","spindexer feed"],"category":"score","tags":["inside","transfer","launcher"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"indexer","type":"kicker","preset":{"states":{"rest":0,"kick":40},"lower":0,"upper":60,"speed":600},"short":"Holds a stack or row of pieces and pushes out exactly one at a time into the shooter or goal.","how":"Pieces wait in a magazine, stack or rotating carousel. A servo pusher (flicker) sweeps one piece forward into the flywheel or deposit, then returns, and the next piece moves into place by gravity, a spring or a carousel turn. Code times each push to the shooter's ready signal, so a piece is never fed early.","uses":["Ultimate Goal style ring stacks pushed into a flywheel","Burst-firing a three-piece load","DECODE-style multi-piece magazines"],"pros":["Known count and timing per shot","Compact storage"],"cons":["Each push takes a moment","Misalignment jams the stack"],"failures":["Pusher double-feeds","Servo stalls on a jammed piece","Pusher fires while the wheel is still recovering"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo stroke","output":"one piece moved into the launcher"},"params":[{"key":"magazine","label":"Pieces held","unit":"count","min":1,"max":5,"default":3,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz","historical:ultimate-goal","historical:decode"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R502"],"note":"Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Programming Tips & Tricks (REV DECODE Starter Bot)","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/ftc-kickoff-concepts/decode-2025-26/programming-tips-and-tricks","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"FTC Starter Bot Resource Guide (DECODE)","url":"https://www.gobilda.com/ftc-starter-bot-resource-guide-decode/","kind":"vendor"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":1,"parent":"kicker"},
{"id":"modular-drivetrain-chassis","name":"Modular kit drivetrain chassis","aliases":["kit chassis","Strafer chassis","BeeLine chassis","Tile Runner","channel chassis","modular chassis","COTS drive chassis"],"category":"drive","tags":["drive","architecture"],"entry":"architecture","family":"chassis","type":null,"short":"A drive base built from a vendor's channel, wheels and motors that can be rearranged into mecanum, 6-wheel or tank layouts.","how":"Standard channel or extrusion, bearings and motor mounts bolt together on a fixed hole pattern, so wheelbase, wheel type and motor layout can change without new parts. Kits like the goBILDA Strafer (mecanum) or BeeLine (6WD) come as complete bases, and teams then mount mechanisms to the same pattern. Holonomic (mecanum) layouts give three controllable planar DOF. Tank layouts give two.","uses":["A reliable base for first-year and veteran teams","A starting point for adding dead wheels and mechanisms"],"pros":["Fast to build and repair","Good documentation and CAD"],"cons":["Heavier and less compact than custom plates","Shared hole patterns limit some layouts"],"failures":["Loose set screws and shaft collars","Frame racking from hits, so the wheels misalign"],"dof":null,"actuation":"motor","params":[{"key":"wheelbase","label":"Wheelbase","unit":"mm","min":200,"max":440,"default":336,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R301","R102"],"note":"R301 exempts COTS drive chassis from the COTS major mechanism ban if no other rule is broken. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Sample Drivetrains (Game Manual 0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/drivetrains/drivetrain-gallery.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML)","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":4},
{"id":"variable-wheelbase-chassis","name":"Transforming or expandable wheelbase chassis","aliases":["variable wheelbase","expandable wheelbase","transforming chassis","foldable vehicle","wheel-leg transformable robot","variable footprint"],"category":"robot","tags":["drive","non-ftc","research"],"entry":"architecture","family":"chassis","type":null,"short":"A research-style chassis that folds, spreads or reshapes its wheels or wheelbase to cross obstacles or fit through gaps.","how":"An actuator changes the angle between chassis plates, or a linkage changes how far apart the wheels are, altering the footprint on the move. Some designs instead use wheels that open into leg segments to climb. Changing footprint trades stability (wide) against fitting through narrow spaces (narrow).","uses":["Stair climbing and crossing gaps (research robots)"],"pros":["One robot adapts to several terrains"],"cons":["Complex and heavy","Rarely useful on a flat FTC field"],"failures":["Fold mechanism jams or loses position under load"],"dof":1,"actuation":"varies","params":[{"key":"footprintChange","label":"Footprint change","unit":"%","min":0,"max":60,"default":30,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["non-ftc"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R105"],"note":"A design from outside FTC, not marked FTC-legal; any expansion would also have to respect FTC size limits."},"refs":[{"title":"α-WaLTR: Adaptive Wheel-and-Leg Transformable Robot (Texas A&M ART Lab)","url":"https://art.engr.tamu.edu/publication/%CE%B1-waltr-adaptive-wheel-and-leg-transformable-robot-for-versatile-multiterrain-locomotion/","kind":"engineering"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"walking-linkage","name":"Walking linkage (Jansen or Klann leg)","aliases":["Jansen linkage","Strandbeest leg","Klann linkage","crank walker","mechanical leg linkage"],"category":"robot","tags":["walking","linkage","non-ftc"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"linkage","type":"custom-spin","short":"A set of bars driven by one spinning crank that makes a foot trace a walking step, with no leg motors.","how":"A motor turns a crank, and pivoted links convert that rotation into a foot path that is nearly flat on the ground for part of the cycle and lifts for the rest. Jansen's version uses more links per leg (an eight-bar class), while Klann's uses six. Several legs out of phase on a shared crank keep the body moving forward. All the motion is set by the crank angle, so it is 1 DOF.","uses":["Walking robots and kinetic sculptures","Teaching linkages"],"pros":["Walks with one motor","No leg control code needed"],"cons":["Can't steer without a second drive side","Slow; many pivots to wear"],"failures":["Pivot slop changes the foot path","Links bind if lengths are wrong"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"crank rotation","output":"foot walking path"},"params":[{"key":"crankRadius","label":"Crank radius","unit":"mm","min":10,"max":60,"default":15,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["non-ftc"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"A design from outside FTC, shown for engineering reference; it is not marked FTC-legal."},"refs":[{"title":"Jansen's linkage (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jansen%27s_linkage","kind":"engineering"},{"title":"Klann linkage (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Klann_linkage","kind":"engineering"}],"image":{"kind":"schematic","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jansen%27s_linkage","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Jansen%27s_Linkage.svg/330px-Jansen%27s_Linkage.svg.png","credit":"Wikimedia Commons, via the Wikipedia article Jansen's linkage","shows":"diagram of Jansen's linkage proportions"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1},
{"id":"quadruped-walker","name":"Quadruped walking robot","aliases":["quadruped","four-legged robot","robot dog","legged robot"],"category":"robot","tags":["walking","non-ftc"],"entry":"architecture","family":"legged","type":"hip","short":"A four-legged robot that walks by moving its legs in a gait, like a robot dog.","how":"Each leg has two or three actuated joints (hip and knee, often a hip abduction joint too). Slow gaits lift one leg at a time while the other three form a stable tripod. Fast gaits are dynamically balanced and need a feedback controller. The count used here assumes 3 joints per leg (12 DOF), but 8-DOF versions exist.","uses":["Research and inspection robots on rough ground"],"pros":["Crosses terrain wheels can't","More stable than two legs"],"cons":["Many actuators and complex control","Slower and less efficient than wheels on flat floors"],"failures":["Servo overheating","Falls when the balance controller can't keep up"],"dof":12,"actuation":"varies","params":[{"key":"jointsPerLeg","label":"Joints per leg","unit":"count","min":2,"max":3,"default":3,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["non-ftc"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R503"],"note":"Outside FTC; not marked FTC-legal. Twelve joints would use most of the 8-motor plus 8-servo budget in R503."},"refs":[{"title":"Legged robot (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Legged_robot","kind":"engineering"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"hexapod","name":"Hexapod walking robot","aliases":["hexapod","six-legged robot","robot spider","tripod-gait walker"],"category":"robot","tags":["walking","non-ftc"],"entry":"architecture","family":"legged","type":"hip","short":"A six-legged robot that can always keep three feet on the ground, which makes it statically stable while walking.","how":"Each leg typically has two or three joints. In the common alternating tripod gait, three legs (front and back on one side, middle on the other) support the body while the other three swing forward, then they swap. With three feet down the body is always supported, so balance control is simple. The count used here assumes 3 joints per leg (18 DOF), though legs can have 2 to 6.","uses":["Rough-terrain research robots","Learning gait programming"],"pros":["Statically stable","Keeps walking if a leg fails"],"cons":["Many actuators","Slow and heavy for the speed"],"failures":["Joint servos stall or overheat","Leg collisions in poorly timed gaits"],"dof":18,"actuation":"varies","params":[{"key":"jointsPerLeg","label":"Joints per leg","unit":"count","min":2,"max":6,"default":3,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["non-ftc"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R503"],"note":"Outside FTC; not marked FTC-legal, and the actuator count would exceed the R503 limits."},"refs":[{"title":"Hexapod (robotics) (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hexapod_(robotics)","kind":"engineering"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hexapod_(robotics)","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Beetle_robot.jpg/330px-Beetle_robot.jpg","credit":"Wikimedia Commons, via the Wikipedia article Hexapod (robotics) (caption: Beetle hexapod)"},"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"snake-robot","name":"Snake robot (snakebot or snake-arm)","aliases":["snakebot","snake-arm robot","serpentine robot","continuum robot","elephant trunk robot","hyper-redundant robot"],"category":"robot","tags":["non-ftc","research","arm"],"entry":"architecture","family":"serial-chain","type":"custom-pivot","short":"A long chain of many small joints that bends like a snake to slither along the ground or reach around obstacles.","how":"Many short segments are joined in series, each with one or two bending joints, so the whole body can take curved shapes. Snakebots move along the ground by passing waves down the body. Snake-arm robots stay fixed at the base and are usually tendon (cable) driven from motors at the base, threading the arm through confined spaces. The DOF count used here assumes an example with 8 single-axis joints.","uses":["Inspection in pipes and confined spaces","Search and rescue research","Surgical tools"],"pros":["Reaches around obstacles","Fits through small openings"],"cons":["Very many actuators or tendons","Complex control and low payload"],"failures":["Tendon stretch or breakage","Joint backlash adds up along the chain"],"dof":8,"actuation":"varies","params":[{"key":"segments","label":"Segments","unit":"count","min":4,"max":20,"default":8,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["non-ftc"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"A design from outside FTC, shown for engineering reference; it is not marked FTC-legal."},"refs":[{"title":"Snake-arm robot (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snake-arm_robot","kind":"engineering"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"self-balancing-two-wheel","name":"Self-balancing two-wheel robot","aliases":["balancing robot","inverted pendulum robot","Segway-style robot","two-wheel balancer","balance bot"],"category":"robot","tags":["drive","control","non-ftc"],"entry":"architecture","family":"inverted-pendulum","type":"drive-wheel","short":"A robot on two side-by-side wheels that stays upright by constantly driving its wheels under its own tipping body.","how":"The body is an inverted pendulum and falls over on its own. An IMU measures the tilt angle and its rate, and a feedback controller drives both wheels in the direction of the fall to move the base back under the center of mass. Driving forward means leaning forward first. It can turn in place by running the wheels in opposite directions.","uses":["Learning feedback control","Personal transporters"],"pros":["Small footprint, turns in place","Great control-theory demo"],"cons":["Falls over without power or if control fails","Can't stand still without balancing"],"failures":["Controller gains badly tuned, so it oscillates and falls","IMU drift"],"dof":2,"actuation":"motor+motor","motion":{"input":"two wheel motors with IMU feedback","output":"balanced translation and turning"},"params":[{"key":"wheelDiameter","label":"Wheel diameter","unit":"mm","min":60,"max":200,"default":100,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["non-ftc"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"A design from outside FTC, shown for engineering reference; it is not marked FTC-legal."},"refs":[{"title":"Inverted pendulum (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inverted_pendulum","kind":"engineering"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inverted_pendulum","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Balancer_with_wine_3.JPG/330px-Balancer_with_wine_3.JPG","credit":"Wikimedia Commons, via the Wikipedia article Inverted pendulum","shows":"a balancing cart (c. 1976) that keeps a rod upright, the same control principle; not a two-wheel robot"},"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":2,"sensors":["IMU","wheel encoders"]},
{"id":"rotating-platform","name":"Rotating platform (turntable base)","aliases":["rotating base","waist joint","slewing ring","base yaw"],"category":"robot","tags":["turret","architecture"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"turret","type":"base-yaw","preset":{"states":{"front":0,"left":90,"right":-90},"lower":-180,"upper":180,"speed":60},"short":"A flat platform on a large bearing that spins everything mounted on it around a vertical axis.","how":"The platform is supported by a lazy susan, a bearing stack around its rim, or a center thrust or x-contact bearing that carries axial and tipping loads. A motor drives a ring gear, sprocket or pulley fixed to the platform through a reduction. An encoder or absolute sensor reports the angle, and cabling needs a service loop or slip ring.","uses":["Robot arm waists","Turrets","Camera or sensor rotators"],"pros":["Reorients a whole mechanism without moving the base"],"cons":["Tipping loads need a stiff bearing","Cable management"],"failures":["Cheap turntables rack under side load","Drive slips and loses angle"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation through a reduction","output":"platform yaw"},"params":[{"key":"diameter","label":"Platform diameter","unit":"mm","min":80,"max":400,"default":150,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","non-ftc"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R303"],"note":"A single-DoF COTS part under R303 (not one of its listed exceptions); legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Full Turrets (Game Manual 0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/turrets/full-turret.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"illustration","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/turrets/full-turret.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/18219-bearing-stack.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), FTC 18219 Primitive Data","shows":"one bearing stack (a radial bearing between two thrust bearings on a shoulder screw) that supports a rotating plate; not the whole platform"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"parent":"turret"},
{"id":"pan-tilt-platform","name":"Pan-and-tilt platform","aliases":["pan tilt","pan/tilt kit","gimbal","camera pan tilt","SPT200","sensor turret"],"category":"robot","tags":["sense","camera","aiming"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"pan-tilt","type":"camera-mount","preset":{"states":{"level":0,"down":30},"lower":-60,"upper":60,"speed":180},"short":"Two servos stacked at right angles that turn a camera or sensor left-right (pan) and up-down (tilt).","how":"One servo rotates a base plate about the vertical axis (pan). A second servo, mounted on that plate, tilts a top plate about a horizontal axis. Bearings on each axis take the load off the servo shafts. Code points the camera by setting each servo angle, for example to keep an AprilTag centered.","uses":["Aiming a camera to track tags or pieces","Sensor scanning"],"pros":["Simple two-servo control","Widens the camera's view"],"cons":["Servo jitter blurs images","Moving the camera complicates pose math"],"failures":["Servo backlash causes aim error","Cable strain at the pan joint"],"dof":2,"actuation":"servo+servo","motion":{"input":"two servo rotations","output":"pan and tilt of the payload"},"params":[{"key":"panRange","label":"Pan range","unit":"deg","min":90,"max":300,"default":180,"estimate":true},{"key":"tiltRange","label":"Tilt range","unit":"deg","min":45,"max":180,"default":90,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","non-ftc"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R303","R708"],"note":"R303 says COTS mechanisms may not exceed one DOF, so a COTS two-axis kit may be a problem in FTC while a team-built one may not; cameras must follow R708. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"SPT200 Pan & Tilt Kit (ServoCity)","url":"https://www.servocity.com/spt200-pan-tilt-kit/","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML)","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://www.servocity.com/spt200-pan-tilt-kit/","src":"https://cdn11.bigcommerce.com/s-f6vfspkkjf/products/630/images/2265/SPT200__60276__08223.1701973283.386.513.jpg?c=1","credit":"ServoCity store listing"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":2},
{"id":"differential-joint","name":"Differential joint (two motors, two axes)","aliases":["diff joint","bevel differential joint","differential mechanism"],"category":"robot","tags":["arm","wrist","drive"],"entry":"concept","family":"differential","type":null,"preset":{"states":{"down":0,"up":90},"lower":-90,"upper":90,"speed":180},"short":"Two actuators drive a pair of bevel gears together, so running them the same way bends the joint and running them opposite ways rotates it.","how":"Two input gears, each driven by its own motor or servo, mesh with an output bevel gear on a cross shaft. When both inputs turn the same way, the whole assembly pitches. When they turn opposite ways, the output gear spins (roll). Control is done in average and difference axes, and both actuators share the load on each axis. The same idea is used in differential swerve, where two motors both steer and drive a wheel.","uses":["Compact two-axis wrists","Differential swerve modules"],"pros":["Both actuators help on each axis","Puts the actuators off the moving joint"],"cons":["Coupled control math","Bevel gear backlash"],"failures":["Gear slip makes one axis drift","One actuator is weaker, so the joint twists under load"],"dof":2,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"two rotations","output":"pitch (average) and roll (difference)"},"params":[{"key":"diffRatio","label":"Difference-axis ratio","unit":":1","min":1,"max":4,"default":2,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","non-ftc"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R303","R503"],"note":"A COTS two-DOF assembly would conflict with R303; team-built ones depend on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Differential Mechanism Setup (CTR Electronics Phoenix 6 docs)","url":"https://pro.docs.ctr-electronics.com/en/latest/docs/api-reference/mechanisms/differential/differential-setup.html","kind":"engineering"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":2,"parent":"mechanical-differential"},
{"id":"cable-driven-joint","name":"Cable-driven (tendon) joint","aliases":["tendon-driven joint","cable joint","Bowden cable joint","pull-cable actuation","remote-actuated joint"],"category":"robot","tags":["arm","non-ftc"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"tendon","type":"custom-pivot","preset":{"states":{"up":0,"down":90},"lower":0,"upper":120,"speed":90},"short":"A joint pulled by cables from a motor or servo mounted elsewhere, keeping heavy actuators off the moving arm.","how":"A cable (or a pair of opposing cables) wraps around a pulley at the joint and runs back to an actuator at the base. Pulling one cable rotates the joint one way. Antagonistic pairs or a return spring bring it back. Snake-arm and robotic-hand designs use tendons this way to keep the arm slim and light.","uses":["Robotic fingers and hands","Slim, light arms"],"pros":["Light moving parts","Actuators can sit at the base"],"cons":["Cable stretch and friction","Needs tensioning"],"failures":["Cable slips on the pulley or breaks","Lost tension gives slop"],"dof":1,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"cable pull from a remote actuator","output":"joint rotation"},"params":[{"key":"pulleyRadius","label":"Joint pulley radius","unit":"mm","min":5,"max":40,"default":15,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["non-ftc"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Shown for engineering reference; not marked FTC-legal."},"refs":[{"title":"Snake-arm robot (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snake-arm_robot","kind":"engineering"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"planetary-gearbox","name":"Planetary gearbox","aliases":["planetary","epicyclic gearbox","UltraPlanetary","Yellow Jacket gearbox","planetary reduction","sun-planet-ring"],"category":"robot","tags":["power transmission","component"],"entry":"component","family":"gearbox","type":null,"short":"A compact gearbox where small planet gears orbit a center sun gear inside a ring gear, giving big reductions in a short cylinder.","how":"The input drives the central sun gear. Planet gears mesh with it and with an outer ring gear that is held still, and the carrier holding the planets is the output. With the ring fixed, each stage's ratio is 1 + ring teeth / sun teeth, and stages stack to multiply. REV's UltraPlanetary stacks 3:1, 4:1 and 5:1 cartridges (actual ratios differ slightly from the nominal ones).","uses":["Most FTC drive and mechanism motors","Arms, lifts and drivetrains"],"pros":["High reduction in a small, coaxial package","Load shared across several planets"],"cons":["Some backlash","Shock loads can crack plastic ring gears"],"failures":["Stripped stage from impact","Wrong actual ratio used in code (nominal vs actual)"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor shaft rotation","output":"slower, higher-torque coaxial rotation"},"params":[{"key":"ratio","label":"Total ratio","unit":":1","min":3,"max":125,"default":20,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R303"],"note":"A single-DoF COTS part under R303; legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"UltraPlanetary Features (REV docs)","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/rev-crossover-products/ultraplanetary/features","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"Epicyclic gearing (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epicyclic_gearing","kind":"engineering"}],"image":{"kind":"illustration","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epicyclic_gearing","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9a/Epicyclic_Gearing_Stationary_Ring.gif","credit":"Wikimedia Commons, via the Wikipedia article Epicyclic gearing","shows":"animated planetary gear train with sun, planets, carrier and fixed ring"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1},
{"id":"cycloidal-gearbox","name":"Cycloidal gearbox","aliases":["cycloidal drive","cycloid reducer","cyclo drive"],"category":"robot","tags":["power transmission","component","non-ftc"],"entry":"component","family":"gearbox","type":null,"short":"A reducer where a lobed disc wobbles around a ring of pins, giving a large, low-backlash reduction in one stage.","how":"The input shaft spins an eccentric bearing, which makes a lobed (cycloidal) disc wobble inside a ring of pins. Because the disc has fewer lobes than there are pins, it slowly turns backward. Output pins in oversized holes take that slow rotation out. The reduction is lobes : (pins - lobes) (e.g. 10:1 with 11 pins and 10 lobes), and the output turns opposite to the input.","uses":["Robot arm joints","Compact high-ratio actuators (often 3D-printed by hobbyists)"],"pros":["High ratio and torque in one stage","Very low backlash possible","Many lobes share load"],"cons":["Vibration from the unbalanced disc unless two or more discs are used","Precise parts needed"],"failures":["Wear on lobes and pins","Vibration loosens fasteners"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"fast input rotation","output":"slow reversed output rotation"},"params":[{"key":"ratio","label":"Ratio","unit":":1","min":5,"max":100,"default":20,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["non-ftc"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R303"],"note":"Shown for engineering reference and not marked FTC-legal; a team-built one would have to meet the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Cycloidal drive (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cycloidal_drive","kind":"engineering"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"high","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"strain-wave-reducer","name":"Strain-wave (harmonic drive) reducer","aliases":["harmonic drive","strain wave gear","flexspline reducer","harmonic gearbox"],"category":"robot","tags":["power transmission","component","non-ftc"],"entry":"component","family":"gearbox","type":null,"short":"A precise gear reducer where a flexible toothed cup is squeezed into an oval and walks slowly around a rigid ring.","how":"An oval wave generator spins inside a thin flexible spline, bending it so its teeth mesh with a rigid circular spline at two points. The flex spline has slightly fewer teeth, so each input turn shifts it back by the tooth difference, giving a very large ratio (for example, 200 vs 202 teeth gives 100:1). Input and output are coaxial.","uses":["Industrial and humanoid robot joints","Precision positioning"],"pros":["Zero backlash","Very high ratio, compact and light"],"cons":["Expensive","Acts like a torsional spring at low torque (wind-up)"],"failures":["Flex spline fatigue cracks","Ratcheting (teeth skipping) under overload"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"fast input rotation","output":"slow coaxial output rotation"},"params":[{"key":"ratio","label":"Ratio","unit":":1","min":30,"max":320,"default":100,"estimate":false,"note":"range given by the Wikipedia article"}],"seasons":["non-ftc"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Shown for engineering reference; not marked FTC-legal."},"refs":[{"title":"Strain wave gearing (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strain_wave_gearing","kind":"engineering"}],"image":{"kind":"illustration","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strain_wave_gearing","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/HarmonicDriveAni.gif/250px-HarmonicDriveAni.gif","credit":"Wikimedia Commons, via the Wikipedia article Strain wave gearing","shows":"animation of the circular spline, flex spline and wave generator"},"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"high","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"worm-gearbox","name":"Worm gearbox","aliases":["worm drive","worm gear","worm and wheel","self-locking gearbox","Super-Duty Worm Drive"],"category":"robot","tags":["power transmission","component","endgame","arm"],"entry":"component","family":"gearbox","type":null,"short":"A screw-like worm turns a gear wheel at 90 degrees, giving a big reduction that often holds its position when power is off.","how":"Each turn of a single-start worm advances the wheel by one tooth, so the ratio equals the wheel's tooth count. Sliding friction between worm and wheel often stops the wheel from turning the worm back (self-locking), depending on lead angle and friction. That holds arms or hangs in place without power, at the cost of lower efficiency. goBILDA sells a 28:1 worm gearbox for its Yellow Jacket motors.","uses":["Arms that must hold position","Endgame hang or lift that must not drop (past games; BIOBUZZ has no hang, G415)"],"pros":["Big reduction in one stage","Often holds load with power off","Right-angle output"],"cons":["Low efficiency and heat","Slow"],"failures":["Wheel teeth wear from sliding friction","Shock loads crack the wheel because it can't backdrive"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"worm rotation","output":"slow wheel rotation at 90 degrees"},"params":[{"key":"ratio","label":"Ratio","unit":":1","min":5,"max":100,"default":28,"estimate":false,"note":"28:1 is goBILDA's listed ratio"}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R303","R501"],"note":"Single-speed gearboxes are single-DOF parts under R303. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Super-Duty Worm Drive Gearbox (28:1 Ratio) (goBILDA)","url":"https://www.gobilda.com/super-duty-worm-drive-gearbox-28-1-ratio/","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"Worm drive (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Worm_drive","kind":"engineering"}],"image":{"kind":"component-photo","page":"https://www.gobilda.com/super-duty-worm-drive-gearbox-28-1-ratio/","src":"https://cdn11.bigcommerce.com/s-x56mtydx1w/images/stencil/2400x2400/products/1783/11401/Ang1__92516.1709581600.jpg?c=1","credit":"goBILDA store listing","shows":"goBILDA 28:1 Super-Duty Worm Drive Gearbox (motor not included)"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1},
{"id":"mechanical-differential","name":"Mechanical differential (gear differential)","aliases":["differential gear","open differential","bevel differential","spur differential","three-shaft differential"],"category":"robot","tags":["power transmission","component"],"entry":"component","family":"differential","type":null,"short":"A gear set with three shafts where one shaft's speed is always the average of the other two, like a car's axle differential.","how":"Input drives a carrier holding spider (planet) gears that mesh with two side gears. When both sides meet equal resistance, the spider gears don't spin and both outputs turn together. When one side slows, the spider gears rotate and the other side speeds up by the same amount. It is the basis of differential wrists, differential swerve and rover suspension averaging.","uses":["Letting two wheels turn at different speeds","Combining two motors into two motions"],"pros":["Splits or combines motion neatly"],"cons":["Power goes to the side with less grip (open differential)","Bevel gears need rigid mounting"],"failures":["Gear mesh loosens, so it slips","Wheel spin on one side stops motion"],"dof":2,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"one or two shaft rotations","output":"shaft speeds related by averaging"},"params":[{"key":"spiderGears","label":"Spider gears","unit":"count","min":2,"max":4,"default":2,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","non-ftc"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R303"],"note":"A COTS differential has more than one DOF and may conflict with R303; legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Differential (mechanical device) (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Differential_(mechanical_device)","kind":"engineering"}],"image":{"kind":"component-photo","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Differential_(mechanical_device)","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ad/BAUMA_2004_ZF_Differentialgetriebe.jpg","credit":"Wikimedia Commons, via the Wikipedia article Differential (mechanical device)","shows":"a car differential unit built by ZF c. 2004, not a robot part"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"foldable-linkage","name":"Foldable (deploying) linkage","aliases":["fold-out mechanism","deploying linkage","stowable linkage","flip-out","start-folded mechanism","over-center fold"],"category":"robot","tags":["arm","starting configuration","packaging"],"entry":"concept","family":"linkage","type":"flip-out-arm","preset":{"states":{"stowed":0,"out":90},"lower":0,"upper":120,"speed":180},"short":"A mechanism that starts folded inside the robot's starting size and unfolds after the match starts.","how":"Links or a whole mechanism pivot into a compact stowed pose, often rotated up, so the robot fits its starting box. After the start, a servo, motor or spring unfolds it to its working position. An over-center linkage can lock it out once deployed. gm0 notes flip-up transfers can start rotated up to fit the sizing cube.","uses":["Intakes, arms and wings that would not fit the starting size"],"pros":["More reach than the starting size allows"],"cons":["Deploy must be reliable every match","Takes time at the start"],"failures":["Mechanism fails to deploy","Unlocks under impact and folds back"],"dof":1,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"servo, motor or spring","output":"fold to unfold rotation"},"params":[{"key":"foldAngle","label":"Fold angle","unit":"deg","min":45,"max":180,"default":90,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R102","R105"],"note":"The robot must start within the 18 in. cube (R102) and stay within the R105.A expansion volume after deploying. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Transfers (Game Manual 0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Linkages (Game Manual 0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linkages.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"stewart-platform","name":"Stewart platform (6-DOF parallel platform)","aliases":["Stewart platform","hexapod platform","6-DOF motion platform","Gough-Stewart platform","motion base"],"category":"robot","tags":["parallel robot","non-ftc"],"entry":"architecture","family":"parallel-platform","type":"stewart-leg","preset":{"states":{"short":0,"long":2},"lower":0,"upper":3,"speed":2},"short":"A top plate held up by six extendable legs, which can move it in all six directions: three slides and three tilts.","how":"Six linear actuators (or rotary servos with linkages) connect a base to a top plate through universal or ball joints. Changing the leg lengths together positions and tilts the plate in x, y, z, roll, pitch and yaw. The inverse kinematics (leg lengths from a desired pose) has a closed-form solution.","uses":["Flight and driving simulators","Precision positioning and telescopes"],"pros":["Stiff and precise","Six DOF with all actuators at the base"],"cons":["Small workspace","Six actuators and complex math"],"failures":["Joint wear gives slop","A leg at full stroke limits the workspace"],"dof":6,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"six leg length changes","output":"6-DOF top plate pose"},"params":[{"key":"stroke","label":"Leg stroke","unit":"in","min":1,"max":12,"default":3,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["non-ftc"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R801"],"note":"Outside FTC; not marked FTC-legal. Pneumatic legs would also conflict with R801."},"refs":[{"title":"Stewart platform (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stewart_platform","kind":"engineering"}],"image":{"kind":"illustration","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stewart_platform","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a7/Hexapod_general_Anim.gif","credit":"Wikimedia Commons, via the Wikipedia article Stewart platform","shows":"animation of a Stewart platform"},"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"high","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"delta-robot","name":"Delta robot (parallel positioning arm)","aliases":["delta robot","delta arm","parallel robot","pick-and-place delta","delta 3D printer"],"category":"robot","tags":["parallel robot","non-ftc"],"entry":"architecture","family":"parallel-platform","type":"delta-arm","preset":{"states":{"up":0,"down":45},"lower":-20,"upper":80,"speed":180},"short":"Three motor-driven arms meet at a small platform; parallelograms keep the platform level while it moves anywhere in 3D.","how":"Three rotary actuators on the fixed base each swing an upper arm. Each upper arm connects to the moving platform through a parallelogram of rods with ball or universal joints. The parallelograms keep the platform's orientation fixed, so the three arm angles set its x, y, z position. A fourth axis can add rotation.","uses":["High-speed pick-and-place","Delta 3D printers"],"pros":["Very fast; light moving parts","Motors stay on the base"],"cons":["Small, dome-shaped workspace","Low payload"],"failures":["Ball-joint pop-out","Arm calibration errors"],"dof":3,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"three base actuator rotations","output":"x, y, z translation of the platform"},"params":[{"key":"upperArm","label":"Upper arm length","unit":"mm","min":50,"max":300,"default":120,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["non-ftc"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Outside FTC; not marked FTC-legal."},"refs":[{"title":"Delta robot (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delta_robot","kind":"engineering"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delta_robot","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Sketchy%2C_portrait-drawing_delta_robot.jpg/330px-Sketchy%2C_portrait-drawing_delta_robot.jpg","credit":"Wikimedia Commons, via the Wikipedia article Delta robot (Sketchy, a portrait-drawing delta robot)"},"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"high","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":3},
{"id":"gantry-stage","name":"Belt-driven positioning stage (gantry)","aliases":["gantry","linear stage","Cartesian robot","XY table","belt stage","CNC axis"],"category":"robot","tags":["linear motion","non-ftc"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"linear-stage","type":"gantry","preset":{"states":{"home":0,"end":12},"lower":0,"upper":12,"speed":6},"short":"A carriage pulled along straight rails by a belt or screw; stack two or three at right angles for an XY or XYZ gantry.","how":"A carriage rides on linear bearings or rails, and a motor (often a stepper in non-FTC machines) pulls it with a timing belt looped over pulleys, or drives a lead screw. Each stage is one straight axis. Stacking stages at right angles gives a Cartesian robot whose position is simply each axis's travel, which makes control easy.","uses":["3D printers, CNC and pick-and-place","Precise horizontal positioning"],"pros":["Simple, predictable kinematics","Precise and repeatable"],"cons":["Big frame for its working area","Belts stretch; cantilevered axes sag"],"failures":["Belt skips teeth, so position is lost","Rails bind if misaligned"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation through belt or screw","output":"carriage translation"},"params":[{"key":"travel","label":"Axis travel","unit":"in","min":2,"max":24,"default":12,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["non-ftc","general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R501"],"note":"Steppers are not on the FTC motor list (R501). FTC builds would use allowed motors or servos; legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Cartesian coordinate robot (Wikipedia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cartesian_coordinate_robot","kind":"engineering"},{"title":"Timing Belt (Game Manual 0)","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/power-transmission/belt.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1},
{"id":"compact-rotary-actuator","name":"Compact rotary actuator (servo gearbox)","aliases":["servo gearbox","Stingray gearbox","geared servo","rotary actuator","servo with external reduction"],"category":"robot","tags":["component","arm","actuator"],"entry":"component","family":"actuator","type":"custom-pivot","preset":{"states":{"up":0,"down":90},"lower":0,"upper":180,"speed":60},"short":"A servo packaged with an external gear reduction and a supported output shaft, giving much more torque in a small block.","how":"A standard servo drives a pinion that turns a larger output gear on a separate bearing-supported shaft. The reduction multiplies torque and divides speed and range, and the bearings keep side loads off the servo's own spline. ServoCity's Stingray-4 pairs an unmodified goBILDA 5-turn servo with a 4:1 reduction, and the listing gives 450 degrees of travel and 1400 oz-in.","uses":["Heavy wrists and small arms","Joints where a motor won't fit"],"pros":["High torque from a servo port","Shaft supported by bearings"],"cons":["Slow","Backlash from the added gears"],"failures":["Gear teeth strip from shock loads","Servo overheats when stalled"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation","output":"slower, higher-torque output shaft rotation"},"params":[{"key":"ratio","label":"External reduction","unit":":1","min":2,"max":9,"default":4,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R502","R303"],"note":"The vendor says the servo is unmodified for FTC legality; the servo must meet R502. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"ServoCity Stingray-4 Servo Gearbox (RobotShop listing)","url":"https://www.robotshop.com/products/servocity-stingray-4-servo-gearbox-067-sec-60-15-rpm-1400oz-in-torque-450-rotation-continuous-mode","kind":"vendor"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":1},
{"id":"roller-intake","name":"Roller intake (active sweeper)","aliases":["active intake","sweeper","sweeper roller","horizontal intake","single horizontal sweeper roller","dual horizontal rollers","top-and-bottom roller","over-under rollers","solid wheel intake","foam wheel intake","spinning intake"],"category":"intake","tags":["collection","active-intake","floor-pickup"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"roller-intake","type":"roller-intake","short":"A powered spinning roller grabs game pieces off the floor and pulls them into the robot. You don't have to line it up exactly, which makes it the usual way to collect many pieces quickly.","how":"A motor (sometimes a continuous-rotation servo) spins one or more shafts that carry wheels, tubing or flaps. Where the roller touches a piece, friction drags it into the robot. Those are active collection rollers, which keep pulling pieces in. Two other ideas are different: passive retention (a shape or spring holds a piece) and gripping (a claw closes on one object). A horizontal intake turns on a horizontal axis and can take several pieces at once. A second roller can be added above or below the first to pinch pieces and lift them.","uses":["collecting small or many game pieces from the floor","feeding a transfer, hopper or shooter","Res-Q and Rover Ruckus small-element collection"],"pros":["fast cycles: pieces don't need to be lined up exactly","can take several pieces at once","simple to build with one motor"],"cons":["less controllable: it can pick up extra pieces","takes a motor port","needs funnelling and a speed that suits the piece"],"failures":["pieces jam at an angle the driver cannot clear","roller material wears or tears at high RPM","the intake is damaged when it sticks out past the frame and gets hit","it spins too fast and throws pieces back out"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"roller spin that pulls pieces inward"},"params":[{"key":"rollerDiameter","label":"Roller diameter","unit":"mm","min":20,"max":120,"default":48,"estimate":true,"note":"depends on the game piece"},{"key":"rollerSpeed","label":"Roller speed","unit":"rpm","min":100,"max":1200,"default":600,"estimate":true,"note":"gm0 cites 800-1000+ rpm for soft surgical tubing and 100-250 rpm for stiffer tubing"},{"key":"rollerCount","label":"Number of rollers","unit":"count","min":1,"max":4,"default":1,"estimate":true,"note":"2 for top-and-bottom pinch designs"},{"key":"intakeWidth","label":"Intake width","unit":"in","min":4,"max":18,"default":12,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz","historical:res-q","historical:velocity-vortex","historical:rover-ruckus"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["G407","R105","R503"],"note":"Checked against the BIOBUZZ manual (TU08). G407 limits a robot to controlling 4 SCORING ELEMENTS, R105 limits expansion and R503 caps motors and servos. Legality still depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Intakes - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/types-of-intakes.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Principles of an Intake - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/principles-of-an-intake.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML)","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/types-of-intakes.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/8393-intake.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo of FTC 8393 BrainSTEM, Velocity Vortex"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":0},
{"id":"compliant-wheel-intake","name":"Compliant-wheel intake","aliases":["compliant wheel intake","GripForce intake","squishy wheel intake","AndyMark compliant wheels","star-wheel intake","Gecko wheel intake"],"category":"intake","tags":["collection","active-intake"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"roller-intake","parent":"roller-intake","type":"roller-intake","short":"Soft rubber wheels squash against a game piece and roll it into the robot. They forgive bad angles better than hard wheels.","how":"A motor spins shafts that carry soft spoked rubber wheels. The wheels deform around the piece, so they touch more of it and grip harder. Lower-durometer (softer) wheels grip better but wear faster. The wheel shafts can be fixed in place or spring-loaded.","uses":["large single game pieces such as Relic Recovery glyphs","the BIOBUZZ goBILDA StarterBot's GripForce Gecko wheel intake for POLLEN","feeding pieces into a conveyor or transfer"],"pros":["very controllable","adapts to the piece's orientation and the intake angle","sold ready-made by goBILDA (Gecko) and AndyMark"],"cons":["usually takes one piece at a time","works best with one piece shape and struggles with balls","can jam on a piece in a bad position"],"failures":["soft wheels tear or wear smooth","wheel slips on its shaft","piece wedges between wheels at an angle"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"wheel spin that draws the piece in"},"params":[{"key":"wheelDiameter","label":"Wheel diameter","unit":"mm","min":32,"max":102,"default":48,"estimate":true,"note":"goBILDA lists 32, 48, 72 and 96 mm Gecko wheels"},{"key":"durometer","label":"Wheel durometer","unit":"Shore A","min":15,"max":60,"default":30,"estimate":true,"note":"goBILDA sells 30A Gecko and 15A GripForce wheels"},{"key":"compression","label":"Squeeze on the piece","unit":"mm","min":0,"max":20,"default":6,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz","historical:relic-recovery"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["G407","R105","R503"],"note":"Checked against the BIOBUZZ G407 control limit, the R105 expansion limit and the R503 motor and servo count. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Intakes - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/types-of-intakes.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"GripForce Gecko Wheel (8mm REX Bore, 48mm Diameter, 15A Durometer)","url":"https://www.servocity.com/gripforce-gecko-wheel-8mm-rex-bore-48mm-diameter-15a-durometer/","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"FTC StarterBot Resource Guide (2026-2027)","url":"https://www.gobilda.com/ftc-starter-bot-resource-guide-2026-2027-season/","kind":"vendor"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/types-of-intakes.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/2856-intake.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo of FTC 2856 Tesseract, Relic Recovery"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":0},
{"id":"vertical-roller-intake","name":"Vertical-axis (side roller) intake","aliases":["vertical intake","opposed side rollers","side rollers","opposed wheel intake","independent dual-side roller","two-sided wheel intake"],"category":"intake","tags":["collection","active-intake"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"roller-intake","parent":"roller-intake","type":"roller-intake","short":"Two sets of wheels on upright shafts squeeze a piece from both sides and pull it in, one piece at a time.","how":"Wheels sit on shafts that turn on the vertical (z) axis, one set on each side of the opening, and spin in opposite directions so both sides pull inward. If each side has its own motor, the sides can be run at different speeds to straighten or spin a piece. That version is the independent dual-side roller. This intake suits large pieces, and the side spacing sets how hard it squeezes.","uses":["large blocks or cubes (Relic Recovery glyphs)","straightening a piece before a transfer"],"pros":["controls one large piece well","running the sides independently can turn a crooked piece"],"cons":["one piece at a time","needs the robot roughly lined up","two motors if the sides are driven separately"],"failures":["piece pinches at an angle and stalls","sides drift apart and stop gripping"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"opposed wheel spin that pulls the piece in"},"params":[{"key":"gap","label":"Gap between sides","unit":"in","min":2,"max":10,"default":5,"estimate":true,"note":"slightly smaller than the piece"},{"key":"wheelDiameter","label":"Wheel diameter","unit":"mm","min":32,"max":102,"default":72,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:relic-recovery"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["G407","R105","R503"],"note":"Checked against the BIOBUZZ G407, R105 and R503 limits. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Intakes - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/types-of-intakes.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/types-of-intakes.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/8680-intake.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo of FTC 8680 Kraken-Pinion, Relic Recovery"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":0},
{"id":"surgical-tubing-intake","name":"Surgical-tubing intake","aliases":["tubing intake","flap tubing roller","zip tie intake","surgical tubing intake"],"category":"intake","tags":["collection","active-intake","multi-piece"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"roller-intake","parent":"roller-intake","type":"roller-intake","short":"Loops of rubber surgical tubing spin fast and flick small or oddly shaped pieces into the robot, often several at once.","how":"Lengths of hollow latex or rubber tubing are fixed to a shaft, and a motor spins the shaft. The tubing slaps the pieces and drags them inward. gm0 notes that polyurethane tubing inserted inside stiffens it. Soft tubing runs at about 800-1000+ rpm and stiffer tubing at about 100-250 rpm. Zip ties can stand in for tubing but have less friction. The intake is almost always fixed at one height and angle.","uses":["small balls and minerals (Velocity Vortex, Rover Ruckus)","collecting several pieces in one pass"],"pros":["collects several pieces at once","handles small and oddly shaped pieces","cheap and easy to rebuild"],"cons":["needs a high RPM","hard to control: it may take extra pieces","poor with large pieces"],"failures":["tubing tears or slides off the shaft","takes in more pieces than the rules allow (watch BIOBUZZ G407)","pieces bounce out when the speed is too high"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"tubing sweep that pulls pieces in"},"params":[{"key":"rollerSpeed","label":"Roller speed","unit":"rpm","min":100,"max":1200,"default":900,"estimate":true,"note":"gm0: about 800-1000+ rpm for soft tubing, 100-250 rpm for stiff tubing"},{"key":"tubeLength","label":"Tubing length past the shaft","unit":"mm","min":20,"max":80,"default":40,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:res-q","historical:velocity-vortex","historical:rover-ruckus"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["G407","R105","R503"],"note":"Checked against the BIOBUZZ G407, R105 and R503 limits. A multi-piece intake makes over-control under G407 more likely. Legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Intakes - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/types-of-intakes.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"2018-19 Rover Ruckus 9410 (F.W. Parker Robotics)","url":"https://www.fwparkerrobotics.org/2018-19-rover-ruckus-9410.html","kind":"team"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/types-of-intakes.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/11115-intake1.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo of FTC 11115 Gluten Free, Rover Ruckus"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":0},
{"id":"rubber-band-roller-intake","name":"Rubber-band roller intake","aliases":["rubber band intake","rubber-band roller","band roller","pliable roller"],"category":"intake","tags":["collection","active-intake","balls"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"roller-intake","parent":"roller-intake","type":"roller-intake","short":"Rubber bands stretched between two hubs form a soft, springy roller that is good at pulling in balls.","how":"Sprockets, wheels or gears sit at both ends of a shaft, and rubber bands run between them to make a flexible roller surface. A motor spins the shaft, and the bands give around the ball while gripping it. gm0 says zip ties can be added to reach smaller balls, and the roller can optionally be servo-adjusted.","uses":["ball collection","common in VEX VRC (Turning Point), adaptable to FTC"],"pros":["very good with balls","cheap and forgiving"],"cons":["poor with cubes and other shapes","slower than surgical tubing","often needs several stages to carry pieces to the deposit"],"failures":["bands snap or lose tension","bands roll off the end hubs"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"band roller spin"},"params":[{"key":"rollerDiameter","label":"Roller diameter","unit":"mm","min":25,"max":100,"default":50,"estimate":true},{"key":"rollerSpeed","label":"Roller speed","unit":"rpm","min":100,"max":800,"default":400,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","non-ftc"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["G407","R105","R503"],"note":"Checked against the BIOBUZZ G407, R105 and R503 limits for an FTC version. The pictured example is a VEX robot, which is not FTC-legal as built."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Intakes - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/types-of-intakes.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/types-of-intakes.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/vrc-intake.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), ball intake for VRC Turning Point (a VEX robot, not FTC)"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":0},
{"id":"flexible-flap-intake","name":"Flexible-flap (TPU) roller intake","aliases":["flexible finger roller","TPU intake","NinjaFlex intake","3D printed intake flaps","finger roller","flap intake"],"category":"intake","tags":["collection","active-intake","3d-printed"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"roller-intake","parent":"roller-intake","type":"roller-intake","short":"3D-printed flexible TPU flaps on a slow-turning roller sweep pieces in. It is low-RPM enough to run on a servo.","how":"Flaps or fingers printed in flexible filament (TPU or NinjaFlex) stick out from a hub. As the hub turns, the flaps bend over each piece and push it inward. Because the flaps are stiffer than tubing they work at low RPM, so gm0 notes they pair well with continuous-rotation servos.","uses":["Rover Ruckus mineral intakes","servo-powered intakes that free a motor port"],"pros":["works at low RPM, so a servo can drive it","flap shape is easy to tune by reprinting"],"cons":["needs 3D-printing experience","printed flaps fatigue and crack"],"failures":["flap cracks at its root","layer separation in the print"],"dof":1,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"motor or CR servo rotation","output":"flap sweep"},"params":[{"key":"flapLength","label":"Flap length","unit":"mm","min":15,"max":60,"default":30,"estimate":true},{"key":"rollerSpeed","label":"Roller speed","unit":"rpm","min":50,"max":400,"default":150,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:rover-ruckus"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["G407","R105","R503"],"note":"Checked against the BIOBUZZ G407, R105 and R503 limits. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Intakes - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/types-of-intakes.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/types-of-intakes.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/8417-3dp-intake.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo of FTC 8417 Lectric Legends, Rover Ruckus, TPU intake flaps"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"spring-loaded-roller-intake","name":"Spring-loaded (floating) roller intake","aliases":["spring-loaded roller","springloaded intake","suspended roller intake","sprung wheel intake","floating roller"],"category":"intake","tags":["collection","active-intake","compliance"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"roller-intake","parent":"roller-intake","type":"roller-intake","short":"The roller sits on springy arms, so it can ride up over a piece and snap back down to grip it.","how":"The roller or wheel shafts are mounted on pivoting plates or arms pulled inward by springs or bands. A piece pushes the wheels apart or upward, and the spring presses them back against it, which gives steady grip across different piece sizes. gm0 shows Relic Recovery intakes with spring-loaded front wheels behind fixed rear wheels.","uses":["pieces that vary in size or orientation","absorbing impacts on an intake that sticks out past the frame"],"pros":["forgiving of piece size and angle","flexes on impact instead of breaking"],"cons":["more parts to tune (spring force)","grip changes as springs wear"],"failures":["spring too weak, so the piece slips","spring too strong, so the piece stalls","pivot binds"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"roller spin with spring-loaded float"},"params":[{"key":"floatTravel","label":"Float travel","unit":"mm","min":5,"max":50,"default":20,"estimate":true},{"key":"springForce","label":"Spring preload","unit":"N","min":2,"max":40,"default":10,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:relic-recovery"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["G407","R105","R503"],"note":"Checked against the BIOBUZZ G407, R105 and R503 limits. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Intakes - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/types-of-intakes.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Principles of an Intake - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/principles-of-an-intake.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/types-of-intakes.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/9971-intake.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo of FTC 9971 LanBros, Relic Recovery, spring-loaded"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1},
{"id":"funnel-guided-roller-intake","name":"Funnel-guided roller intake","aliases":["funnel-guided roller","angled roller","angled roller intake","V-intake","funnel intake with roller"],"category":"intake","tags":["collection","active-intake","funnel"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"roller-intake","parent":"roller-intake","type":"roller-intake","short":"A roller paired with angled walls or angled rollers that steer every piece to the same spot, so the robot doesn't need perfect aim.","how":"Angled side plates, or rollers set at an angle, narrow the wide opening down to one exit point. The main roller pulls pieces in, and the funnel geometry turns their sideways offset into a single consistent path into the transfer. gm0 stresses that good funnelling makes pieces follow a consistent path.","uses":["catching pieces across a wide front and delivering them to one hopper","feeding a single-file shooter or conveyor"],"pros":["wide capture area","consistent hand-off to the next mechanism"],"cons":["funnel walls take space","pieces can wedge at the narrow throat"],"failures":["two pieces jam side by side in the throat","funnel walls bend after impacts"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"roller spin plus funnelling"},"params":[{"key":"mouthWidth","label":"Mouth width","unit":"in","min":6,"max":18,"default":14,"estimate":true},{"key":"throatWidth","label":"Throat width","unit":"in","min":2,"max":8,"default":4,"estimate":true,"note":"slightly wider than one piece"}],"seasons":["general","historical:res-q"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["G407","R105","R503"],"note":"Checked against the BIOBUZZ G407, R105 and R503 limits. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Intakes - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/types-of-intakes.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Principles of an Intake - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/principles-of-an-intake.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/types-of-intakes.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/8375-intake.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo of FTC 8375 Vulcan Robotics, Res-Q, 'great example of funneling'"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1},
{"id":"multi-stage-roller-intake","name":"Multi-stage roller intake (roller conveyor)","aliases":["multi-stage roller","roller conveyor transfer","staged rollers"],"category":"intake","tags":["collection","transfer","conveyor"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"roller-intake","parent":"roller-conveyor","type":"roller-intake","short":"A row of rollers or wheels, one after another, collects a piece and carries it up or back inside the robot in one motion.","how":"Several roller shafts, usually belted or geared to one motor, are spaced along a path. Each stage hands the piece to the next, so the intake also works as a transfer. gm0 shows an Ultimate Goal intake where a series of omni wheels lifts rings at a steep angle, and Rover Ruckus robots that used several tubing sets to raise minerals into elevated boxes.","uses":["lifting pieces from the floor into a hopper or shooter","Ultimate Goal ring intakes"],"pros":["collect and transfer in one mechanism","can store several pieces along the path"],"cons":["more shafts, belts and alignment work","jams where a piece moves from one roller to the next"],"failures":["piece stalls in the gap between stages","belt between stages slips or snaps"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"piece carried through successive rollers"},"params":[{"key":"stages","label":"Number of roller stages","unit":"count","min":2,"max":8,"default":4,"estimate":true},{"key":"pathAngle","label":"Path angle","unit":"deg","min":0,"max":80,"default":45,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:ultimate-goal","historical:rover-ruckus"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["G407","R105","R503"],"note":"Checked against the BIOBUZZ G407, R105 and R503 limits. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Transfer Types - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Types of Intakes - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/types-of-intakes.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/7244-roller.jpg","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo of FTC 7244 OUT of the BOX Robotics, Ultimate Goal"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1},
{"id":"deployable-intake","name":"Deployable (drop-down) intake","aliases":["drop-down intake","pivoting intake","flip-out intake","servo-deployed intake","motor-driven deployable intake","swing-arm intake","retractable roller","hinged ramp intake"],"category":"intake","tags":["collection","transfer","deploy"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"roller-intake","type":"deploy-intake","preset":{"states":{"up":0,"down":90},"upper":110,"lower":0,"speed":180},"short":"An intake on a hinge that folds inside the robot to start, swings down to collect, and flips back up to pass the piece on.","how":"The roller (or other collector) is mounted on an arm that pivots about a hinge on the chassis. A servo, or a motor for heavy intakes, rotates the arm between a stowed or transfer angle and a down angle at floor level. A separate motor or CR servo spins the roller. gm0 calls the flip-up hand-off a flip-up transfer: compact and flexible, but slower and exposed to hits while deployed.","uses":["fitting a wide intake inside the 18 in starting cube","lifting a collected piece up to a transfer"],"pros":["stows inside the starting size","the pivot can double as the transfer"],"cons":["two actuators (pivot and roller)","slower than a fixed intake","can be hit by other robots while deployed"],"failures":["pivot servo stalls under the intake's weight","intake bounces on the floor and misses pieces","wires to the roller motor snag at the hinge"],"dof":2,"actuation":"motor+servo","motion":{"input":"servo or motor rotation at the hinge, plus roller motor","output":"intake swings down or up; roller spins"},"params":[{"key":"deployAngle","label":"Deploy angle","unit":"deg","min":30,"max":180,"default":90,"estimate":true},{"key":"armLength","label":"Pivot to roller distance","unit":"in","min":3,"max":12,"default":6,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:rover-ruckus","historical:freight-frenzy"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R102","R105","G407","R503"],"note":"Checked against BIOBUZZ R102 (starts inside an 18 in cube), R105 (expansion limit enforced physically), G407 and R503. Legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Transfer Types - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Principles of an Intake - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/principles-of-an-intake.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"2018-19 Rover Ruckus 9410 (F.W. Parker Robotics)","url":"https://www.fwparkerrobotics.org/2018-19-rover-ruckus-9410.html","kind":"team"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":1},
{"id":"four-bar-deployable-intake","name":"Four-bar deployable intake","aliases":["four-bar intake","4-bar intake","parallelogram intake","linkage-deployed intake"],"category":"intake","tags":["collection","linkage","deploy"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"four-bar","parent":"deployable-intake","type":"four-bar","preset":{"states":{"down":0,"up":90},"upper":120,"lower":0,"speed":180},"short":"The intake rides on a four-bar linkage, so it moves down and out while staying at the same angle to the floor.","how":"Two parallel links of equal length join the intake to the chassis. When one link is rotated, the intake head translates along an arc without tilting, as gm0 describes for four-bars. That keeps the roller at a constant angle to the floor and the pieces at every height. Without special construction, travel is limited to less than 180 degrees.","uses":["intakes that must stay level while lowering","clearing a bumper or frame rail before reaching the floor"],"pros":["intake angle stays constant","linkage holds position well near over-centre"],"cons":["takes more frame space than a simple pivot","under 180 degrees of travel"],"failures":["link pins wear and the head starts to tilt","uneven links twist the mechanism"],"dof":2,"actuation":"motor+servo","motion":{"input":"servo or motor rotating one link, plus roller motor","output":"intake head translates on an arc, still level; roller spins"},"params":[{"key":"linkLength","label":"Link length","unit":"in","min":2,"max":10,"default":5,"estimate":true},{"key":"travel","label":"Travel angle","unit":"deg","min":30,"max":170,"default":90,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R105","G407","R503"],"note":"Checked against the BIOBUZZ R105, G407 and R503 limits. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Linkages - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linkages.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"telescoping-intake","name":"Telescoping (slide-extended) intake","aliases":["telescoping intake","horizontal extension intake","slide intake","linkage-slide intake"],"category":"intake","tags":["collection","linear-slide","reach"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"linear-slide","parent":"extendo","type":"linear-slide","preset":{"states":{"in":0,"out":5},"upper":6,"lower":0,"speed":30},"short":"The intake sits on horizontal slides that shoot it out to reach pieces far from the robot, then pull it back to hand pieces off.","how":"Drawer, extrusion or linkage slides carry the intake forward. They are driven by a string spool, a belt or a servo-powered linkage. Usually a pivot at the end of the slides drops the roller or claw to the floor. gm0 shows linkage-driven slides (Skystone) and a flip-up intake on linear slides (Freight Frenzy).","uses":["reaching into a crowded or protected zone","collecting without driving the whole robot"],"pros":["long reach without moving the drivetrain","fast cycles when paired with a transfer"],"cons":["more actuators and wiring","must respect the expansion limit"],"failures":["string or belt slips, so the slide won't fully retract","cables snag during extension","slides bind when racked sideways"],"dof":2,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"motor or servo driving the slides, plus intake actuator","output":"intake translates outward; roller or claw collects"},"params":[{"key":"extension","label":"Extension","unit":"in","min":4,"max":6,"default":5,"estimate":true,"note":"BIOBUZZ R105: 24 in. total length, so about 6 in. beyond an 18 in. frame"},{"key":"extendTime","label":"Full extension time","unit":"s","min":0.2,"max":2,"default":0.5,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:skystone","historical:freight-frenzy","historical:into-the-deep"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R105","G407","R503"],"note":"Checked against BIOBUZZ R105 (expansion limit, enforced physically, not in software), G407 and R503. Legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Linkages - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linkages.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Transfer Types - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linkages.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/7236-linkage-slides.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo of FTC 7236 Recharged Green, Skystone, linkage-driven slides"},"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"scoop-intake","name":"Scoop intake","aliases":["scoop","shovel intake","plow scoop","passive scoop"],"category":"intake","tags":["collection","passive"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"scoop","type":"bucket","preset":{"states":{"hold":0,"dump":120},"upper":150,"lower":0,"speed":180},"short":"A flat lip or tray that the robot drives under a piece, then tilts up to keep it. There are no spinning parts.","how":"A thin leading edge slides under the piece as the robot drives forward. A servo or motor then tilts the scoop up so the piece sits in a pocket. It is passive collection: the drivetrain does the collecting and the shape holds the piece, unlike a roller intake that actively pulls. Tilting further dumps the piece.","uses":["flat or heavy pieces that rollers struggle with","simple first-year collectors"],"pros":["very simple","no roller motor needed"],"cons":["slow and needs accurate driving","pieces can be pushed away instead of scooped"],"failures":["lip catches on tile seams","piece slides off while tilting"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation","output":"scoop tilts to hold or dump"},"params":[{"key":"scoopWidth","label":"Scoop width","unit":"in","min":3,"max":16,"default":8,"estimate":true},{"key":"tiltAngle","label":"Hold tilt","unit":"deg","min":10,"max":60,"default":30,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["G407","R105"],"note":"There is no game-specific check. The BIOBUZZ G407 and R105 limits apply to any collector, and legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Passive Intakes and Claws - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/passive-intake-claw.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"low","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":1},
{"id":"pivoting-bucket-intake","name":"Pivoting bucket intake (collect, flip, dump)","aliases":["pivoting bucket intake","intake box","collection box","bucket intake","grate transfer bucket"],"category":"intake","tags":["collection","transfer","bucket"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"scoop","parent":"scoop-intake","type":"bucket","preset":{"states":{"hold":0,"dump":130},"upper":160,"lower":0,"speed":180},"short":"A box with a roller in front fills up on the floor, then pivots up to dump its pieces into the scoring mechanism.","how":"A roller or tubing feeds pieces into a box or bucket. The box sits on a pivot driven by a motor or servo that flips it over to empty into a deposit. A variant is gm0's grate transfer: forks on the bucket pass between forks on the deposit, so the pieces move across without the two parts colliding.","uses":["Rover Ruckus mineral collection boxes","holding a fixed number of pieces before transfer"],"pros":["holds several pieces","the pivot doubles as the transfer"],"cons":["slower than a direct transfer","box size must match the allowed piece count"],"failures":["pieces fall out while flipping","grate forks misalign and collide"],"dof":2,"actuation":"motor+servo","motion":{"input":"roller motor plus pivot actuator","output":"bucket fills, then rotates to dump"},"params":[{"key":"capacity","label":"Piece capacity","unit":"count","min":1,"max":6,"default":2,"estimate":true,"note":"BIOBUZZ G407 allows controlling at most 4 SCORING ELEMENTS"},{"key":"dumpAngle","label":"Dump angle","unit":"deg","min":60,"max":180,"default":130,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:rover-ruckus"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["G407","R105","R503"],"note":"Checked against the BIOBUZZ G407, R105 and R503 limits. Legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Transfer Types - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Principles of an Intake - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/principles-of-an-intake.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"historical","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"passive-funnel-intake","name":"Passive funnel or guide plates","aliases":["passive funnel","guide plates","flexible guide-plate","Lexan guides","polycarbonate funnel"],"category":"intake","tags":["collection","passive","funnel"],"entry":"component","family":"funnel","type":null,"short":"Fixed angled plates that steer pieces into the intake or hopper as the robot drives. They have no motor, and flexible plastic lets them bend instead of break.","how":"Angled walls narrow the path so a piece hitting anywhere across the front slides toward one spot. It is passive guiding: nothing moves, and the drivetrain or an active roller supplies the motion. gm0 recommends clear materials such as Lexan so the driver can see pieces, and flexible materials that spring back after impacts. Because nothing moves, there is no joint to animate.","uses":["widening an intake's capture zone","centring pieces before a claw or transfer"],"pros":["no actuator or code","cheap and light","polycarbonate survives impacts"],"cons":["only works with something else providing motion","takes space at the front of the robot"],"failures":["plate bends permanently","pieces wedge in the corners"],"dof":0,"actuation":"passive","motion":{"input":"robot driving or roller","output":"pieces steered to one point"},"params":[{"key":"funnelAngle","label":"Wall angle","unit":"deg","min":10,"max":60,"default":30,"estimate":true},{"key":"plateThickness","label":"Plate thickness","unit":"mm","min":0.5,"max":3,"default":1.5,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:res-q"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R105","R302"],"note":"BIOBUZZ R302 allows modified raw materials such as plastics, and plates must stay inside R105. Legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Principles of an Intake - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/active-intake/principles-of-an-intake.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","parent":"guide-channel"},
{"id":"claw","name":"Claw (servo gripper)","aliases":["gripper","pincer","pivoting-jaw claw","single-servo claw","grabber","end effector","inward-rotating gripper","passive intake (gm0 term)"],"category":"intake","tags":["scoring","gripper","single-piece"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"claw","type":"claw","preset":{"states":{"open":0,"closed":35},"upper":60,"lower":0,"speed":240},"short":"Servo-driven jaws close on one game piece at a time. It is slower than a roller but grips and places odd-shaped pieces precisely.","how":"One or more jaws pivot on pins, and a servo closes them around the piece. This grips one object, which is different from active collection (rollers keep pulling pieces in) and from passive retention (a shape or spring holds the piece). gm0 calls this a passive intake: the driver lines up and grabs each piece. Claws often score too, because handing a held piece to another mechanism is hard.","uses":["large or awkward pieces (Relic Recovery relic and glyphs)","placing pieces precisely on a scoring location","beginner robots without a motor to spare"],"pros":["simple and quick to build","no motor slot needed","handles odd shapes","places precisely"],"cons":["slower cycles: the driver must line up each grab","more fragile","the long time spent collecting leaves the robot open to defense"],"failures":["servo strips or stalls when the jaws are over-closed","piece slips because the jaws are too smooth","jaws bend after a collision"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation","output":"jaws pivot closed or open"},"params":[{"key":"jawOpening","label":"Max jaw opening","unit":"mm","min":20,"max":200,"default":80,"estimate":true,"note":"depends on the game piece"},{"key":"servoTravel","label":"Servo travel used","unit":"deg","min":10,"max":180,"default":40,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz","historical:relic-recovery","historical:centerstage","historical:into-the-deep"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["G407","R105","R503"],"note":"Checked against the BIOBUZZ G407, R105 and R503 limits. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Passive Intakes and Claws - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/passive-intake-claw.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Servo-Driven Gripper Kit (Servo Included) - ServoCity","url":"https://www.servocity.com/servo-driven-gripper-kit-servo-included/","kind":"vendor"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://www.servocity.com/servo-driven-gripper-kit-servo-included/","src":"https://cdn11.bigcommerce.com/s-f6vfspkkjf/images/stencil/original/products/4866/21111/3219-0002-0002_Ang1__87203__94021.1716233997.jpg?c=1","credit":"ServoCity store listing, Servo-Driven Gripper Kit 3219-0002-0002"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":1},
{"id":"parallel-gripper","name":"Parallel-jaw gripper","aliases":["parallel gripper","single-servo parallel-jaw claw","four-bar synchronized claw","parallelogram claw","self-centering gripper","parallel claw"],"category":"intake","tags":["gripper","single-piece"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"claw","parent":"claw","type":"parallel-gripper","preset":{"states":{"open":2,"closed":0},"upper":3,"lower":0,"speed":4},"short":"The two jaws slide toward each other and stay parallel the whole way, so they grip flat-sided pieces evenly at any width.","how":"Gears and parallelogram linkages tie both jaws to one servo, so each jaw face moves straight in while staying parallel. ServoCity describes its kit's jaws as staying parallel throughout their range, driven by one servo. Because both jaws move together, the piece ends up centred between them.","uses":["boxes and blocks with flat sides","centring a piece for a precise placement"],"pros":["even grip across the whole face","self-centring","sold as a ready-made goBILDA-compatible kit"],"cons":["more linkage parts than a pivoting claw","heavier (the ServoCity kit is 133 g)"],"failures":["linkage play lets the jaws splay","servo overload when closed on a hard object"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation","output":"both jaws translate inward, staying parallel"},"params":[{"key":"jawStroke","label":"Jaw opening","unit":"in","min":0.5,"max":5,"default":2,"estimate":true,"note":"ServoCity does not list the kit's opening range"}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["G407","R503"],"note":"Checked against the BIOBUZZ G407 control limit and R503 servo count. Check R502 servo limits for the servo you use. Legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Servo-Driven Parallel Gripper Kit (Servo Included) - ServoCity","url":"https://www.servocity.com/servo-driven-parallel-gripper-kit-servo-included/","kind":"vendor"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://www.servocity.com/servo-driven-parallel-gripper-kit-servo-included/","src":"https://cdn11.bigcommerce.com/s-f6vfspkkjf/images/stencil/original/products/4867/21113/3219-0001-0002_Ang_1_Open__94041__27435.1716233998.jpg?c=1","credit":"ServoCity store listing, Servo-Driven Parallel Gripper Kit 3219-0001-0002 (open)"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":1},
{"id":"gear-synchronized-claw","name":"Gear-synchronized claw (one powered jaw, one geared jaw)","aliases":["gear-synchronized claw","geared claw","meshed-gear claw","synchronized jaw claw"],"category":"intake","tags":["gripper"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"claw","parent":"claw","type":"claw","preset":{"states":{"open":0,"closed":35},"upper":60,"lower":0,"speed":240},"short":"One servo turns one jaw, and a pair of meshed gears makes the other jaw mirror it, so both close together.","how":"Each jaw is fixed to a gear, and the two gears mesh. The servo drives one jaw's gear, and the mesh turns the other jaw the opposite way by the same angle. The RoboFTC claw (Into the Deep) is described this way: a single servo opens and closes jaws that are geared together, with one side powered and one passive.","uses":["symmetric grabbing of samples or pixels","3D-printed claws"],"pros":["one servo for two moving jaws","jaws stay symmetric, so the piece centres"],"cons":["gear backlash adds play","printed gears wear"],"failures":["gear teeth skip under load","jaws drift out of sync after a crash"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation","output":"two jaws rotate symmetrically"},"params":[{"key":"jawOpening","label":"Max jaw opening","unit":"mm","min":20,"max":150,"default":60,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:into-the-deep"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["G407","R503"],"note":"Checked against the BIOBUZZ G407 and R503 limits. Legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"RoboFTC claw CAD","url":"https://roboftc.github.io/cad/claw.html","kind":"team"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":1},
{"id":"dual-servo-claw","name":"Dual-servo claw (independent jaws)","aliases":["dual-servo claw","two-servo claw","independent jaw claw"],"category":"intake","tags":["gripper"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"claw","parent":"claw","type":"claw","preset":{"states":{"open":0,"closed":35},"upper":60,"lower":0,"speed":240},"short":"Each jaw has its own servo, so the jaws can move together or one at a time.","how":"Two servos each pivot one jaw. Code commands them together for a normal grab or separately to nudge a piece sideways, release one side first, or grab off-centre. It costs a second servo port (R503 caps a robot at 8 servos).","uses":["releasing a piece in a controlled tip","grabbing pieces against a wall"],"pros":["flexible control","more total gripping torque"],"cons":["two servo ports","jaws can fight each other if poorly calibrated"],"failures":["servo endpoints mismatched, so one jaw stalls","jaws collide when both close too far"],"dof":2,"actuation":"servo+servo","motion":{"input":"two servo rotations","output":"each jaw pivots independently"},"params":[{"key":"jawOpening","label":"Max jaw opening","unit":"mm","min":20,"max":200,"default":80,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R503","G407"],"note":"Checked against the BIOBUZZ R503 limit of 8 servos and the G407 control limit. Legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Passive Intakes and Claws - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/passive-intake-claw.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":2},
{"id":"asymmetric-claw","name":"Asymmetric claw (one fixed jaw)","aliases":["asymmetric gripper","single-jaw claw","one-sided claw","fixed-finger claw"],"category":"intake","tags":["gripper"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"claw","parent":"claw","type":"claw","preset":{"states":{"open":0,"closed":40},"upper":70,"lower":0,"speed":240},"short":"One jaw is fixed and only the other moves, pressing the piece against the fixed side.","how":"A rigid finger is part of the frame, and one servo swings a single moving jaw against it. The piece is always referenced to the fixed jaw, which makes placement repeatable from that side, but the piece doesn't centre.","uses":["picking pieces off a wall or out of a corner","the simplest possible gripper"],"pros":["fewest parts","fixed jaw gives a known reference edge"],"cons":["piece doesn't centre","the robot must line up the fixed jaw"],"failures":["moving jaw pushes the piece away before it closes","fixed jaw bends"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation","output":"one jaw pivots against a fixed jaw"},"params":[{"key":"jawOpening","label":"Max opening","unit":"mm","min":20,"max":150,"default":60,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["G407","R503"],"note":"The general BIOBUZZ G407 and R503 limits apply. Legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Passive Intakes and Claws - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/passive-intake-claw.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"low","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":1},
{"id":"dual-object-claw","name":"Dual-object claw (holds two pieces)","aliases":["dual-object claw","double claw","two-pixel claw","split claw","dual-finger claw"],"category":"intake","tags":["gripper","scoring"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"claw","parent":"claw","type":"claw","preset":{"states":{"open":0,"closed":35},"upper":60,"lower":0,"speed":240},"short":"A claw built to grab two pieces at once, often with a separate finger for each so it can drop them one at a time.","how":"The jaws are wide enough for two pieces side by side or stacked, and are usually split into two fingers with their own servos. One CENTERSTAGE team (Team Inspiration) added a claw that picks up two pixels for placing on the backdrop. Separate fingers let the driver place each piece in a different spot.","uses":["CENTERSTAGE two-pixel placement","halving trips when two pieces go to nearby spots"],"pros":["twice the pieces per cycle","each piece can be released separately"],"cons":["wider and heavier","more servos"],"failures":["second piece slips when the first is released","fingers catch on each other"],"dof":2,"actuation":"servo+servo","motion":{"input":"two servo rotations","output":"each finger grips or releases its own piece"},"params":[{"key":"pieces","label":"Pieces held","unit":"count","min":2,"max":2,"default":2,"estimate":false}],"seasons":["historical:centerstage"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["G407","R503"],"note":"Checked against BIOBUZZ G407 (at most 4 SCORING ELEMENTS controlled) and the R503 servo count. Legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Team Inspiration 2023-2024 robot (Andy)","url":"https://team-inspiration.squarespace.com/2023-2024andy","kind":"team"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"historical","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":2},
{"id":"adaptive-claw","name":"Adaptive (Fin Ray / compliant finger) claw","aliases":["underactuated adaptive claw","compliant finger claw","Fin Ray gripper","FinGripper","soft gripper","TPU fingers"],"category":"intake","tags":["gripper","compliant"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"claw","parent":"claw","type":"claw","preset":{"states":{"open":0,"closed":35},"upper":60,"lower":0,"speed":240},"short":"Flexible fingers that bend around whatever they press on, so one claw can hold many shapes without careful aiming.","how":"Each finger is a V-shaped flexible frame with cross ribs (the Fin Ray effect). Pressing on one side makes the finger curl toward the object instead of away, so it wraps around the contact point. The finger is passive: an ordinary servo or motor closes the fingers, and the shape does the adapting. Make Magazine describes a hobby gripper built from Festo FinGripper fingers.","uses":["irregular or delicate pieces","grabbing pieces at uncertain angles"],"pros":["conforms to many shapes","gentle on pieces","forgiving of aim"],"cons":["less precise placement","printed or soft fingers wear","weaker hold than rigid jaws for heavy pieces"],"failures":["ribs crack from fatigue","finger folds the wrong way under an edge load"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation","output":"fingers close and bend around the piece"},"params":[{"key":"fingerLength","label":"Finger length","unit":"mm","min":30,"max":150,"default":70,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["G407","R503"],"note":"These are engineering references, not FTC rulings. The BIOBUZZ G407 and R503 limits still apply, and legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Adaptive Gripping Mechanism - Make:","url":"https://makezine.com/article/maker-news/adaptive-gripping-mechanism","kind":"engineering"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":1},
{"id":"three-finger-gripper","name":"Three-finger gripper","aliases":["three-finger gripper","3-finger gripper","tripod gripper","robotic hand gripper"],"category":"intake","tags":["gripper","industrial"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"claw","parent":"claw","type":"claw","preset":{"states":{"open":0,"closed":35},"upper":60,"lower":0,"speed":180},"short":"Three fingers close from three sides to cradle round or irregular objects. It is mostly an industrial and research design.","how":"Three fingers are spaced around a palm and closed by one or more actuators, often through linkages so they close together. Research versions such as the UdeM prototype use Fin Ray fingers so each one conforms to the object. FTC use is not verified here.","uses":["round or irregular objects in industry and research","a concept to explore for unusual game pieces"],"pros":["centres round objects","stable three-point hold"],"cons":["complex linkage or several actuators","bulky for an FTC robot"],"failures":["fingers out of sync","linkage binding"],"dof":1,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"actuator rotation","output":"three fingers close together"},"params":[{"key":"graspDiameter","label":"Max grasp diameter","unit":"mm","min":20,"max":200,"default":80,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["non-ftc"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"This is an industrial and research design, so it is not marked FTC-legal. Check the current manual if you build one."},"refs":[{"title":"Prototype design and implementing of a three-finger adaptive robotic gripper based on the FinRay structure","url":"https://revistas.udem.edu.co/index.php/ingenierias/article/view/2199","kind":"engineering"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"toggle-linkage-claw","name":"Toggle (over-centre) linkage claw","aliases":["toggle-linkage gripper","over-center claw","over-centre gripper","locking linkage claw","cam-actuated gripper"],"category":"intake","tags":["gripper","linkage"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"claw","parent":"claw","type":"claw","preset":{"states":{"open":0,"closed":40},"upper":60,"lower":0,"speed":240},"short":"A linkage pushes the jaws just past a straight-line point, so they lock shut and the servo doesn't have to keep squeezing.","how":"The servo drives a link that pushes the jaw links. As the links come into line, the jaw force rises sharply. Driving slightly past that line (over-centre) locks the jaws, so the piece can't backdrive them open. gm0 notes that over-centred linkages are harder to backdrive and that linkage force and speed change across the stroke. A cam-actuated gripper reaches a similar lock with a cam profile.","uses":["holding a piece through collisions","reducing servo load while holding"],"pros":["high holding force from a small servo","holds even if servo power drops"],"cons":["needs careful geometry (check it in CAD)","the servo must pull it back over centre to release"],"failures":["linkage not quite over centre, so it springs open","jams past its stop"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation","output":"jaws close and lock over centre"},"params":[{"key":"overCentre","label":"Past-centre angle","unit":"deg","min":1,"max":15,"default":5,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["G407","R503"],"note":"The general BIOBUZZ G407 and R503 limits apply. Legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Linkages - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linkages.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":1},
{"id":"passive-retention-claw","name":"Passive-retention gripper (snap or spring hold)","aliases":["passive retention claw","snap gripper","spring clip gripper","wedge gripper","passive gripper","passive latch"],"category":"intake","tags":["gripper","passive"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"claw","parent":"claw","type":"passive-gripper","preset":{"states":{"open":20,"closed":0},"upper":40,"lower":0,"speed":240},"short":"A shaped pocket or spring-loaded finger snaps over a piece and holds it without power. A servo or push is only needed to let go.","how":"The piece is pushed into a wedge, pocket or spring-loaded finger that flexes open and then snaps back behind it. This is passive retention: the shape or spring does the holding, unlike a claw that is actively closed or a roller that keeps pulling. A small servo or a release ramp frees the piece.","uses":["holding a preload during autonomous","picking pieces off a wall by driving into them"],"pros":["holds with no power","very simple"],"cons":["release needs its own mechanism","works for one piece shape only"],"failures":["spring too weak, so the piece falls out","snap too stiff, so it won't seat or release"],"dof":1,"actuation":"spring","motion":{"input":"robot pushing into the piece, plus a release servo","output":"finger flexes and snaps shut"},"params":[{"key":"springForce","label":"Retention force","unit":"N","min":1,"max":30,"default":5,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["G407","R105"],"note":"The general BIOBUZZ G407 and R105 limits apply. Legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Passive Intakes and Claws - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/passive-intake-claw.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"low","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"winch","name":"Winch (spool) for hanging or lifting","aliases":["cable-driven lift winch","string spool","winch pulley","cable winch"],"category":"endgame","tags":["hang","lift","rigging"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"winch","type":"winch","short":"A motor winds string or cable onto a spool to pull a mechanism or, in past games, the robot itself.","how":"A motor, usually heavily geared, turns a spool. String or Kevlar cable runs from the spool to a hook already placed on a bar, or through a slide's rigging. Winding shortens the line and lifts the robot. gm0 notes that spool radius sets speed against force, and that string must not overlap on the spool because overlap changes the effective diameter. In CENTERSTAGE, a robot Suspended from the Rigging scored 20 points.","uses":["CENTERSTAGE suspension from the rigging","pulling a robot up after a hook is placed","retracting slides used as a hang"],"pros":["lots of lift from one geared motor","compact spool"],"cons":["needs a separate way to reach and hook the bar","the robot may sag when power is cut unless something locks it"],"failures":["string overlaps or wraps off the spool","Kevlar frays on sharp edges","motor stalls or overheats under the robot's weight"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"cable wound in; robot lifted"},"params":[{"key":"spoolCircumference","label":"Spool circumference","unit":"mm","min":40,"max":160,"default":112,"estimate":false,"note":"112 mm matches goBILDA's 3407/3410 dual-spool winch pulleys"},{"key":"liftHeight","label":"Lift height","unit":"in","min":2,"max":24,"default":6,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["historical:centerstage","historical:into-the-deep"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["G415"],"note":"Hanging from ARENA elements is prohibited in BIOBUZZ (G415); a winch used to rig slides or other mechanisms is judged by the other rules. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Rigging - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/rigging.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"CENTERSTAGE game one-page summary","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/archive/2024/game/game-one-page","kind":"official"},{"title":"3407 Series Hub-Mount Winch Pulley (Dual Spool, 112mm Circumference) - goBILDA","url":"https://www.gobilda.com/3407-series-hub-mount-winch-pulley-dual-spool-112mm-circumference/","kind":"vendor"}],"image":{"kind":"component-photo","page":"https://www.gobilda.com/3407-series-hub-mount-winch-pulley-dual-spool-112mm-circumference/","src":"https://cdn11.bigcommerce.com/s-x56mtydx1w/images/stencil/original/products/1679/8766/3407-0002-0112_Ang2__62884__38806.1701993572.jpg?c=1","credit":"goBILDA store listing","shows":"the winch pulley (spool) only, not a full winch"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"historical","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1},
{"id":"hook-and-winch-hang","name":"Hook-and-winch hang","aliases":["hook and winch","hook-on then winch","raise hook then pull up","winch-based hanging","pull-up winch"],"category":"endgame","tags":["hang"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"winch","parent":"winch","type":"winch","short":"An arm or slide lifts a hook over the bar, then a separate winch reels in to pull the robot up.","how":"Stage one: a light arm, slide or spring places a hook on the bar. Stage two: a cable from the hook runs down to a motor-driven spool on the chassis, and winding it lifts the robot. Splitting the job lets the reach mechanism stay light, while the winch's gearing carries the robot's weight.","uses":["CENTERSTAGE rigging suspension"],"pros":["reach mechanism doesn't carry the robot's weight","strong pull from a geared winch"],"cons":["two steps take endgame time","the hook can fall off before the cable is tight"],"failures":["hook slips off the bar while slack is taken up","cable tangles during deployment"],"dof":2,"actuation":"motor+servo","motion":{"input":"hook deploy actuator, then winch motor","output":"hook placed; robot lifted"},"params":[{"key":"hookReach","label":"Hook reach above floor","unit":"in","min":12,"max":36,"default":24,"estimate":true,"note":"set by the season's bar height"}],"seasons":["historical:centerstage"],"legality":{"status":"not-allowed","rules":["G415"],"note":"Historical reference only. BIOBUZZ G415 forbids suspending from ARENA elements, and the game has no hang task."},"refs":[{"title":"CENTERSTAGE game one-page summary","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/archive/2024/game/game-one-page","kind":"official"},{"title":"Rigging - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/rigging.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"historical","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":1},
{"id":"hang-hook","name":"Hang hook (climber)","aliases":["hanging hook","climb hook","hook","rotating hanging hook","dual-hook hang","latch hook","climber"],"category":"endgame","tags":["hang"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"hang-hook","type":"hang-hook","preset":{"states":{"stowed":0,"raised":90},"upper":120,"lower":0,"speed":90},"short":"A hook that is raised to catch a field bar so the robot can lift itself. It was used in games with a hang or ascent task.","how":"A hook on an arm, slide or rotating mount is raised above a field bar, the robot drives or lowers it onto the bar, and a winch, slide or arm then lifts the robot. Two hooks, one on each side, keep the robot from twisting. INTO THE DEEP scored ASCENT LEVELs 1-3 on the RUNGS (3, 15 and 30 points in TELEOP). CENTERSTAGE scored 20 points for suspension from the rigging.","uses":["INTO THE DEEP ascent","CENTERSTAGE suspension","Rover Ruckus lander hanging"],"pros":["high endgame points in hang games","simple shape"],"cons":["must carry the whole robot's weight","needs a way to reach the bar and then lift"],"failures":["hook misses or slips off the bar","hook bends under load","robot swings and hits the field structure"],"dof":1,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"motor or servo raising the hook","output":"hook raised to the bar; robot later lifted"},"params":[{"key":"hookThroat","label":"Hook opening","unit":"mm","min":20,"max":80,"default":40,"estimate":true,"note":"wider than the bar"},{"key":"hookCount","label":"Number of hooks","unit":"count","min":1,"max":2,"default":2,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["historical:rover-ruckus","historical:centerstage","historical:into-the-deep"],"legality":{"status":"not-allowed","rules":["G415"],"note":"Historical reference only. BIOBUZZ has no hang task, and G415 forbids a ROBOT grasping, attaching to or suspending from ARENA elements."},"refs":[{"title":"INTO THE DEEP Competition Manual, Section 10 Game Details","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/archive/2025/game/manual-10","kind":"official"},{"title":"CENTERSTAGE game one-page summary","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/archive/2024/game/game-one-page","kind":"official"},{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML)","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"historical","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"linear-slide-hang","name":"Slide or telescoping hang","aliases":["linear-slide hanging assembly","telescoping hanging hook","compact retracting extension","rack-and-pinion hang","slide hang","elevator hang","lift hang"],"category":"endgame","tags":["hang","lift","linear-slide"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"linear-slide","parent":"hang-hook","type":"lift","preset":{"states":{"down":0,"up":20},"upper":30,"lower":0,"speed":15},"short":"Slides extend straight up to put hooks over a bar, then retract to pull the robot up.","how":"Vertical slides (string-rigged, belt or rack-and-pinion) carry hooks at the top. Extending reaches the bar, and powering the slides back down lifts the chassis, so the retract direction must carry the robot's weight. FTC 9410 used an HDPE/Delrin rack and pinion with a passive Delrin latch for hanging in Rover Ruckus. For an INTO THE DEEP LEVEL 3 ASCENT, the robot had to be fully supported by the HIGH RUNG and completely above the LOW RUNG.","uses":["Rover Ruckus lander hang","INTO THE DEEP level 2 and 3 ascent","reusing scoring slides to hang"],"pros":["straight, repeatable reach","can share hardware with a scoring lift"],"cons":["slides and rigging must carry the full robot","needs stiff slides to avoid bending"],"failures":["retract string snaps under load","slides bow and bind","robot slides back down when power is cut"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor via spool, belt or pinion","output":"hooks extend up, then the chassis is pulled up"},"params":[{"key":"extension","label":"Extension","unit":"in","min":6,"max":36,"default":20,"estimate":true},{"key":"stages","label":"Slide stages","unit":"count","min":1,"max":4,"default":2,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["historical:rover-ruckus","historical:into-the-deep","historical:centerstage"],"legality":{"status":"not-allowed","rules":["G415","R105"],"note":"As a hang, this is a historical reference: BIOBUZZ G415 forbids suspending from ARENA elements. Slides used for other jobs still follow R105."},"refs":[{"title":"2018-19 Rover Ruckus 9410 (F.W. Parker Robotics)","url":"https://www.fwparkerrobotics.org/2018-19-rover-ruckus-9410.html","kind":"team"},{"title":"INTO THE DEEP Competition Manual, Section 10 Game Details","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/archive/2025/game/manual-10","kind":"official"},{"title":"Rigging - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/rigging.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"historical","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":2},
{"id":"pivoting-hang-arm","name":"Pivoting hang arm","aliases":["pivoting hanging arm","fold-out endgame arm","rotating hook arm","swing-up hook","flip-out hang arm"],"category":"endgame","tags":["hang","arm"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"arm","parent":"hang-hook","type":"hang-hook","preset":{"states":{"stowed":0,"raised":110},"upper":150,"lower":0,"speed":60},"short":"An arm folds up from the chassis to swing a hook over the bar. Rotating it back down, or winching, lifts the robot.","how":"A hook sits at the end of an arm pivoting on the chassis. A geared motor (or a servo for a deploy-only arm) swings it up past the bar. Rotating it back, or pulling with a winch, transfers the robot's weight onto the bar. The motor needs a lot of gearing because the robot's weight acts at the arm's full length.","uses":["CENTERSTAGE suspension","reaching a bar that sits higher than the robot"],"pros":["simple pivot","folds flat inside the starting size"],"cons":["high torque needed at the pivot","hook arcs, so the robot must be positioned well"],"failures":["arm gearbox strips under the robot's weight","arm flexes and the hook slips"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation at the pivot","output":"hook arm swings up, then lifts the robot"},"params":[{"key":"armLength","label":"Arm length","unit":"in","min":6,"max":24,"default":14,"estimate":true},{"key":"gearReduction","label":"Gear reduction","unit":":1","min":20,"max":200,"default":100,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["historical:centerstage"],"legality":{"status":"not-allowed","rules":["G415"],"note":"Historical reference only. BIOBUZZ G415 forbids suspending from ARENA elements."},"refs":[{"title":"CENTERSTAGE game one-page summary","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/archive/2024/game/game-one-page","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"low","status":"historical","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1},
{"id":"passive-latch-hook","name":"Passive latching hook","aliases":["passive engagement hook","auto-latching hook","spring latch hook","self-centering hang hook","passive Delrin latch"],"category":"endgame","tags":["hang","passive"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"hang-hook","parent":"hang-hook","type":"hang-hook","preset":{"states":{"stowed":0,"raised":90},"upper":120,"lower":0,"speed":90},"short":"A hook with a spring latch that snaps shut once the bar or bracket is inside, so it can't fall off and holds without power.","how":"The hook's mouth has a sprung gate or swivelling latch. When the bar or bracket pushes past it, the latch flips closed behind it. That locks the robot on even when the motors are off at the end of the match. FTC 9410 used a passive Delrin latch on its Rover Ruckus rack-and-pinion hang.","uses":["Rover Ruckus lander latching","any hang that must hold after power is cut"],"pros":["holds without power","forgiving of driver timing"],"cons":["needs a release method to unlatch (Rover Ruckus robots had to drop from the lander)","the latch spring must be tuned"],"failures":["latch doesn't snap fully closed","latch jams and won't release"],"dof":1,"actuation":"spring","motion":{"input":"bar pushing the latch","output":"latch snaps closed behind the bar"},"params":[{"key":"latchSpring","label":"Latch spring force","unit":"N","min":1,"max":20,"default":4,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["historical:rover-ruckus"],"legality":{"status":"not-allowed","rules":["G415"],"note":"Historical reference only. BIOBUZZ G415 forbids attaching to ARENA elements."},"refs":[{"title":"2018-19 Rover Ruckus 9410 (F.W. Parker Robotics)","url":"https://www.fwparkerrobotics.org/2018-19-rover-ruckus-9410.html","kind":"team"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"historical","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"two-stage-ascent","name":"Two-stage (rung-to-rung) ascent","aliases":["level 3 ascent","rung-to-rung climb","two-stage hang","multi-level climb"],"category":"endgame","tags":["hang","sequence"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"hang-hook","parent":"hang-hook","type":"lift","preset":{"states":{"down":0,"up":20},"upper":30,"lower":0,"speed":15},"short":"A climb in two steps: hang from a low bar first, then reach up and transfer to a higher bar. It was required for INTO THE DEEP's top ascent level.","how":"First hooks catch the LOW RUNG and lift the robot off the tiles. A second set of hooks, or the same slides re-extended, then reaches the HIGH RUNG and pulls the robot up until the HIGH RUNG alone supports it. The INTO THE DEEP manual did not allow touching the HIGH RUNG while the robot was supported by the tiles, so the low-rung step was mandatory. That made it a sequenced climb, not a single pull.","uses":["INTO THE DEEP LEVEL 3 ASCENT (30 points)"],"pros":["reaches the highest-value ascent"],"cons":["long, complex sequence","needs strong slides and careful code or driver timing"],"failures":["robot swings and loses the low rung during transfer","hooks hit the frame of the SUBMERSIBLE","the sequence runs out of endgame time"],"dof":2,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"slides or arms plus hooks","output":"robot climbs from the low rung to the high rung"},"params":[{"key":"climbTime","label":"Climb time","unit":"s","min":3,"max":30,"default":10,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["historical:into-the-deep"],"legality":{"status":"not-allowed","rules":["G415"],"note":"Historical reference only. BIOBUZZ G415 forbids suspending from ARENA elements, and the game has no ascent."},"refs":[{"title":"INTO THE DEEP Competition Manual, Section 10 Game Details","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/archive/2025/game/manual-10","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"high","status":"historical","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"shared-mechanism-hang","name":"Hang using the scoring arm or slides","aliases":["shared-mechanism hang","hang on the claw arm","reuse scoring slides to hang","integrated hang"],"category":"endgame","tags":["hang","arm","linear-slide"],"entry":"concept","family":"hang-hook","parent":"hang-hook","type":"hang-hook","preset":{"states":{"stowed":0,"raised":90},"upper":120,"lower":0,"speed":60},"short":"Instead of building a separate climber, hooks are added to an arm or slides the robot already uses for scoring.","how":"Hooks are mounted on the scoring arm or lift, which already reaches upward. At the end of the match the driver raises it over the bar and drives the actuator back down to lift the robot. One CENTERSTAGE team (Team Inspiration) added its hang to the claw arm to reuse actuators that already extend upward. The scoring actuators must then be strong enough to carry the whole robot.","uses":["saving motor ports and weight","CENTERSTAGE suspension"],"pros":["no extra actuator","less weight"],"cons":["scoring mechanism must carry the full robot","a hang crash can break the scorer"],"failures":["arm gearbox or slide rigging overloads","hooks get in the way during scoring"],"dof":1,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"existing arm or lift actuator","output":"hooks raised, then the robot lifted"},"params":[],"seasons":["historical:centerstage"],"legality":{"status":"not-allowed","rules":["G415"],"note":"Historical reference only. BIOBUZZ G415 forbids suspending from ARENA elements."},"refs":[{"title":"Team Inspiration 2023-2024 robot (Andy)","url":"https://team-inspiration.squarespace.com/2023-2024andy","kind":"team"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"historical","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"drone-launcher","name":"Drone (paper plane) launcher with servo release","aliases":["servo-release mechanism","plane launcher","paper airplane launcher","PEM","plane ejection mechanism","rubber band launcher","spring launcher","Spring / elastic launcher","drone launcher","plunger launcher"],"category":"endgame","tags":["launcher","servo-release","scoring"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"launcher","type":"drone-launcher","preset":{"states":{"locked":0,"released":45},"upper":90,"lower":0,"speed":360},"short":"A stretched rubber band or spring is held back by a servo latch. Releasing it flings a paper drone into a scoring zone.","how":"The drone sits on a carrier or rail, pulled forward by a stretched rubber band or spring. A servo arm holds the carrier back until a button press rotates the servo and lets the stored energy launch the drone. Distance is tuned with band tension and drone design. CENTERSTAGE scored drones in Landing Zones 1-3 for 30, 20 or 10 points. The Licejus team launched along a REV 15 mm extrusion, tested springs and rubber bands, and settled on a rubber band.","uses":["CENTERSTAGE endgame drone launch","any one-shot spring or band release"],"pros":["one servo, no motor","the stored energy gives a fast launch"],"cons":["one shot","accuracy depends on band tension and the paper plane"],"failures":["band loses tension over a long event","servo latch releases early from vibration","drone hits the truss"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation (latch release)","output":"band or spring launches the drone"},"params":[{"key":"launchAngle","label":"Launch angle","unit":"deg","min":10,"max":60,"default":35,"estimate":true},{"key":"bandStretch","label":"Band stretch","unit":"mm","min":20,"max":200,"default":80,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["historical:centerstage"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["G405"],"note":"BIOBUZZ has no drone task. G405 forbids deliberately ejecting a SCORING ELEMENT from the FIELD, and stored-energy rules must be checked in the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"CENTERSTAGE game one-page summary","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/archive/2024/game/game-one-page","kind":"official"},{"title":"The Plane Ejection Mechanism (Licejus robotics)","url":"https://robotics.licejus.lt/resources/component-pages/pem.html","kind":"team"},{"title":"Team Inspiration 2023-2024 robot (Andy)","url":"https://team-inspiration.squarespace.com/2023-2024andy","kind":"team"}],"image":{"kind":"cad","page":"https://robotics.licejus.lt/resources/component-pages/pem.html","src":"https://robotics.licejus.lt/resources/robot-img/vliegtuigschieter_render.png","credit":"Licejus robotics team site, render of their Plane Ejection Mechanism"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"historical","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":1},
{"id":"relic-extension-arm","name":"Long-reach placement arm (relic arm)","aliases":["extension placement arm","high-reach placement","rotating placement assembly","relic arm","relic extension"],"category":"endgame","tags":["placement","linear-slide","claw"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"linear-slide","type":"linear-slide","preset":{"states":{"in":0,"out":24},"upper":36,"lower":0,"speed":20},"short":"A long extension carrying a claw that reaches far beyond the robot to pick up and set down one special field object, as Relic Recovery robots did with the relic.","how":"Telescoping slides, often extending horizontally well past the frame, carry a claw at the tip. A motor-driven spool or belt extends them, a servo claw grabs the object, and a wrist or pivot may rotate it upright before release. gm0 shows the relic claws teams used in Relic Recovery.","uses":["Relic Recovery relic placement","any task that places one object far from the robot"],"pros":["very long reach","compact when retracted"],"cons":["a long extension sags and sways","must stay within that season's expansion rules"],"failures":["slides droop and the claw misses","rigging tangles on retraction"],"dof":2,"actuation":"motor+servo","motion":{"input":"spool motor plus claw servo","output":"claw extends far out, grips and places"},"params":[{"key":"extension","label":"Reach","unit":"in","min":12,"max":48,"default":30,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["historical:relic-recovery"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R105"],"note":"Historical reference. A reach this long would need checking against the BIOBUZZ R105 expansion limit."},"refs":[{"title":"Passive Intakes and Claws - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/passive-intake-claw.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"component-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/passive-intake-claw.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/8680-relic-claw.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo of FTC 8680 Kraken-Pinion, Relic Recovery","shows":"the relic claw at the end of the arm, not the whole extension"},"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"historical","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":1},
{"id":"hang-alignment-guide","name":"Hang alignment guide","aliases":["field-element alignment assembly","bar guide","rung guide","stabiliser bumper","deployable alignment guide"],"category":"endgame","tags":["hang","alignment","passive"],"entry":"concept","family":"guide","type":null,"short":"Passive ramps or bumpers that steer hooks onto a bar and steady the robot against the field structure while it climbs.","how":"Angled plates or rollers on the robot contact the bar or the structure around it, so small driving errors are corrected as the hooks rise. During the climb they rest against the structure to stop swinging. The INTO THE DEEP manual allowed lateral contact with non-RUNG parts of the SUBMERSIBLE for stabilization while ascending. This is a design idea drawn from that rule, not a documented team mechanism.","uses":["making hook engagement repeatable","reducing swing during a climb"],"pros":["no actuator","cheap"],"cons":["only helps if the field allows that contact","adds parts at the robot's edges"],"failures":["guide catches on the structure and stops the climb"],"dof":0,"actuation":"passive","motion":{"input":"robot movement","output":"hooks steered onto the bar"},"params":[],"seasons":["historical:into-the-deep"],"legality":{"status":"not-allowed","rules":["G415"],"note":"Historical concept only. BIOBUZZ G415 forbids grasping or suspending from ARENA elements."},"refs":[{"title":"INTO THE DEEP Competition Manual, Section 10 Game Details","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/archive/2025/game/manual-10","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"linear-slide","name":"Telescoping linear slide (1 to 4+ stages)","aliases":["linear slides","slides","viper slide","viper slides","telescoping slide","multi-stage slide","2-stage slide","3-stage slide","4-stage slide","linear extension","parallel slides","dual slide set","slide pair"],"category":"lift","tags":["extension","scoring","intake","lift"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"linear-slide","type":"linear-slide","preset":{"states":{"in":0,"out":24},"lower":0,"upper":30},"short":"A set of nested rails that slide past each other so a motor and string or belt can push a carriage far out of the robot in a straight line.","how":"Each stage is a rail that slides inside or beside the previous one; a motor-driven spool, belt or chain pulls the stages apart and a second run pulls them back. Total extension is roughly the number of stages times the travel per stage. Cascade rigging moves every stage at once (stage n moves at n times stage 1 relative to the base), while continuous rigging moves one stage at a time. Teams usually run two slide sets side by side, tied together, to stop sag and twisting.","uses":["vertical lift to a high goal or basket","horizontal intake extension (extendo)","hang or climb (past games; BIOBUZZ has no hang, G415)","pivoting slides on an arm"],"pros":["long, straight reach from a compact retracted size","many COTS slide kits exist","easy to control with an encoder and PID"],"cons":["string or belt rigging needs tensioning and maintenance","long horizontal extensions sag","more stages add weight and lower stiffness"],"failures":["string slips off a pulley or loosens","stages bind from misalignment or one side being driven harder","end stops break from shock at full extension","wire snags on the moving stages"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation (spool, pulley or sprocket)","output":"carriage translation along the slide"},"params":[{"key":"stages","label":"Moving stages","unit":"count","min":1,"max":5,"default":3,"estimate":true,"note":"stage count of the slide set"},{"key":"travelPerStage","label":"Travel per stage","unit":"mm","min":100,"max":450,"default":244,"estimate":true,"note":"a 336 mm goBILDA Viper-Slide lists 244 mm travel"},{"key":"totalExtension","label":"Total extension","unit":"mm","min":150,"max":1400,"default":700,"estimate":true,"note":"about stages x travel per stage; check the R105 limit"},{"key":"rigging","label":"Rigging","unit":"choice","choices":["cascade","continuous","belt","chain"],"default":"continuous","estimate":true},{"key":"spoolDiameter","label":"Spool or pulley diameter","unit":"mm","min":15,"max":60,"default":38,"estimate":true,"note":"bigger spool = faster, less force"}],"seasons":["general","historical:into-the-deep","historical:centerstage","historical:power-play","historical:rover-ruckus"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R303"],"note":"A single-DoF COTS part under R303; legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Linear Motion Guide - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/index.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Rigging - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/rigging.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"4 Stage Viper-Slide Kit (Belt-Driven, 336mm Slides) - goBILDA","url":"https://www.gobilda.com/4-stage-viper-slide-kit-belt-driven-336mm-slides/","kind":"vendor"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://www.gobilda.com/4-stage-viper-slide-kit-belt-driven-336mm-slides/","src":"https://cdn11.bigcommerce.com/s-x56mtydx1w/images/stencil/original/products/1822/16155/3210-0003-0004_REV4Hero__20626.1771523133.jpg?c=1","credit":"goBILDA store listing (4 Stage Viper-Slide Kit, belt-driven)"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":2,"servos":0,"sensors":["motor encoder","limit switch or touch sensor (optional)"]},
{"id":"drawer-slide","name":"Drawer slide rail (ball-bearing slide)","aliases":["drawer slides","ball bearing slide","MiSUMI slides","SAR2","SAR3","SAR240","Long Robotics slides","cabinet slides"],"category":"lift","tags":["component","linear-slide","extension"],"entry":"component","family":"linear-slide","parent":"linear-slide","type":"linear-slide","preset":{"states":{"in":0,"out":9.6},"lower":0,"upper":12},"short":"A ball-bearing rail like a kitchen-drawer slide; stacked together they form the stages of most FTC lifts and extensions.","how":"Two or three nested profiles roll on caged steel balls, giving a smooth one-axis sliding guide with no drive of its own. Several are bolted together (often with printed spacers) to make a multi-stage slide, and a separate string, belt or chain rigging drives them. Steel slides such as goBILDA Viper-Slides carry more load per slide than aluminum MiSUMI-style slides.","uses":["stages of vertical lifts","horizontal intake extensions","small linear guides for end effectors"],"pros":["very smooth","stiff and strong for their size","widely sold for FTC"],"cons":["steel slides are heavy","aluminum slides wear and develop play","often need spacers to stack"],"failures":["balls wear into aluminum rails causing play","chips or dirt make the slide gritty","end stops fail and spill balls","slide bends if not mounted to channel"],"dof":1,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"pull from rigging","output":"rail translation"},"params":[{"key":"length","label":"Rail length","unit":"mm","min":200,"max":500,"default":336,"estimate":true},{"key":"travel","label":"Travel per slide","unit":"mm","min":150,"max":400,"default":244,"estimate":false,"note":"goBILDA 336 mm steel Viper-Slide listing"},{"key":"material","label":"Material","unit":"choice","choices":["steel","aluminum"],"default":"steel","estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R303"],"note":"Single-DoF COTS slide rails fit R303 in the BIOBUZZ TU08 manual; the mechanism built from them must still meet all robot rules."},"refs":[{"title":"Drawer Slides - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/drawer-slides.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Steel Viper-Slide (14-Ball Carriage, 336mm Length, 244mm Travel) - goBILDA","url":"https://www.gobilda.com/steel-viper-slide-14-ball-carriage-336mm-length-244mm-travel/","kind":"vendor"}],"image":{"kind":"component-photo","page":"https://www.gobilda.com/steel-viper-slide-14-ball-carriage-336mm-length-244mm-travel/","src":"https://cdn11.bigcommerce.com/s-x56mtydx1w/images/stencil/2400x2400/products/1622/13610/Hero__94085.1729286440.jpg?c=1","credit":"goBILDA store listing","shows":"a single steel Viper-Slide drawer slide"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"extrusion-slide","name":"Extrusion slide (bushing or V-wheel on extrusion)","aliases":["REV linear motion kit","REV slides","15mm extrusion slides","V-wheel slide","V-groove bearing slide","goRAIL","OpenBuilds V-slot","X-rail","8020 slide","bushing slide"],"category":"lift","tags":["component","linear-slide"],"entry":"component","family":"linear-slide","parent":"linear-slide","type":"linear-slide","preset":{"states":{"in":0,"out":12},"lower":0,"upper":16},"short":"Lengths of aluminum extrusion that slide along each other on plastic bushings or V-groove wheels, forming the stages of a slide.","how":"Each stage is a piece of extrusion; plastic sliders ride in the T-slots, or V-groove bearings on both sides bite the extrusion's grooves. The stages stack and are driven by the same string, belt or chain rigging as drawer slides. Crossbeams between the left and right sets keep stages aligned.","uses":["kit-built lifts for REV or goBILDA teams","gantries and light extensions"],"pros":["cheap and light (REV)","bolts straight to the kit structure","V-wheel kits can be very sturdy"],"cons":["bushing kits bind and flex out of the box","V-wheel kits can be heavy","needs tuning"],"failures":["sliders bind or wear loose","stages twist out of alignment without crossbeams","end caps break from shock at full extension"],"dof":1,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"pull from rigging","output":"stage translation"},"params":[{"key":"guide","label":"Guide type","unit":"choice","choices":["bushing","v-wheel"],"default":"v-wheel","estimate":true},{"key":"extrusionSize","label":"Extrusion size","unit":"mm","min":15,"max":20,"default":15,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:rover-ruckus","historical:relic-recovery"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R303"],"note":"COTS slide kits are single-DoF parts under BIOBUZZ R303; check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Extrusion Slides - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/extrusion-slides.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/extrusion-slides.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/8680-slides.jpg","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo by FTC 8680 Kraken-Pinion (REV slides, Rover Ruckus)"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"linear-bearing-slide","name":"Linear bearing slide (MGN rail or round shaft)","aliases":["MGN slides","MGN12","MGN9","linear rail","linear guide","round linear bearings","carbon fiber tube slides"],"category":"lift","tags":["component","linear-slide"],"entry":"component","family":"linear-slide","parent":"linear-slide","type":"linear-slide","preset":{"states":{"in":0,"out":10},"lower":0,"upper":14},"short":"A precise steel rail or round shaft with a recirculating-ball block that rides on it, used as a stiff, smooth slide stage.","how":"Recirculating balls in the block roll along the rail or shaft, giving smooth, low-play one-axis motion. Blocks and rails are joined with custom or printed parts into stages and driven by normal rigging. Round bearings on a shaft can also let a part rotate about that shaft.","uses":["stiff custom lifts","lightweight carbon-tube extensions"],"pros":["very smooth and stiff","steel rails do not wear like aluminum"],"cons":["heavier than aluminum drawer slides","needs custom mounts","cheap rails need cleaning and grease"],"failures":["balls fall out when a block is removed from its rail","soft tubes wear under round bearings","poor quality control on cheap rails"],"dof":1,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"pull from rigging","output":"block or stage translation"},"params":[{"key":"railSize","label":"Rail size","unit":"MGN","min":7,"max":12,"default":9,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:rover-ruckus","historical:skystone"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R303"],"note":"Single linear bearings are single-DoF parts, but check every part and the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Linear Bearing Slides - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/linear-bearing-slides.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"component-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/linear-bearing-slides.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/mgn-slides.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0)","shows":"MGN linear rails and blocks in several sizes"},"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"extendo","name":"Horizontal extension slide (extendo)","aliases":["extendo","horizontal slides","horizontal extension","intake slides","horizontal extendo"],"category":"lift","tags":["intake","extension","linear-slide"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"linear-slide","parent":"linear-slide","type":"linear-slide","preset":{"states":{"in":0,"out":5},"upper":6,"lower":0},"short":"Slides mounted flat that shoot the intake forward across the floor to grab game pieces without driving.","how":"Same telescoping slides as a lift, laid horizontally, usually with continuous rigging and a separate retract run because gravity does not pull them home. Slides are often mounted facing each other and the outer end may ride on omni wheels to cut sag. Carriage travel equals spool travel for continuous rigging, or n times the first stage for cascade.","uses":["reaching into a submersible or zone for pieces","feeding a transfer to a vertical lift"],"pros":["fast reach without moving the drivetrain","no gravity load on the motor"],"cons":["sag at full extension","needs a positive retract","takes floor-level frame space"],"failures":["slides sag and the intake drags","retract string slack lets slides drift out","cable carrier or wires snag"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor or servo rotation","output":"intake translation forward"},"params":[{"key":"extension","label":"Extension","unit":"mm","min":50,"max":700,"default":150,"estimate":true,"note":"BIOBUZZ R105: 24 in. total length, so about 6 in. (150 mm) beyond an 18 in. frame; longer only in past games such as INTO THE DEEP"},{"key":"stages","label":"Stages","unit":"count","min":1,"max":4,"default":2,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:into-the-deep","historical:rover-ruckus"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R105"],"note":"BIOBUZZ TU08 R105 limits how far the robot may expand and requires the limit to be physical, so check maximum extension at inspection."},"refs":[{"title":"Drawer Slides - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/drawer-slides.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Rigging - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/rigging.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/drawer-slides.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/7236-sar3.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo by FTC 7236 Recharged Green (Rover Ruckus horizontal extension)"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":0,"sensors":["motor encoder"]},
{"id":"angled-extension","name":"Angled extension slide","aliases":["angled slides","inclined slides","tilted lift","slanted elevator"],"category":"lift","tags":["linear-slide","scoring"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"linear-slide","parent":"linear-slide","type":"linear-slide","preset":{"states":{"in":0,"out":24},"lower":0,"upper":30},"short":"A slide fixed at an angle so one motion reaches both up and out toward a goal.","how":"A normal telescoping slide mounted at a fixed incline; the carriage moves along the slide axis, so it rises by extension times sin(angle) and reaches out by extension times cos(angle). Gravity load on the rigging is the payload weight times sin(angle). Teams often pair one horizontal slide with one angled slide for a transfer.","uses":["scoring on a high goal set back from the robot","outtake paired with a horizontal intake slide"],"pros":["one actuator gives height and reach","no pivot to control"],"cons":["fixed angle cannot be changed in a match","tall angled slides make the robot tippy"],"failures":["side load bends stages","carriage overshoots past the goal"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"carriage translation along an inclined axis"},"params":[{"key":"angle","label":"Slide angle from floor","unit":"deg","min":30,"max":90,"default":70,"estimate":true},{"key":"extension","label":"Extension","unit":"mm","min":200,"max":1000,"default":600,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R105"],"note":"Expanded size is capped by BIOBUZZ TU08 R105; legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Transfers - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1},
{"id":"telescoping-tube-extension","name":"Telescoping tube extension (fishing-pole style)","aliases":["fishing pole extension","telescoping tubes","nested tube extension"],"category":"lift","tags":["custom","extension"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"linear-slide","parent":"linear-slide","type":"linear-slide","preset":{"states":{"in":0,"out":20},"lower":0,"upper":26},"short":"Nested tubes that slide out of each other like a fishing pole, a custom alternative to slide rails.","how":"Each smaller tube rides inside the next larger one on bushings or printed guides, so the tubes themselves are both structure and guide. FTC 7172 drove theirs with a gear rack; string rigging also works. Extension is roughly the sum of each tube's overlap-free length.","uses":["long, light custom reach"],"pros":["compact and can be very light"],"cons":["custom design and machining","play grows as guides wear"],"failures":["tubes bind under side load","tubes rotate in each other without keying"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"tube translation"},"params":[{"key":"tubes","label":"Tube sections","unit":"count","min":2,"max":5,"default":3,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["historical:rover-ruckus"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R105"],"note":"A custom build; check every part and the maximum extension against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Custom Options - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/custom.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/custom.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/7172-fishing-pole-extension.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo by FTC 7172 Technical Difficulties (Rover Ruckus)"},"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1},
{"id":"cascade-rigging","name":"Cascade rigging","aliases":["cascade","cascading string","cascade lift","cascading elevator","cascade string"],"category":"lift","tags":["rigging","subassembly"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"rigging","type":"linear-slide","short":"A way to string a slide with one line per stage so every stage moves at once and the top stage moves fastest.","how":"Each stage has its own string anchored to the stage below and running over a pulley on the stage it lifts. Relative to the base, stage 1 moves at 1x, stage 2 at 2x, stage 3 at 3x, so the last stage moves N times the first-stage (spool) travel for N stages. That multiplies speed by N and divides available force by N, so more reduction is needed as stages are added. Retraction can use a second cascade set or a continuous run with a spool N times larger.","uses":["fast vertical lifts","multi-stage extensions"],"pros":["all stages move together","fast end speed"],"cons":["one string per stage to tension","force at the carriage falls with each stage"],"failures":["one stage's string slackens and the stages desync","undersized motor stalls under load"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"spool or pulley rotation","output":"last stage moves N x first-stage travel"},"params":[{"key":"stages","label":"Stages","unit":"count","min":2,"max":5,"default":3,"estimate":true},{"key":"medium","label":"String, belt or chain","unit":"choice","choices":["string","belt","chain"],"default":"string","estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Rigging is part of a slide mechanism; legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Rigging - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/rigging.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"schematic","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/rigging.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/cascade-extension.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), cascade rigging diagram"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"continuous-rigging","name":"Continuous rigging","aliases":["continuous","continuous string","continuous lift","sequential rigging"],"category":"lift","tags":["rigging","subassembly"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"rigging","type":"linear-slide","short":"A way to string a slide with one long line through every stage so the stages extend one after another.","how":"One extension string runs from a motor spool through pulleys on each stage and ends on the last stage, with a retract string on a same-diameter spool on the same shaft. The carriage moves one-to-one with the string paid in, so carriage travel = spool radius x spool angle; the last stage goes out first, then the next, in sequence. Because there is no stage multiplication the spool can use a lower gear ratio.","uses":["vertical lifts","horizontal extensions with a with-slide retract"],"pros":["only two strings to tension","full motor force at the carriage"],"cons":["stages move in sequence, not together","long string runs"],"failures":["string slack as the run's polygon changes length","retract string not parallel bends the stages"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"spool rotation","output":"carriage translation equal to string length wound"},"params":[{"key":"spoolDiameter","label":"Spool diameter","unit":"mm","min":15,"max":60,"default":38,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Rigging is part of a slide mechanism; legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Rigging - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/rigging.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"schematic","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/rigging.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/continuous-extension.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), continuous rigging diagram"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"string-spool-drive","name":"String and spool drive with tensioner","aliases":["string-driven slide","spool","winch spool","string rigging","Dyneema rigging","spring tensioner","PTFE tube routing"],"category":"lift","tags":["rigging","subassembly","winch"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"rigging","type":"winch","short":"A motor winds string onto a spool to pull slide stages out and back, with a spring tensioner to keep the string tight.","how":"The spool converts motor rotation to string travel: travel = spool radius x angle, so a larger spool is faster but gives less force. Extension and retract spools share a shaft and must match in diameter (unless mixing cascade and continuous). A spring tensioner at the end of the run takes up slack as the string path changes length; string can also be routed through PTFE tubing to place the motor away from the slides.","uses":["driving cascade or continuous slides","hang winches (past games; BIOBUZZ has no hang, G415)"],"pros":["thin pulleys save space","easy to change speed by changing spool size"],"cons":["string stretches and loosens","tensioning is tedious"],"failures":["string overlaps on the spool and changes speed and tension","string jumps a pulley","tensioner spring fatigues"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"string travel"},"params":[{"key":"spoolDiameter","label":"Spool diameter","unit":"mm","min":15,"max":60,"default":38,"estimate":true},{"key":"tensioner","label":"Tensioner","unit":"choice","choices":["spring","sliding pulley","none"],"default":"spring","estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Part of a larger mechanism; check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Rigging - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/rigging.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"component-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/rigging.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/13075-string-tensioner.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo by FTC 13075 Coram Deo Robotics (Rover Ruckus)","shows":"a string tensioner on linear slides"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1},
{"id":"belt-driven-slide","name":"Belt-driven slide","aliases":["belt rigging","timing belt slide","belt-driven Viper-Slide kit","cascading belt","continuous belt"],"category":"lift","tags":["rigging","subassembly"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"rigging","type":"linear-slide","short":"Slides moved by a toothed timing belt instead of string, so they never need re-tensioning.","how":"A timing belt runs over pulleys on the stages and is clamped so pulling one side feeds the other one-to-one, giving positive drive both ways. Carriage travel per motor turn = pulley tooth count x belt pitch (the goBILDA 4-stage kit lists 120 mm per rotation from a 60-tooth GT2 pulley), and can be rigged cascade or continuous. Belts rarely stretch, but pulleys are thicker and wider than string pulleys.","uses":["COTS belt-driven Viper-Slide kits","custom belt elevators"],"pros":["little tensioning","repeatable encoder positions","drives both directions"],"cons":["thicker stages","heavier than string"],"failures":["belt skips teeth under shock","belt clamp slips"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"pulley rotation","output":"carriage translation (tooth count x pitch per turn, times N if cascaded)"},"params":[{"key":"pulleyTeeth","label":"Pulley teeth","unit":"count","min":16,"max":80,"default":60,"estimate":false,"note":"goBILDA 4-stage kit lists a 60T GT2 pulley"},{"key":"beltPitch","label":"Belt pitch","unit":"mm","min":2,"max":5,"default":2,"estimate":false,"note":"GT2"}],"seasons":["general","historical:rover-ruckus","historical:relic-recovery"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R303"],"note":"A single-DoF COTS part under R303; legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Rigging - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/rigging.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"4 Stage Viper-Slide Kit (Belt-Driven, 336mm Slides) - goBILDA","url":"https://www.gobilda.com/4-stage-viper-slide-kit-belt-driven-336mm-slides/","kind":"vendor"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/rigging.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/5064-cascading-belt.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo by FTC 5064 Aperture Science (Rover Ruckus cascade belt-driven)"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"chain-driven-lift","name":"Chain-driven lift or elevator","aliases":["chain elevator","chain-driven slide","chain lift","sprocket elevator"],"category":"lift","tags":["rigging","subassembly","hang"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"rigging","type":"lift","short":"A loop of roller chain between top and bottom sprockets is bolted to the carriage, so turning a sprocket lifts the carriage.","how":"Sprockets sit at the top and bottom of the fixed structure and the chain loop is attached to the carriage, so carriage travel per turn = sprocket teeth x chain pitch. It drives both directions positively. It is common for single-stage elevators; multi-stage chain designs are harder to power.","uses":["single-stage elevators","heavy hang mechanisms (past games; BIOBUZZ has no hang, G415)"],"pros":["strong","positive drive both ways"],"cons":["heavy","mostly single stage","chain needs tension"],"failures":["chain jumps a sprocket when slack","master link pops"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"sprocket rotation","output":"carriage translation (teeth x pitch per turn)"},"params":[{"key":"sprocketTeeth","label":"Sprocket teeth","unit":"count","min":10,"max":30,"default":16,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:rover-ruckus"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Rigging - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/rigging.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Custom Options - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/custom.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Elevator Design - Chief Delphi","url":"https://www.chiefdelphi.com/t/elevator-design/130084","kind":"team"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/custom.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/13075-8020-hang.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo by FTC 13075 Coram Deo Academy Robotics (Rover Ruckus chain-driven 8020 hang)"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1},
{"id":"elastic-retraction","name":"Elastic retraction (surgical tubing)","aliases":["surgical tubing retraction","bungee retraction","elastic return","rubber band retraction"],"category":"lift","tags":["rigging","subassembly","spring"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"rigging","type":"linear-slide","short":"Surgical tubing pulls a slide back in, so only one string has to be driven and tensioned.","how":"Tubing anchored to the base and the last stage stretches as the slide extends and pulls the stages home when the spool lets string out. Tubing force rises with stretch, so retraction force is uneven along the travel. It self-tensions the extension string but makes extension slower.","uses":["simple kit slides","light horizontal extensions"],"pros":["one string","self-tensioning"],"cons":["slows extension","uneven, unsmooth retraction"],"failures":["extension spool unwinds and tangles","tubing fatigues or snaps"],"dof":1,"actuation":"spring","motion":{"input":"stored elastic energy","output":"stage retraction"},"params":[{"key":"tubingLength","label":"Tubing length","unit":"mm","min":100,"max":800,"default":300,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"The BIOBUZZ manual sets no limit on mechanical springs; pneumatic parts are covered by R801. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Rigging - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/rigging.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"lead-screw","name":"Lead screw linear actuator","aliases":["lead screw","leadscrew","linear actuator","screw lift","ACME screw","linear actuator kit","screw hang"],"category":"lift","tags":["hang","endgame","linear"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"lead-screw","type":"lead-screw","preset":{"states":{"in":0,"out":8},"lower":0,"upper":8},"short":"A threaded rod spun by a motor pushes a nut along it, giving slow but very strong straight-line motion.","how":"A motor turns the screw; the nut is kept from rotating so it translates, with travel = turns x lead (a 4-start, 2 mm pitch screw has an 8 mm lead). The screw trades speed for force and can carry far more load than unmodified kit slides. Running the nut past its end stops damages the actuator, so encoder limits are needed.","uses":["hanging or lifting the robot (past games; BIOBUZZ has no hang, G415)","tilting an arm platform"],"pros":["very high force","compact","usually one motor"],"cons":["extremely slow","limited stroke"],"failures":["nut driven past its end stops","screw whips or binds if misaligned"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"nut translation (turns x lead)"},"params":[{"key":"lead","label":"Lead","unit":"mm/rev","min":2,"max":16,"default":8,"estimate":false,"note":"goBILDA 3501 Series lead screw: 8 mm lead"},{"key":"stroke","label":"Stroke","unit":"mm","min":50,"max":400,"default":201,"estimate":false,"note":"ServoCity Linear Actuator Kit (1120 series) lists 201 mm stroke"}],"seasons":["general","historical:rover-ruckus"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R303"],"note":"A single-DoF COTS part under R303; legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Lead Screws - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/lead-screws.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Linear Actuator Kit (1120 Series, 201mm Stroke) - ServoCity","url":"https://www.servocity.com/linear-actuator-kit-1120-series-201mm-stroke-8mm-lead/","kind":"vendor"}],"image":{"kind":"component-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/lead-screws.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/lead-screw.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0)","shows":"a lead screw with the nut that rides it"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"sensors":["motor encoder"]},
{"id":"dual-screw-lift","name":"Dual lead screw lift","aliases":["dual-screw lift","twin lead screw","two-screw hang"],"category":"lift","tags":["hang","endgame"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"lead-screw","parent":"lead-screw","type":"lead-screw","preset":{"states":{"in":0,"out":8},"lower":0,"upper":8},"short":"Two lead screws side by side, driven together, to lift a wide or heavy load without twisting.","how":"Two screws carry one platform, turned in sync by one motor through a belt or chain or by two motors; travel = turns x lead on each. Both screws must turn exactly together or the platform racks and binds.","uses":["robot hang (past games; BIOBUZZ has no hang, G415)","heavy platforms"],"pros":["resists twisting","very high force"],"cons":["slow","must stay synchronized"],"failures":["screws drift out of sync and bind","belt between screws skips"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"platform translation (turns x lead)"},"params":[{"key":"lead","label":"Lead","unit":"mm/rev","min":2,"max":16,"default":8,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R303"],"note":"Built from single-DoF COTS screws; check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Lead Screws - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/lead-screws.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1},
{"id":"servo-linear-actuator","name":"Servo linear actuator (linear servo)","aliases":["linear servo","micro linear actuator","Actuonix L16-R","L16 linear servo","servo actuator"],"category":"lift","tags":["component","linear"],"entry":"component","family":"lead-screw","parent":"lead-screw","type":"lead-screw","preset":{"states":{"in":0,"out":1.9},"lower":0,"upper":1.9},"short":"A small all-in-one actuator that pushes a rod in and out to a commanded position from a normal servo signal.","how":"A small motor and gearbox drive an internal screw that moves the rod; internal position feedback holds the commanded position like a rotary servo. Stroke is short (the Actuonix L16-R listing gives 48 mm electrical stroke at up to 20 mm/s). It plugs into a servo port.","uses":["latches and locks","small pushers","odometry retraction"],"pros":["position control from a servo port","holds position"],"cons":["slow","short stroke","low force"],"failures":["side loads bend the rod","stalling overheats it"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo PWM signal","output":"rod translation"},"params":[{"key":"stroke","label":"Stroke","unit":"mm","min":20,"max":140,"default":48,"estimate":false,"note":"Actuonix L16-R 50 mm listing: 48 mm electrical stroke"}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R502","R503"],"note":"BIOBUZZ TU08 R502 limits linear servos to 1 A stall at 6 V and R503 counts them toward 8 servos; check the specific part."},"refs":[{"title":"Actuonix L16 Actuator 50mm 63:1 6V RC Control - RobotShop","url":"https://www.robotshop.com/products/actuonix-l16-actuator-50mm-631-6v-rc-control","kind":"vendor"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"medium","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":1},
{"id":"rack-pinion","name":"Rack and pinion slide","aliases":["rack and pinion","gear rack","rack drive","Tetrix rack and pinion"],"category":"lift","tags":["linear"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"rack-pinion","type":"rack-pinion","preset":{"states":{"in":0,"out":10},"lower":0,"upper":12},"short":"A round gear turns against a straight toothed bar, pushing the bar in a straight line.","how":"The motor turns the pinion, which meshes with the toothed rack; travel = pinion angle in radians x pitch radius. It combines the drive and the guide in one package but is usually single-stage, since more stages need another drive. The mesh needs firm support to carry heavy loads.","uses":["single-stage lifts","light extensions","hang if well supported (past games; BIOBUZZ has no hang, G415)"],"pros":["simple","positive drive both ways"],"cons":["usually one stage","mesh fails without support"],"failures":["teeth skip or strip under load","rack deflects out of mesh"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"pinion rotation","output":"rack translation (angle x pitch radius)"},"params":[{"key":"pitchDiameter","label":"Pinion pitch diameter","unit":"mm","min":10,"max":60,"default":25,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:rover-ruckus"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Rack and Pinion - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/rack-and-pinion.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"component-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/rack-and-pinion.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/tetrix-rack-and-pinion.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (TETRIX marketing image)","shows":"the TETRIX rack and pinion parts"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"historical","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1},
{"id":"lift","name":"Vertical elevator lift","aliases":["elevator","vertical lift","single-stage elevator","multi-stage elevator","cable-and-pulley elevator","box tube elevator","vertical slides"],"category":"lift","tags":["scoring","hang","linear-slide"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"lift","type":"lift","preset":{"states":{"down":0,"up":24},"lower":0,"upper":28},"short":"A carriage that rides straight up a tower of slides or tubes to lift a scoring mechanism high.","how":"A carriage on vertical slides, box tubes or rails is lifted by string, belt, chain or a screw from a motor at the base; gravity always loads it, so it needs a holding force or a counterbalance. Single-stage elevators move one carriage the height of the tower; multi-stage elevators use cascade or continuous rigging for more height. Gravity feedforward is roughly constant along the travel.","uses":["scoring on high goals or baskets","lifting the robot to hang (past games; BIOBUZZ has no hang, G415)"],"pros":["straight vertical path is easy to control","compact footprint"],"cons":["raises the center of gravity","constantly loaded by gravity"],"failures":["slips down when unpowered","tips the robot when extended while driving","rigging breaks under shock"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"carriage vertical translation"},"params":[{"key":"stages","label":"Stages","unit":"count","min":1,"max":4,"default":2,"estimate":true},{"key":"height","label":"Lift height","unit":"mm","min":200,"max":1000,"default":600,"estimate":true,"note":"robot height is capped by R105"}],"seasons":["general","historical:power-play","historical:centerstage","historical:rover-ruckus"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R105"],"note":"BIOBUZZ TU08 R105 caps expanded robot height, so measure the lift at full extension."},"refs":[{"title":"Custom Options - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/custom.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Rigging - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/rigging.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linear-motion-guide/custom.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/7236-box-tube.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo by FTC 7236 Recharged Green (Rover Ruckus box tube elevator)"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":2,"sensors":["motor encoder","limit switch (optional)"]},
{"id":"scissor-lift","name":"Scissor lift","aliases":["scissor","scissors mechanism","double scissor","scissor extension","lazy tongs"],"category":"lift","tags":["linkage"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"scissor","type":"scissor-lift","preset":{"states":{"down":0,"up":16},"lower":0,"upper":20},"short":"Criss-crossed bars pinned in X shapes that stretch tall when the bottom ends are pushed together.","how":"Pairs of bars cross at center pivots; one end of the bottom pair is fixed and the other slides, so closing the bottom spacing extends every X at once and the top rises by many times the input travel. The motion ratio, and so the force needed, changes with angle: it is worst when collapsed, so starting from flat needs a lot of force. Double scissors stack two sets for more height.","uses":["lifting a platform straight up","specialty extensions"],"pros":["large extension from a short stack","platform stays level"],"cons":["huge force needed near collapsed","wobbles at height","many joints to keep tight"],"failures":["play in pivots causes sway","jams near flat","bars bend"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"lead screw or slide closing the bottom pivots","output":"platform vertical translation"},"params":[{"key":"levels","label":"X levels","unit":"count","min":1,"max":6,"default":3,"estimate":true},{"key":"barLength","label":"Bar length","unit":"mm","min":100,"max":400,"default":200,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R105","R202"],"note":"Check expanded size and pinch or entanglement risk against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Scissors mechanism - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scissors_mechanism","kind":"engineering"},{"title":"Scissor Lifter? - Chief Delphi","url":"https://www.chiefdelphi.com/t/scissor-lifter/139872","kind":"team"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1},
{"id":"lift-counterbalance","name":"Lift counterbalance (springs or tubing)","aliases":["counterbalanced elevator","spring-assisted elevator","spring-assisted lift","surgical tubing assist","gravity compensation spring"],"category":"lift","tags":["spring","subassembly"],"entry":"component","family":"counterbalance","type":null,"short":"Springs or tubing that carry part of a lift's weight so the motor works less and the lift does not fall when unpowered.","how":"A spring element pulls up on the carriage or a stage, offsetting a share of the moving weight. Surgical tubing loses force as the lift rises, so it only partly supports the load; constant-force springs or spring motors give a steadier force. In a cascade lift a spring on a later stage acts with the stage multiplication.","uses":["heavy elevators","holding height without motor power"],"pros":["less motor load","lift holds position better"],"cons":["stored energy hazards","tubing force varies with stretch"],"failures":["tubing snaps","spring end comes loose","over-balanced lift drifts up"],"dof":0,"actuation":"spring","motion":{"input":"stored spring energy","output":"upward force on the carriage"},"params":[{"key":"supportFraction","label":"Share of weight supported","unit":"%","min":0,"max":100,"default":60,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"The BIOBUZZ manual sets no limit on mechanical springs; pneumatic parts are covered by R801. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Elevator Design - Chief Delphi","url":"https://www.chiefdelphi.com/t/elevator-design/130084","kind":"team"},{"title":"Constant force Springs - Chief Delphi","url":"https://www.chiefdelphi.com/t/constant-force-springs/165834","kind":"team"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"constant-force-spring-lift","name":"Constant-force spring elevator","aliases":["constant force spring","CF spring","spring motor","tool balancer","constant-force-spring elevator"],"category":"lift","tags":["spring","counterbalance"],"entry":"component","family":"counterbalance","parent":"lift-counterbalance","type":null,"short":"A coiled steel strip that pulls with nearly the same force at any length, used to carry an elevator's weight.","how":"The strip uncoils from a drum as the lift moves and pulls back with a nearly constant force, so it can cancel a fixed share of carriage weight over the full travel. Both ends need straight, guided mounts. Exposed springs are a cut hazard; enclosed spring motors are safer but heavier.","uses":["counterbalancing elevators","self-retracting tethers"],"pros":["almost constant force over travel"],"cons":["sharp, can injure","costs more than tubing"],"failures":["spring spirals or tears from misalignment","end pulls loose and wraps","bolts elongate mount holes"],"dof":0,"actuation":"spring","motion":{"input":"stored spring energy","output":"constant upward force"},"params":[{"key":"springForce","label":"Spring force","unit":"N","min":5,"max":100,"default":20,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["non-ftc","general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"The BIOBUZZ manual sets no limit on mechanical springs; pneumatic parts are covered by R801. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Constant force Springs - Chief Delphi","url":"https://www.chiefdelphi.com/t/constant-force-springs/165834","kind":"team"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"linkage-slide","name":"Linkage-driven slide (linkage extension)","aliases":["linkage slides","linkage extension","servo linkage extendo","crank slides","two-bar linkage slide"],"category":"lift","tags":["linkage","extension","intake"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"crank-slider","parent":"crank-slider","type":"linkage-slide","preset":{"states":{"in":0,"out":12},"lower":0,"upper":14},"short":"A servo swings a two-bar linkage that pushes a slide out and pulls it back, with no string to tension.","how":"A slider-crank: the servo turns a crank, a connecting link pushes the slide carriage, and the slide rails constrain it to a line. Output speed is highest near the middle of the stroke, where the crank and link are near perpendicular; output force is highest near the ends of the stroke, close to the toggle positions; past center the linkage locks against backdriving. Stroke is set by the link lengths (up to about twice the crank length for an in-line layout).","uses":["fast horizontal intake extensions"],"pros":["no rigging","quick","can lock over center"],"cons":["stroke limited by link length","non-linear speed"],"failures":["ball joints pop off","servo overloaded near end of stroke"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation","output":"slide translation"},"params":[{"key":"crankLength","label":"Crank length","unit":"mm","min":50,"max":250,"default":120,"estimate":true},{"key":"linkLength","label":"Link length","unit":"mm","min":80,"max":350,"default":180,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:skystone","historical:into-the-deep"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R105"],"note":"Check maximum extension and the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Linkages - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linkages.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linkages.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/7236-linkage-slides.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo by FTC 7236 Recharged Green (Skystone)"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":2},
{"id":"arm","name":"Single-pivot arm","aliases":["arm","single arm","pivot arm","rotating arm","gear-driven arm","chain-driven arm","belt-driven arm","dual-motor arm","synchronized arm"],"category":"arm","tags":["scoring","intake"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"arm","type":"arm","preset":{"states":{"down":0,"up":90},"lower":-10,"upper":180},"short":"A bar that swings about one pivot to raise or reach out with a claw or intake.","how":"A motor (or strong servo) turns the arm about one axis, usually through gears, chain or belt for a large reduction rather than straight off the motor shaft. The end follows a circle, so height and reach are coupled and the end effector tilts as it swings. Gravity torque is proportional to cos(angle), so a cosine feedforward holds it; two motors are sometimes geared to the same shaft for more torque.","uses":["simple scorer","intake lifted over a barrier","starter-bot arms"],"pros":["simple and quick to build","few parts"],"cons":["reach limited by length, about 16 in for an arm that starts inside the 18 in cube","needs large reduction"],"failures":["sudden stops strip gearboxes or tip the robot","servos at the end break in collisions","backlash makes the end wobble"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"arm rotation about the pivot"},"params":[{"key":"length","label":"Arm length","unit":"mm","min":100,"max":450,"default":350,"estimate":true},{"key":"reduction","label":"Total reduction","unit":":1","min":20,"max":300,"default":100,"estimate":true,"note":"gm0's example single arm uses 254.5:1"},{"key":"drive","label":"Drive","unit":"choice","choices":["gear","chain","belt","direct","cable"],"default":"gear","estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:into-the-deep"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R105","R503"],"note":"Check reach against BIOBUZZ TU08 R105 and motor count against R503; legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Arms - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/arms.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Control Loops - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/software/concepts/control-loops.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"cad","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/arms.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/gb-single-arm.jpg","credit":"goBILDA product render, as shown on Game Manual 0"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"sensors":["motor encoder"]},
{"id":"arm-with-wrist","name":"Shoulder-wrist arm","aliases":["shoulder-wrist arm","arm and wrist","pivot arm with wrist","Articulated scoring arm","scoring arm","outtake arm","two-joint arm","shoulder-wrist deposit"],"category":"arm","tags":["scoring","intake"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"arm","parent":"arm","type":"arm","preset":{"states":{"down":0,"up":120},"lower":-10,"upper":200},"short":"A single-pivot arm with a servo wrist at the end so the claw can be aimed independently of the arm angle.","how":"The shoulder joint sets where the end is on its circle and the wrist servo sets the end effector's angle, two independent rotations in series (2 DOF). Software usually commands wrist angle = target angle - arm angle so the claw keeps a chosen orientation.","uses":["pick up from the floor and score flipped over the robot"],"pros":["aims the claw at any arm angle"],"cons":["wrist servo carries load at the end of a lever"],"failures":["wrist servo strips in a collision","software angle math wrong"],"dof":2,"actuation":"motor+servo","motion":{"input":"motor at shoulder, servo at wrist","output":"end effector position on an arc plus orientation"},"params":[{"key":"length","label":"Arm length","unit":"mm","min":100,"max":450,"default":350,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R503"],"note":"Check actuator counts and size against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Arms - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/arms.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":1},
{"id":"elbow","name":"Two-link arm (shoulder and elbow)","aliases":["elbow","two-link arm","articulated arm","double-jointed arm","dual-pivot arm","multi-axis arm","multi-joint arm"],"category":"arm","tags":["scoring"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"arm","type":"elbow","preset":{"states":{"straight":0,"bent":90},"lower":-150,"upper":150},"short":"An arm with a second joint partway along, like a human elbow, so it can reach more places.","how":"A shoulder joint turns the first link and an elbow joint turns the second link relative to the first; the two serial joints give 2 DOF so the tip can reach a 2D area, found with inverse kinematics. The elbow is often driven from the base by chain or belt to keep weight off the arm.","uses":["reaching over obstacles","flexible outtakes"],"pros":["reaches a whole area, not just an arc"],"cons":["harder to design, build and control","backlash stacks up at the tip"],"failures":["backlash and flex make the tip miss","packaging collisions","elbow motor overloaded"],"dof":2,"actuation":"motor+motor","motion":{"input":"shoulder and elbow rotations","output":"tip position in a plane"},"params":[{"key":"upperLength","label":"First link","unit":"mm","min":100,"max":350,"default":250,"estimate":true},{"key":"foreLength","label":"Second link","unit":"mm","min":80,"max":300,"default":200,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:freight-frenzy","historical:into-the-deep"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R503","R105"],"note":"Check actuator counts and maximum reach against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Arms - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/arms.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/arms.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/20700-snap.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo by FTC 20700 Snap (Freight Frenzy multi-axis arm)"},"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":2},
{"id":"shoulder-elbow-wrist","name":"Shoulder-elbow-wrist arm","aliases":["3-joint arm","high DOF arm","high degree of freedom outtake","articulated arm with wrist"],"category":"arm","tags":["scoring"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"arm","parent":"elbow","type":"elbow","short":"A two-link arm plus a wrist, so both tip position and claw angle can be chosen.","how":"Three serial rotary joints in one plane give 3 DOF: shoulder and elbow place the wrist, and the wrist sets the end effector's angle. Inverse kinematics solves the two arm angles, then wrist angle = desired angle - (shoulder + elbow).","uses":["high-DOF outtakes","precise placement"],"pros":["full planar position and angle"],"cons":["complex control","weight far from the pivot"],"failures":["accumulated backlash","servo or gearbox overload"],"dof":3,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"three joint rotations","output":"end effector position and angle in a plane"},"params":[],"seasons":["general","historical:centerstage"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R503"],"note":"Check actuator counts and size against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Arms - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/arms.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/arms.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/12993-RoboKings-Aurum.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo by FTC 12993 RoboKings Aurum (Centerstage high-DOF outtake arm)"},"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"pivot-extension","name":"Pivoting slide (pivot extension)","aliases":["pivot slides","pivoting linear slides","telescoping arm","arm with slides","pivot-assisted lift","pinkbot","slide arm"],"category":"arm","tags":["lift","linear-slide","scoring","intake"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"pivot-extension","type":"pivot-extension","preset":{"states":{"down":0,"up":80},"lower":-5,"upper":100},"short":"Linear slides mounted on a pivot, so one mechanism can reach far forward to intake and swing up to score.","how":"A motor tilts the slide assembly about a pivot while a second motor extends the slides (2 DOF: angle and length, polar coordinates). Gravity torque on the pivot grows with extension x cos(angle), so the pivot needs a big reduction and the feedforward must change with extension. It can remove the need for a transfer.","uses":["intake and outtake with one mechanism","reaching into a zone then scoring high"],"pros":["long reach in any direction in a plane","no transfer"],"cons":["huge pivot torque at full extension","can tip the robot"],"failures":["pivot gearbox strips","slides bend at the pivot","tipping when extended high"],"dof":2,"actuation":"motor+motor","motion":{"input":"pivot rotation and slide extension","output":"end effector position in polar coordinates"},"params":[{"key":"pivotReduction","label":"Pivot reduction","unit":":1","min":50,"max":500,"default":150,"estimate":true},{"key":"extension","label":"Slide extension","unit":"mm","min":200,"max":1000,"default":600,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:into-the-deep","historical:rover-ruckus"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R105"],"note":"BIOBUZZ TU08 R105 caps expanded size and needs a physical limit, so check every pivot angle at full extension."},"refs":[{"title":"Arms - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/arms.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/arms.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/19098-pivot-extension.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo by FTC 19098 Eastern Foxes (Into The Deep pivoting slides)"},"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":3,"sensors":["motor encoder"]},
{"id":"arm-counterbalance","name":"Arm counterbalance (counterweight, spring or gas spring)","aliases":["counterweighted arm","counterweight","spring-assisted arm","gas spring arm","gas shock","surgical tubing arm assist"],"category":"arm","tags":["spring","subassembly"],"entry":"component","family":"counterbalance","type":null,"short":"A weight, spring or gas spring that offsets an arm's weight so the motor works less and the arm holds position.","how":"Gravity torque on an arm is weight x lever arm x cos(angle); a counterweight on the opposite side of the pivot cancels it at every angle, while a spring or gas spring between frame and arm cancels it best near one angle. Size it by measuring the force needed to hold the arm at the mount point. Software adds a cos(angle) feedforward for what remains.","uses":["long or heavy arms","arms that must hold position unpowered"],"pros":["smaller motor load","smoother motion"],"cons":["counterweights add mass","springs only balance well near one angle"],"failures":["over-balanced arm springs up","tubing fatigue","gas spring loses force with temperature"],"dof":0,"actuation":"spring","motion":{"input":"stored spring energy or a counterweight","output":"torque opposing gravity at the pivot"},"params":[{"key":"balanceAngle","label":"Best-balanced angle","unit":"deg","min":0,"max":180,"default":45,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R801"],"note":"Pneumatic parts are limited to sealed, manufacturer-pre-charged COTS items such as gas shocks (R801); the manual sets no limit on mechanical springs. Legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Control Loops - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/software/concepts/control-loops.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Gas shocks - Chief Delphi","url":"https://www.chiefdelphi.com/t/gas-shocks/126158","kind":"team"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"four-bar","name":"Four-bar linkage (parallel four-bar)","aliases":["four bar","4 bar","4B","parallel four-bar","parallelogram linkage","parallelogram lift linkage","parallel arm"],"category":"arm","tags":["linkage","lift","scoring"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"four-bar","type":"four-bar","preset":{"states":{"down":0,"up":80},"lower":-80,"upper":80},"short":"Two parallel bars that swing together so the end plate stays level while it moves up and out.","how":"Four links joined by four pivots form one closed loop with 1 DOF; driving one bar moves the whole loop. When opposite links are equal (a parallelogram) the end bar stays at a constant angle while it follows an arc, so a claw stays level. A parallel four-bar's travel is limited to under 180 degrees (less than 90 degrees each way) without special construction.","uses":["keeping a claw or bucket level while lifting","short lifts"],"pros":["level end effector","stiff"],"cons":["bars use a lot of frame space","limited travel"],"failures":["slop in pivots","bars collide near limits"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"rotation of one bar","output":"end bar translation along an arc at a fixed angle"},"params":[{"key":"barLength","label":"Bar length","unit":"mm","min":80,"max":400,"default":250,"estimate":true},{"key":"barSpacing","label":"Bar spacing","unit":"mm","min":20,"max":150,"default":60,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Linkages - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linkages.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Four-bar linkage - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Four-bar_linkage","kind":"engineering"}],"image":{"kind":"cad","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linkages.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/goBILDA-four-bar.png","credit":"Game Manual 0, goBILDA four bar image"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1},
{"id":"v4b","name":"Virtual four-bar (V4B)","aliases":["V4B","virtual four bar","virtual 4 bar","chain four-bar","belt-leveled arm"],"category":"arm","tags":["linkage","scoring","transfer"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"four-bar","type":"v4b","preset":{"states":{"down":0,"up":180},"lower":-10,"upper":200},"short":"An arm with a chain or belt inside it that keeps the claw at the same angle while the arm swings, even past 180 degrees.","how":"A sprocket or pulley fixed to the frame at the shoulder is linked by chain or belt to an equal one on the end effector, so the end effector keeps its angle to the ground as the arm rotates (1 DOF). Unlike a real four-bar it can swing more than 180 degrees and is compact. A zip tie can tension the chain.","uses":["flipping a deposit from intake to scoring side","outtakes on slides"],"pros":["over 180 degrees of travel","compact"],"cons":["chain or belt can stretch","angle fixed by the ratio"],"failures":["chain slack lets the end effector droop","belt skips under load"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"arm rotation","output":"end effector arcs while keeping its angle"},"params":[{"key":"armLength","label":"Arm length","unit":"mm","min":80,"max":350,"default":180,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:ultimate-goal","historical:centerstage"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Linkages - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linkages.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":2},
{"id":"dr4b","name":"Double reverse four-bar (DR4B)","aliases":["DR4B","double reverse four bar","double four-bar","stacked four-bar","reverse four bar lift"],"category":"arm","tags":["lift","linkage"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"four-bar","type":"dr4b","preset":{"states":{"down":0,"up":16},"lower":0,"upper":20},"short":"Two four-bars stacked and geared to move opposite ways so the top goes straight up and stays level.","how":"A second four-bar is mounted on the coupler of the first and driven in reverse (often geared 1:1 between the stages), so the horizontal parts of the two arcs cancel and the top moves in a straight line at a fixed angle (1 DOF). Both sides must be driven equally to avoid twisting.","uses":["compact vertical lift"],"pros":["large straight lift from a compact package","level end"],"cons":["large space","heavy","must drive both sides equally"],"failures":["one side lifts first and racks","gear mesh between stages slips"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"bottom four-bar rotation","output":"top platform vertical translation"},"params":[{"key":"barLength","label":"Bar length","unit":"mm","min":100,"max":350,"default":220,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["historical:skystone","general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R105"],"note":"Check expanded height against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Linkages - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linkages.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linkages.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/11115-dr4b.jpg","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo by FTC 11115 Gluten Free (Skystone)"},"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"high","status":"historical","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":2},
{"id":"virtual-dr4b","name":"Virtual double reverse four-bar (VDR4B)","aliases":["VDR4B","virtual DR4B","virtual double reverse four bar"],"category":"arm","tags":["lift","linkage"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"four-bar","parent":"dr4b","type":"dr4b","preset":{"states":{"down":0,"up":12},"lower":-12,"upper":12},"short":"A chain-driven arm with a 2:1 ratio and a second bar so the end moves in a straight line and stays level.","how":"A virtual four-bar whose end sprocket or pulley is half the size of the fixed one (2:1) turns a second bar twice as fast in reverse, and another chain or belt keeps the end effector level. The result is straight-line motion with a constant angle (1 DOF), and it can extend in both directions.","uses":["compact straight lift or reach"],"pros":["more compact than a DR4B","works both directions"],"cons":["two chain or belt runs to tension"],"failures":["chain slack bends the path","ratio errors make the path curve"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"arm rotation","output":"end effector straight-line translation"},"params":[],"seasons":["historical:freight-frenzy"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Linkages - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linkages.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linkages.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/8644-vdr4b.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo by FTC 8644 Brainstormers (Freight Frenzy)"},"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"high","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1},
{"id":"five-bar","name":"Five-bar linkage","aliases":["five bar","5-bar","parallel SCARA","geared five-bar"],"category":"arm","tags":["linkage","robot"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"five-bar","type":"custom-pivot","short":"Five links in a loop driven by two motors at the base, which together place the tip anywhere in a flat area.","how":"With one link grounded, a five-bar loop has 2 DOF, so two base-mounted actuators each set one link angle and the tip position follows from both (solved with inverse kinematics). Keeping both motors at the base lowers moving inertia but gives a smaller workspace than a serial arm. Gearing two links together reduces it to 1 DOF.","uses":["planar positioning","drawing robots","haptic devices"],"pros":["both motors stay on the base","stiff"],"cons":["small workspace","singular positions"],"failures":["passes through a singularity and jams","coupled control errors"],"dof":2,"actuation":"servo+servo","motion":{"input":"two base link rotations","output":"tip position in a plane"},"params":[{"key":"linkLength","label":"Link length","unit":"mm","min":50,"max":300,"default":150,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["non-ftc","general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Not a documented FTC mechanism here; check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Five-bar linkage - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Five-bar_linkage","kind":"engineering"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":2},
{"id":"six-bar","name":"Six-bar linkage (Watt or Stephenson)","aliases":["six bar","6-bar","Watt six-bar","Stephenson six-bar"],"category":"arm","tags":["linkage"],"entry":"concept","family":"six-bar","type":"custom-pivot","short":"Six links and seven pivots that make a more complex one-motor motion than a four-bar can.","how":"Six links joined by seven revolute joints have 1 DOF: one input sets every link. Watt types join two four-bar loops (used for straight-line parallel motion); Stephenson types combine a four-bar and a five-bar loop (e.g. walking-leg linkages). They let one actuator trace paths or angle profiles a four-bar cannot.","uses":["custom output paths","walking legs"],"pros":["richer motion from one input"],"cons":["hard to design without CAD","many pivots"],"failures":["pivot slop accumulates","toggle positions jam"],"dof":1,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"one link rotation","output":"coupled motion of the output link"},"params":[],"seasons":["non-ftc","general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"A general engineering concept; check any build against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Six-bar linkage - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Six-bar_linkage","kind":"engineering"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"crank-rocker","name":"Crank-rocker four-bar","aliases":["crank rocker","Grashof crank-rocker","rocker arm drive"],"category":"arm","tags":["linkage"],"entry":"concept","family":"four-bar","parent":"four-bar","type":"custom-pivot","short":"A four-bar where one bar spins all the way round and makes another bar rock back and forth.","how":"When s + l <= p + q (Grashof) and the shortest link is the crank (adjacent to the fixed link), the crank turns fully while the rocker, the other ground-pivoted link, only swings through a limited angle (1 DOF). Continuous motor rotation thus becomes an oscillation.","uses":["oscillating sweepers","kickers driven by a motor"],"pros":["continuous motor gives repeated swing"],"cons":["swing angle fixed by link lengths"],"failures":["toggle points stall a weak motor"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"continuous crank rotation","output":"rocker oscillation"},"params":[],"seasons":["non-ftc","general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"A general engineering concept; check any build against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Four-bar linkage - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Four-bar_linkage","kind":"engineering"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"double-rocker","name":"Double-rocker four-bar","aliases":["double rocker","rocker-rocker"],"category":"arm","tags":["linkage"],"entry":"concept","family":"four-bar","parent":"four-bar","type":"custom-pivot","short":"A four-bar where both grounded bars only swing, used to turn one swing into a different swing.","how":"Neither grounded link can turn fully, so a limited input swing produces a limited output swing with a ratio that changes along the travel (1 DOF). Ackermann steering is a classic example.","uses":["steering geometry","changing a servo's swing range"],"pros":["shapes input-to-output angle"],"cons":["limited travel","non-linear ratio"],"failures":["driving past a toggle position locks it"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"rocker swing","output":"second rocker swing"},"params":[],"seasons":["non-ftc","general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"A general engineering concept; check any build against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Four-bar linkage - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Four-bar_linkage","kind":"engineering"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"crank-slider","name":"Crank-slider (slider-crank)","aliases":["slider crank","crank slider","piston linkage","connecting rod linkage"],"category":"arm","tags":["linkage","linear"],"entry":"concept","family":"crank-slider","type":"linkage-slide","preset":{"states":{"in":0,"out":4},"lower":0,"upper":4},"short":"A crank and connecting rod that turn rotation into back-and-forth straight motion, like an engine piston.","how":"Three pivots and one sliding joint form a 1-DOF loop: the crank turns, the rod pushes the slider along a line. For an in-line layout the stroke is twice the crank length and slider position is x = r cos a + sqrt(l^2 - r^2 sin^2 a). Offsetting the line of travel makes the return faster (quick return).","uses":["linkage-driven slides","pushers and kickers"],"pros":["simple, rigid","can lock at the ends"],"cons":["speed varies along the stroke"],"failures":["rod pin wears","side load on the slider binds it"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"crank rotation","output":"slider translation"},"params":[{"key":"crankLength","label":"Crank length","unit":"mm","min":10,"max":250,"default":60,"estimate":true},{"key":"rodLength","label":"Rod length","unit":"mm","min":20,"max":400,"default":150,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Check any build against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Slider-crank linkage - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slider-crank_linkage","kind":"engineering"},{"title":"Linkages - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linkages.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"scotch-yoke","name":"Scotch yoke","aliases":["slotted yoke","slotted link mechanism"],"category":"arm","tags":["linkage","linear"],"entry":"concept","family":"scotch-yoke","type":"custom-slide","short":"A pin on a spinning wheel rides in a slot on a sliding bar, pushing the bar back and forth smoothly.","how":"A pin on the rotating crank engages a slot in a yoke that can only slide, so the yoke position is r cos(angle): pure simple harmonic motion with stroke 2r (1 DOF). The slot wears from sliding contact unless a sliding block is used.","uses":["reciprocating pushers","valve actuators"],"pros":["pure sinusoidal motion","compact"],"cons":["slot wear","sliding friction"],"failures":["slot wears loose","pin binds"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"crank rotation","output":"yoke translation (r cos angle)"},"params":[{"key":"crankRadius","label":"Pin radius","unit":"mm","min":5,"max":100,"default":25,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["non-ftc","general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"A general engineering concept; check any build against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Scotch yoke - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scotch_yoke","kind":"engineering"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"toggle-linkage","name":"Toggle (over-center) linkage","aliases":["toggle linkage","toggle clamp","over-center lock","over center linkage"],"category":"arm","tags":["linkage","endgame","lock"],"entry":"concept","family":"four-bar","parent":"four-bar","type":"custom-pivot","short":"A linkage pushed just past straight so it locks in place and takes a large load with little holding force.","how":"As two links approach a straight line the mechanical advantage becomes very large, so a small input force makes a big output force. Driving slightly past straight (over center) makes the load push it further into a hard stop rather than back, so it stays locked without power (1 DOF).","uses":["clamps and grabbers","locking a hang or arm (past games; BIOBUZZ has no hang, G415)","linkage slides that resist backdrive"],"pros":["holds without power","huge force near straight"],"cons":["needs a hard stop","must be driven back out deliberately"],"failures":["stop missing so it folds the wrong way","servo cannot pull it back past center"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"link rotation","output":"locked or high-force output"},"params":[],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Check any build against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Linkage (mechanical) - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toggle_mechanism","kind":"engineering"},{"title":"Linkages - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linkages.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"pantograph","name":"Pantograph linkage","aliases":["pantograph","parallelogram scaling linkage"],"category":"arm","tags":["linkage"],"entry":"concept","family":"pantograph","type":"custom-pivot","short":"A linkage of parallelograms that copies a motion at one point to another point, scaled up or down.","how":"Links form parallelograms around a fixed pivot so the output point always moves in the same direction as the input point, by a fixed ratio set by where the links are pinned (2 DOF in the plane, since the input point can move freely). Its original use is copying and scaling drawings.","uses":["scaling a small motion to a larger one","copying a path"],"pros":["fixed, adjustable scaling"],"cons":["many joints, slop multiplies"],"failures":["pivot play makes the output wander"],"dof":2,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"input point motion","output":"scaled output point motion"},"params":[{"key":"scale","label":"Scale ratio","unit":"x","min":1,"max":5,"default":2,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["non-ftc"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"A general engineering concept; check any build against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Pantograph - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pantograph","kind":"engineering"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"bell-crank","name":"Bell crank","aliases":["bellcrank","L-crank","angle lever"],"category":"arm","tags":["linkage","component"],"entry":"component","family":"lever","type":"custom-pivot","short":"An L-shaped lever on a pivot that turns a push or pull in one direction into a push or pull in another.","how":"Rods attach to the two arms of a pivoting L (or straight) lever; pulling one rod rotates the crank and pulls the other, redirecting motion by about 90 or 180 degrees. The arm-length ratio sets the mechanical advantage; motion becomes less linear at large angles (1 DOF).","uses":["redirecting a servo's motion","linking a remote servo to a latch"],"pros":["simple","changes direction and ratio"],"cons":["non-linear at big angles"],"failures":["pivot wears","rod ends pop off"],"dof":1,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"push or pull on one arm","output":"push or pull on the other arm at an angle"},"params":[{"key":"armRatio","label":"Arm length ratio","unit":"x","min":0.3,"max":3,"default":1,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Check any build against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Bellcrank - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bell_crank","kind":"engineering"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"pushrod","name":"Pushrod link","aliases":["pushrod","connecting rod","servo linkage rod","tie rod","ball-link rod"],"category":"arm","tags":["linkage","component"],"entry":"component","family":"lever","type":"custom-pivot","short":"A rod with a pivot at each end that carries a servo's motion to a part some distance away.","how":"A servo horn moves one end of the rod and the other end moves a lever on the driven part, forming a four-bar with the horn, the lever and the frame (1 DOF). The horn and lever lengths set the angle ratio between servo and output.","uses":["driving a wrist or claw from a servo mounted lower down","remote latches"],"pros":["moves servo weight off the end of an arm"],"cons":["ratio changes with angle","adds pivots"],"failures":["ball links pop off","rod buckles under push"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo horn rotation","output":"driven lever rotation"},"params":[{"key":"rodLength","label":"Rod length","unit":"mm","min":20,"max":300,"default":100,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Check any build against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Bellcrank - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bell_crank","kind":"engineering"},{"title":"Linkages - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linkages.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":1},
{"id":"cam-follower","name":"Cam and follower","aliases":["cam","cam follower","track follower","eccentric cam"],"category":"arm","tags":["linkage","component"],"entry":"component","family":"cam","type":"custom-slide","short":"A shaped spinning lobe pushes a roller or lever, so the lobe's shape decides how the follower moves.","how":"As the cam rotates, the follower (a roller, flat or pointed tip, usually spring-loaded against the cam) rises and falls by the cam's radius at the contact point, so the profile directly programs follower position versus angle (1 DOF). Followers may translate or pivot.","uses":["kickers","timed latches","indexers"],"pros":["any motion profile from one rotation"],"cons":["needs a return spring or track","wear at contact"],"failures":["follower lifts off at speed","profile wears"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"cam rotation","output":"follower translation or swing"},"params":[{"key":"lift","label":"Follower lift","unit":"mm","min":2,"max":60,"default":15,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Check any build against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Cam follower - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cam_follower","kind":"engineering"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"geneva-drive","name":"Geneva drive","aliases":["Geneva","Geneva mechanism","Maltese cross","intermittent indexer","Cam or Geneva indexing platform","Geneva drive","cam indexer","intermittent drive"],"category":"inside","tags":["inside","indexer","linkage"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"geneva","type":"geneva-indexer","short":"A pin on a spinning wheel clicks a slotted wheel forward one step per turn, then locks it still.","how":"Each turn of the drive wheel, its pin enters a slot and turns the driven wheel 360/n degrees for n slots; a raised disc then locks the driven wheel until the next turn (1 DOF, intermittent output). A 4-slot wheel steps 90 degrees per drive revolution.","uses":["indexing a carousel or spindexer slot by slot"],"pros":["exact, locked steps without sensors"],"cons":["fixed step size","shock at each engagement"],"failures":["pin or slot wears","misaligned center distance jams"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"continuous drive wheel rotation","output":"stepwise rotation of 360/n degrees"},"params":[{"key":"slots","label":"Slots","unit":"count","min":3,"max":8,"default":4,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Check any build against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Geneva drive - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geneva_drive","kind":"engineering"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1},
{"id":"quick-return","name":"Quick-return mechanism","aliases":["quick return","Whitworth quick return","offset slider-crank","crank and slotted lever"],"category":"arm","tags":["linkage"],"entry":"concept","family":"crank-slider","parent":"crank-slider","type":"custom-slide","short":"A crank linkage that pushes forward slowly and snaps back fast, from a motor turning at constant speed.","how":"An offset slider-crank (or a crank driving a slotted lever) makes the forward and return strokes take different fractions of each revolution, so the return is faster than the working stroke (1 DOF).","uses":["cutting tools like shapers","repeated push-and-return pushers"],"pros":["fast reset each cycle"],"cons":["fixed timing ratio","more complex than a plain crank"],"failures":["slot wear","jams at extremes"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"constant crank rotation","output":"slow forward, fast return translation"},"params":[],"seasons":["non-ftc"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"A general engineering concept; check any build against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Quick return mechanism - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quick_return_mechanism","kind":"engineering"},{"title":"Slider-crank linkage - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slider-crank_linkage","kind":"engineering"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"wrist","name":"Servo wrist (pitch)","aliases":["wrist","servo wrist","pitch wrist","claw pivot","wrist pivot"],"category":"arm","tags":["intake","scoring"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"wrist","type":"wrist","preset":{"states":{"down":0,"up":90},"lower":-90,"upper":180},"short":"A servo at the end of an arm or slide that tilts the claw or intake to the right angle.","how":"A servo, usually through a servo hub or a small gear or belt stage, rotates the end effector about one axis at the tip of an arm or slide (1 DOF). The servo holds the commanded angle; on an arm, software adds the arm angle to keep the claw at a world angle.","uses":["angling a claw to pick up from the floor","tilting to score"],"pros":["simple","light"],"cons":["servo exposed to impacts at the tip"],"failures":["servo gears strip in collisions","loose horn spline slips"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation","output":"end effector pitch"},"params":[{"key":"range","label":"Servo range","unit":"deg","min":90,"max":300,"default":180,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R502","R503"],"note":"BIOBUZZ TU08 R502 caps servo power and R503 caps servo count; check the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Arms - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/arms.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":1},
{"id":"motor-wrist","name":"Motor-driven wrist","aliases":["motor wrist","DC motor wrist","encoder wrist"],"category":"arm","tags":["intake","scoring"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"wrist","parent":"wrist","type":"wrist","preset":{"states":{"down":0,"up":90},"lower":-180,"upper":180},"short":"A wrist turned by a small DC motor and encoder instead of a servo, for more torque or unlimited rotation.","how":"A geared motor, often mounted back on the arm and linked by chain or belt, rotates the end effector (1 DOF); position comes from the motor encoder with PID, not from a servo's internal feedback. It costs one of the robot's motor slots.","uses":["heavy end effectors","wrists needing more than a servo's range"],"pros":["more torque","continuous rotation possible"],"cons":["uses a motor slot","heavier","needs homing"],"failures":["encoder loses position without homing","chain slack adds backlash"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"end effector pitch"},"params":[],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R503"],"note":"Counts toward the 8-motor limit in BIOBUZZ TU08 R503; check the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Arms - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/arms.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"sensors":["motor encoder"]},
{"id":"two-axis-wrist","name":"Two-axis wrist (pitch and roll in series)","aliases":["two-axis wrist","pitch-roll wrist","wrist with twist","stacked servo wrist","wrist roll"],"category":"arm","tags":["intake","scoring"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"wrist","parent":"wrist","type":"wrist","preset":{"states":{"down":0,"up":90},"lower":-90,"upper":180},"short":"Two servos stacked, one tilting and one twisting the claw, so it can grab pieces at any angle.","how":"A pitch servo carries a second roll servo, which turns the claw about its own axis; the two rotations are independent and in series (2 DOF). The pitch servo has to carry the roll servo's weight.","uses":["picking up pieces lying at an angle","orienting pieces for scoring"],"pros":["independent, simple control of each axis"],"cons":["bulky and heavy at the tip"],"failures":["pitch servo overloaded","wires twisted by roll"],"dof":2,"actuation":"servo+servo","motion":{"input":"two servo rotations","output":"end effector pitch and roll"},"params":[],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R503"],"note":"Two servos toward the BIOBUZZ TU08 R503 limit; check the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Transfers - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Mini Turrets - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/turrets/mini-turret.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"low","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":2},
{"id":"diffy-wrist","name":"Differential wrist (diffy)","aliases":["diffy","diffy wrist","differential wrist","bevel differential wrist","differential arm joint"],"category":"arm","tags":["intake","scoring"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"wrist","type":"diffy-wrist","preset":{"states":{"down":0,"up":90},"lower":-90,"upper":180},"short":"Two servos on either side of a bevel-gear set share the work of tilting and twisting the claw.","how":"Two input bevel gears, each driven by a servo, mesh with an output bevel gear carrying the end effector. Turning both inputs the same way pitches the output; turning them opposite ways rolls it, so pitch is the average and roll the half-difference of the two inputs (2 DOF, coupled). Both servos help with each motion and their weight sits near the pivot.","uses":["compact pitch-and-roll claws"],"pros":["both servos share load","compact"],"cons":["coupled control math","gear backlash"],"failures":["bevel gears skip under load","servos fight if calibrated differently"],"dof":2,"actuation":"servo+servo","motion":{"input":"two servo rotations","output":"end effector pitch (sum) and roll (difference)"},"params":[{"key":"gearRatio","label":"Input to output bevel ratio","unit":":1","min":1,"max":3,"default":1,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R503","R303"],"note":"Two servos count toward BIOBUZZ TU08 R503; a team-built differential from single-DoF parts is the usual approach under R303."},"refs":[{"title":"Differential wrist - RoboFTC (FTC 29692)","url":"https://roboftc.github.io/cad/differential.html","kind":"team"}],"image":{"kind":"cad","page":"https://roboftc.github.io/cad/differential.html","src":"https://roboftc.github.io/images/differential.png","credit":"RoboFTC, FTC team 29692"},"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":2},
{"id":"rotating-deposit","name":"Rotating deposit (flip-out outtake on a carriage)","aliases":["rotating deposit","flip deposit","pivoting extension carriage","pivoting carriage"],"category":"score","tags":["arm","transfer","deposit"],"entry":"mechanism","family":"arm","parent":"arm","type":"arm","preset":{"states":{"down":0,"up":180},"lower":-10,"upper":200},"short":"A short arm on the lift carriage that flips the held piece from the intake side over to the scoring side.","how":"A servo (often through a virtual four-bar or a gear) rotates a holder about a pivot on the lift carriage or intake, so the piece moves from where it was received to where it is scored (1 DOF). A flip-up transfer is the same idea on the intake side.","uses":["outtakes on vertical slides","flip-up intake transfers"],"pros":["compact; can start folded in the sizing cube"],"cons":["needs careful alignment","slower than a direct transfer"],"failures":["hit by other robots when out of the frame","servo stalls under load"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation","output":"holder rotation across the robot"},"params":[{"key":"sweep","label":"Sweep angle","unit":"deg","min":90,"max":220,"default":180,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","historical:freight-frenzy","historical:rover-ruckus"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Types of Transfers - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/transfers/transfer-types.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Linkages - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/linkages.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":2},
{"id":"two-wheel-differential-drive","name":"Two-wheel differential drive (pushbot)","aliases":["2WD","pushbot","pushbot drive","two-wheel drive","differential drive","2 wheel tank","caster drive"],"category":"drive","tags":["tank","beginner","differential"],"entry":"architecture","family":"tank-drive","parent":"four-wheel-tank-drive","type":"drive-wheel","short":"Two powered wheels steer by changing speed on each side, while unpowered omni wheels or casters hold up the other end. It is the simplest drivetrain to build.","how":"One motor drives the left wheel and one drives the right wheel. The front is held up by free-spinning omni wheels. Forward speed is v = (vL + vR)/2 and turn rate is w = (vR - vL)/track width, so the robot turns about the point midway between the powered wheels. It cannot strafe.","uses":["first-season or classroom robots","prototyping a chassis before choosing a competitive drivetrain"],"pros":["fewest parts and only two drive motors","easy to program (tank or arcade drive)"],"cons":["slow and underpowered with only two motors","poor turning and acceleration compared with 4WD","Game Manual 0 calls it subpar to every other drivetrain in a competitive context"],"failures":["direct-driven wheels bending motor shafts or damaging gearboxes","robot pivots off-center if weight sits away from the powered axle","pushed around easily by other robots"],"dof":2,"actuation":"motor+motor","motion":{"input":"two motor rotations (left, right)","output":"forward travel and yaw of the chassis"},"params":[{"key":"wheelDiameter","label":"Drive wheel diameter","unit":"mm","min":72,"max":140,"default":96,"estimate":true},{"key":"trackWidth","label":"Track width (wheel to wheel)","unit":"in","min":8,"max":18,"default":14,"estimate":true},{"key":"gearRatio","label":"Wheel gear ratio (motor:wheel)","unit":"ratio","min":0.5,"max":3,"default":1,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R503"],"note":"Uses 2 of the 8 motors R503 allows; Legality still depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Tank (Skid-Steer) Drivetrains - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/drivetrains/tank.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Odometry - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/software/concepts/odometry.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"cad","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/drivetrains/tank.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/tetrix-pushbot.jpg","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), example pushbot drivetrain from the starter FTC guide"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":2,"servos":0},
{"id":"four-wheel-tank-drive","name":"Four-wheel tank drive (skid-steer)","aliases":["4WD","tank drive","skid steer","skid-steer drive","4 wheel tank"],"category":"drive","tags":["tank","differential","pushing"],"entry":"architecture","family":"tank-drive","type":"drive-wheel","short":"Four powered wheels, two per side, steer like a tank by running the left and right sides at different speeds.","how":"Each side's wheels are linked or driven together, so the chassis has two inputs. Forward speed is v = (vL + vR)/2 and turn rate is w = (vR - vL)/track width. Turning makes the wheels scrub sideways, so teams often fit two traction wheels and two omni wheels to make turning easier. It cannot strafe.","uses":["pushing and defense","games with raised terrain or obstacles","teams that value reliability over agility"],"pros":["good acceleration, traction and pushing power","can cross raised terrain","simple to program and reliable"],"cons":["cannot strafe","four traction wheels turn poorly because of wheel scrub","can tip if the center of mass is high"],"failures":["turning stalls or hops when all four wheels are traction wheels","uneven weight makes the robot pull to one side","chain or belt between wheels loosening"],"dof":2,"actuation":"motor+motor","motion":{"input":"left and right side motor rotation","output":"forward travel and yaw"},"params":[{"key":"wheelDiameter","label":"Wheel diameter","unit":"mm","min":72,"max":140,"default":96,"estimate":true},{"key":"trackWidth","label":"Track width","unit":"in","min":8,"max":18,"default":14,"estimate":true},{"key":"wheelbase","label":"Wheelbase (front to back axle)","unit":"in","min":6,"max":18,"default":12,"estimate":true,"note":"Game Manual 0 says close to square turns best"},{"key":"omniCount","label":"Number of omni wheels","unit":"count","min":0,"max":4,"default":2,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R301","R503"],"note":"R301 allows a COTS drive chassis if its parts meet the other rules and R503 caps the robot at 8 motors; legality still depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Tank (Skid-Steer) Drivetrains - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/drivetrains/tank.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Drivetrains - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/drivetrains/index.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/drivetrains/tank.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/7209-4wd.jpg","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo of FTC 7209 Tech Hogs, Relic Recovery"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":4,"servos":0},
{"id":"six-wheel-drop-center-drive","name":"Six-wheel tank drive (drop-center or corner omnis)","aliases":["6WD","drop center","drop-center 6WD","corner omni 6WD","8WD","eight wheel drive","goBILDA BeeLine","west coast drive (FRC term)"],"category":"drive","tags":["tank","defense","traction"],"entry":"architecture","family":"tank-drive","parent":"four-wheel-tank-drive","type":"drive-wheel","short":"A tank drive with three wheels per side; the middle wheel sits slightly lower (or the corners are omnis) so the robot rocks onto four wheels and turns easily.","how":"Same differential kinematics as any tank drive: v = (vL + vR)/2, w = (vR - vL)/track. In a drop-center build the middle axle is 1/16 to 1/4 in lower (about 1/8 in is common), so only four wheels touch at a time and the turning wheelbase is short. The corner-omni version keeps all axles level and uses omni wheels at the corners instead. 8WD drops the middle four wheels the same way.","uses":["defense and pushing","fast cycling games where traction matters"],"pros":["strong traction and acceleration","turns better than an all-traction 4WD","stable"],"cons":["drop-center is hard to build with channel kits","cannot strafe","corner-omni version gives up some traction"],"failures":["too little center drop so the robot will not turn","too much drop so it rocks and wobbles","sluggish feel with too slow a gear ratio"],"dof":2,"actuation":"motor+motor","motion":{"input":"left and right side motors","output":"forward travel and yaw"},"params":[{"key":"centerDrop","label":"Center wheel drop","unit":"in","min":0,"max":0.25,"default":0.125,"estimate":false,"note":"Game Manual 0 range 1/16 to 1/4 in, about 1/8 in recommended; 0 means corner omnis"},{"key":"wheelDiameter","label":"Wheel diameter","unit":"mm","min":72,"max":140,"default":96,"estimate":true},{"key":"trackWidth","label":"Track width","unit":"in","min":8,"max":18,"default":14,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R301","R503"],"note":"R301 allows a COTS drive chassis whose parts meet the other rules; R503 limits motors to 8. Check the whole robot against the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Tank (Skid-Steer) Drivetrains - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/drivetrains/tank.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/drivetrains/tank.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/7236-6wd.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo of FTC 7236 Recharged Green, Rover Ruckus, drop-center 6WD"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":4,"servos":0},
{"id":"tread-drive","name":"Tread (track) drive","aliases":["tank treads","track drive","belt tread drive","crawler","Tracked (tread) drive","tread drive","continuous track","caterpillar track"],"category":"drive","tags":["tank","terrain"],"entry":"architecture","family":"tank-drive","parent":"four-wheel-tank-drive","type":"drive-wheel","short":"Continuous treads wrapped around wheels on each side drive the robot like a real tank.","how":"Each side's tread is driven by a sprocket or pulley, so the kinematics are differential: v = (vL + vR)/2, w = (vR - vL)/track. The long contact patch gives grip on rough ground but drags sideways when turning, which makes it turn poorly.","uses":["games with ramps or terrain","pushing"],"pros":["handles terrain well","strong traction and pushing power"],"cons":["poor maneuverability and top speed","complex to build","Game Manual 0 does not consider it competitive in FTC"],"failures":["treads breaking, slipping off or misaligning","TETRIX tracks snapping and derailing"],"dof":2,"actuation":"motor+motor","motion":{"input":"left and right side motors","output":"forward travel and yaw"},"params":[{"key":"trackWidth","label":"Track width","unit":"in","min":8,"max":18,"default":14,"estimate":true},{"key":"sprocketDiameter","label":"Drive sprocket pitch diameter","unit":"mm","min":30,"max":100,"default":60,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R503"],"note":"Not checked against specific BIOBUZZ rules beyond the R503 motor limit."},"refs":[{"title":"Tank (Skid-Steer) Drivetrains - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/drivetrains/tank.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"cad","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/drivetrains/tank.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/5975-tread.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), render of FTC 5975 Cybots, Res-Q"},"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"high","status":"historical","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":2,"servos":0},
{"id":"mecanum-drive","name":"Four-wheel mecanum drive","aliases":["mecanum","mech drive","strafer","goBILDA Strafer","holonomic mecanum","mecanum chassis"],"category":"drive","tags":["holonomic","strafing","field-centric"],"entry":"architecture","family":"mecanum","type":"drive-wheel","short":"Four independently powered mecanum wheels with 45-degree rollers let the robot drive, strafe sideways and turn at the same time.","how":"Each wheel's rollers sit at 45 degrees, so each wheel pushes diagonally; combining four diagonal forces gives any motion. Wheel powers are frontLeft = y + x + rx, backLeft = y - x + rx, frontRight = y - x - rx, backRight = y + x - rx (y forward, x strafe, rx turn), divided by the largest sum so ratios are kept. Viewed from above the rollers form an X; from below they form a rhombus. Field-centric drive rotates (x, y) by minus the IMU heading first.","uses":["the most common competitive FTC drivetrain","lining up on scoring locations by strafing"],"pros":["strafes and turns at once","good acceleration and top speed","many COTS kits and examples"],"cons":["poor traction; easily pushed by a tank drive","strafing is slower than driving forward","every wheel needs its own motor"],"failures":["rollers slipping when strafing, which ruins drive-encoder odometry","wheels mounted in the wrong orientation so strafing fails","bushing rollers strafing worse than bearing rollers"],"dof":3,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"four motor rotations","output":"forward, sideways and yaw motion of the chassis"},"params":[{"key":"wheelDiameter","label":"Mecanum wheel diameter","unit":"mm","min":75,"max":140,"default":96,"estimate":false,"note":"96 mm is the goBILDA Strafer wheel"},{"key":"motorRpm","label":"Wheel free speed","unit":"rpm","min":150,"max":1150,"default":312,"estimate":false,"note":"312 rpm Yellow Jacket on the goBILDA Strafer V5"},{"key":"trackWidth","label":"Track width","unit":"in","min":8,"max":18,"default":14,"estimate":true},{"key":"wheelbase","label":"Wheelbase","unit":"in","min":8,"max":18,"default":13,"estimate":true},{"key":"strafeCorrection","label":"Strafe correction factor","unit":"x","min":1,"max":1.3,"default":1.1,"estimate":false,"note":"Game Manual 0 starting value"}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R303","R301","R503"],"note":"R303 makes holonomic (mecanum) wheels an exception to the single-DoF COTS limit and R301 allows COTS drive chassis; the whole robot still has to pass inspection."},"refs":[{"title":"Holonomic Drivetrains - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/drivetrains/holonomic.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Mecanum TeleOp - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/software/tutorials/mecanum-drive.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Strafer Chassis Kit V5 - goBILDA","url":"https://www.gobilda.com/strafer-chassis-kit-v5/","kind":"vendor"}],"image":{"kind":"cad","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/drivetrains/holonomic.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/731-mecanum.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), render of FTC 731 Wannabee Strange, Rover Ruckus, AndyMark HD mecanum wheels"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":4,"servos":0,"sensors":["IMU","drive encoders or dead wheels"]},
{"id":"kiwi-drive","name":"Kiwi drive (three omni wheels at 120 degrees)","aliases":["kiwi","3-wheel omni","three wheel omni","triangle drive","Killough platform","A-frame drive","3WD holonomic"],"category":"drive","tags":["holonomic","omni"],"entry":"architecture","family":"omni-drive","type":"drive-wheel","short":"Three powered omni wheels spaced 120 degrees apart let a triangular robot move in any direction and spin with only three motors.","how":"Each omni wheel can only push along its own rolling direction and rolls freely sideways. With the front wheel perpendicular to X, wheel speeds are M_front = X + w, M_right = -1/2 (X - sqrt3 Y) + w, M_left = -1/2 (X + sqrt3 Y) + w (scaled by wheel radius). Adding the three wheel vectors gives any translation plus rotation.","uses":["compact or triangular robots","off-season holonomic experiments"],"pros":["fully holonomic with three motors","frees motor ports compared with four-wheel holonomic drives"],"cons":["triangle footprint wastes space in a square size limit","low traction, like all omni drives","uneven speed in different directions"],"failures":["wheel slip makes encoder odometry unreliable","robot drifts or spins if one wheel's speed is off"],"dof":3,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"three motor rotations","output":"forward, sideways and yaw motion"},"params":[{"key":"wheelDiameter","label":"Omni wheel diameter","unit":"mm","min":60,"max":140,"default":96,"estimate":true},{"key":"wheelRadiusFromCenter","label":"Wheel distance from robot center","unit":"in","min":4,"max":10,"default":7,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R303","R503"],"note":"Omni wheels are an R303 exception to the single-DoF COTS limit; overall legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Kiwi drive - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kiwi_drive","kind":"engineering"},{"title":"Holonomic Drivetrains - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/drivetrains/holonomic.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":3,"servos":0},
{"id":"x-drive","name":"X-drive (four omni wheels at 45 degrees)","aliases":["X drive","omni X","holonomic X","4-wheel omni"],"category":"drive","tags":["holonomic","omni"],"entry":"architecture","family":"omni-drive","type":"drive-wheel","short":"Four omni wheels mounted on the corners at 45 degrees let the robot move in any direction at the same speed forward or sideways.","how":"Each corner wheel faces 45 degrees from the robot's axes, so each pushes diagonally. Wheel speed = (+/-vx +/- vy)/sqrt2 + w R, where R is the corner distance from center. Because every wheel contributes to forward motion at 45 degrees, it moves about sqrt2 times faster than a standard layout with the same gearing.","uses":["fast, agile robots","games with little defense"],"pros":["strafes as fast as it drives forward","good maneuverability and acceleration"],"cons":["easily pushed around","awkward packaging around other mechanisms","diagonal moves are slower (about 0.71 of forward speed)"],"failures":["direct-driven corner wheels wearing gearboxes","low traction causing slip"],"dof":3,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"four motor rotations","output":"forward, sideways and yaw motion"},"params":[{"key":"wheelDiameter","label":"Omni wheel diameter","unit":"mm","min":60,"max":140,"default":96,"estimate":true},{"key":"cornerRadius","label":"Wheel distance from center","unit":"in","min":5,"max":12,"default":8,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R303","R503"],"note":"Omni wheels are an R303 exception; overall legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Holonomic Drivetrains - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/drivetrains/holonomic.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/drivetrains/holonomic.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/5040-xdrive.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo of FTC 5040 Nuts and Bolts, Relic Recovery"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"historical","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":4,"servos":0},
{"id":"h-drive","name":"H-drive (omni wheels plus a sideways strafer wheel)","aliases":["H drive","U-drive","slide drive","strafer wheel drive"],"category":"drive","tags":["holonomic","omni"],"entry":"architecture","family":"omni-drive","type":"drive-wheel","short":"Omni wheels on the sides drive forward like a tank, and one or two extra omni wheels mounted sideways push the robot left and right.","how":"Left and right omni wheels act like a tank drive: v = (vL + vR)/2, w = (vR - vL)/track. One or two perpendicular strafer wheels give the sideways speed vy directly. Rollers let each set of wheels slide while the other set drives. Works with 3 or 5 motors.","uses":["holonomic motion with more traction than mecanum"],"pros":["good traction, top speed and agility","holonomic with as few as 3 motors"],"cons":["strafing slightly weaker than mecanum","strafer wheel may need a suspension to stay on the ground"],"failures":["strafing drifts and rotates unless corrected with a gyro","strafer wheel lifting off the floor"],"dof":3,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"3 to 5 motor rotations","output":"forward, sideways and yaw motion"},"params":[{"key":"strafers","label":"Number of strafer wheels","unit":"count","min":1,"max":2,"default":1,"estimate":true},{"key":"wheelDiameter","label":"Omni wheel diameter","unit":"mm","min":60,"max":140,"default":96,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R303","R503"],"note":"Omni wheels are an R303 exception; overall legality depends on the whole robot."},"refs":[{"title":"Holonomic Drivetrains - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/drivetrains/holonomic.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/drivetrains/holonomic.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/9804-hdrive.jpg","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo of FTC 9804 Bomb Squad, Relic Recovery"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"historical","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":3,"servos":0,"sensors":["IMU"]},
{"id":"swerve-drive","name":"Swerve drive (four steerable modules)","aliases":["swerve","4-module swerve","coaxial swerve drive","crab drive","independent steering drive"],"category":"drive","tags":["holonomic","advanced"],"entry":"architecture","family":"swerve","type":"drive-wheel","short":"Each wheel sits in a module that can both steer and drive, so the robot can point every wheel in any direction and move holonomically with traction wheels.","how":"Each module has a drive motor that spins the wheel and a steering actuator that turns it. For a robot velocity v and turn rate w, each module's wheel velocity is v + w x r_i (r_i is the module's offset from center); the steering angle is the direction of that vector and the wheel speed its length. Modules re-optimize to turn at most 90 degrees and reverse the wheel when needed.","uses":["advanced teams wanting holonomic motion with traction wheels"],"pros":["holonomic with traction wheels, so harder to push","wheel slip is lower than mecanum, which can help odometry"],"cons":["uses many motor and servo slots under the 8-motor/8-servo limit","bulky, expensive and hard to build and program","servos used for steering can be underpowered"],"failures":["steering angle drift from a slipping or unzeroed encoder","modules fighting each other when angles are wrong","steering servo stalls or overheats"],"dof":3,"actuation":"motor+servo","motion":{"input":"drive motor and steering actuator per module","output":"forward, sideways and yaw motion"},"params":[{"key":"modules","label":"Number of modules","unit":"count","min":3,"max":4,"default":4,"estimate":true},{"key":"wheelDiameter","label":"Module wheel diameter","unit":"mm","min":40,"max":100,"default":63,"estimate":true},{"key":"moduleOffset","label":"Module distance from center","unit":"in","min":4,"max":9,"default":6.5,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R303","R503"],"note":"R503 limits the robot to 8 motors and 8 servos; R303 limits COTS parts to one degree of freedom, and whether a given COTS steering module complies was not verified."},"refs":[{"title":"FTC swerve is useless? - Chief Delphi","url":"https://www.chiefdelphi.com/t/ftc-swerve-is-useless/521783","kind":"team"},{"title":"RoboFTC swerve module","url":"https://roboftc.github.io/cad/swervemodule.html","kind":"team"},{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML)","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":4,"servos":4,"sensors":["absolute steering encoders","IMU"]},
{"id":"three-module-swerve","name":"Three-module swerve drive","aliases":["3-module swerve","three wheel swerve","triangle swerve"],"category":"drive","tags":["holonomic","advanced"],"entry":"architecture","family":"swerve","parent":"swerve-drive","type":"drive-wheel","short":"A swerve drive with only three steerable modules, saving motor slots for other mechanisms.","how":"Same kinematics as four-module swerve: each module points along v + w x r_i and drives at that speed. With three modules there is one less drive motor and steering actuator, which matters under the 8-motor limit. The triangular support makes it less stable and gives less pushing force.","uses":["swerve robots that need more motors for mechanisms"],"pros":["frees a motor and a servo compared with four modules"],"cons":["less stable and less pushing power","the triangle footprint fits a square size limit poorly"],"failures":["tipping toward the unsupported side","steering drift as in any swerve"],"dof":3,"actuation":"motor+servo","motion":{"input":"three drive motors and three steering actuators","output":"forward, sideways and yaw motion"},"params":[{"key":"moduleOffset","label":"Module distance from center","unit":"in","min":4,"max":9,"default":6.5,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R503","R303"],"note":"Same concerns as four-module swerve: motor/servo count and the single-DoF COTS limit."},"refs":[{"title":"FTC swerve is useless? - Chief Delphi","url":"https://www.chiefdelphi.com/t/ftc-swerve-is-useless/521783","kind":"team"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":3,"servos":3},
{"id":"coaxial-swerve-module","name":"Coaxial swerve module","aliases":["coax swerve","swerve pod","swerve module","servo-steered swerve module"],"category":"drive","tags":["swerve","module"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"swerve","parent":"swerve-drive","type":"swerve-steer","preset":{"states":{"forward":0,"left":90},"lower":-180,"upper":180,"speed":360},"short":"One swerve corner where a drive motor spins the wheel through the steering axis while a separate actuator turns the whole wheel fork.","how":"The drive motor's power passes down through the steering axis (often via bevel gears) to the wheel, so the wheel can spin while the fork rotates. A separate servo or motor rotates the fork; a sensor reports the fork angle. Steering rotation can add a small amount of wheel spin that software must cancel unless the gearing keeps them independent.","uses":["corners of a swerve drive"],"pros":["drive and steering are independent","one actuator per function is easy to reason about"],"cons":["complex gearing inside a small space","needs a reliable steering angle sensor"],"failures":["bevel gears skipping or wearing","steering angle lost when the encoder slips","cross-coupling between steering and wheel speed"],"dof":2,"actuation":"motor+servo","motion":{"input":"drive motor rotation and steering actuator rotation","output":"wheel spin and wheel heading"},"params":[{"key":"wheelDiameter","label":"Wheel diameter","unit":"mm","min":40,"max":100,"default":63,"estimate":true},{"key":"steerRatio","label":"Steering reduction","unit":"ratio","min":1,"max":4,"default":1,"estimate":true},{"key":"driveRatio","label":"Drive reduction","unit":"ratio","min":1,"max":6,"default":2,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R303"],"note":"A team-built module is the usual route; a pre-assembled COTS module may exceed R303's single-DoF limit."},"refs":[{"title":"RoboFTC swerve module","url":"https://roboftc.github.io/cad/swervemodule.html","kind":"team"},{"title":"Differential Swerve - Bionics Docs","url":"https://bionics-docs.netlify.app/reference/robots/designs/diff-swerve","kind":"team"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":1,"sensors":["absolute steering encoder"]},
{"id":"differential-swerve-module","name":"Differential swerve module","aliases":["diff swerve","diffy swerve","differential swerve"],"category":"drive","tags":["swerve","module","differential"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"swerve","parent":"swerve-drive","type":"swerve-steer","preset":{"states":{"forward":0,"left":90},"lower":-180,"upper":180,"speed":360},"short":"A swerve module where two motors share a gear differential: turning them opposite ways spins the wheel, and the same way steers it.","how":"Two motors feed a differential, usually epicyclic gearing. Wheel speed is proportional to half the difference of the two motor speeds and module rotation to their average, each scaled by its own gear ratio. Both motors always help, so drive and steer share all the power.","uses":["high-power swerve when two motors per module are available"],"pros":["both motors contribute to driving and steering","no servo in the steering path"],"cons":["two motors per module uses up the 8-motor limit fast","complex gearing and control","FTC teams report it as very hard to finish"],"failures":["gear wear in the differential","coupled control errors that make the module wander"],"dof":2,"actuation":"motor+motor","motion":{"input":"two motor rotations","output":"wheel spin and wheel heading"},"params":[{"key":"driveRatio","label":"Translation ratio","unit":"ratio","min":1,"max":8,"default":3,"estimate":true},{"key":"steerRatio","label":"Yaw ratio","unit":"ratio","min":1,"max":8,"default":3,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R503"],"note":"Four modules would need all 8 motors R503 allows; not checked further."},"refs":[{"title":"Differential Swerve - Bionics Docs","url":"https://bionics-docs.netlify.app/reference/robots/designs/diff-swerve","kind":"team"},{"title":"FTC swerve is useless? - Chief Delphi","url":"https://www.chiefdelphi.com/t/ftc-swerve-is-useless/521783","kind":"team"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":2,"servos":0},
{"id":"steered-wheel-drive","name":"Steered-wheel drive (car-like or single steer wheel)","aliases":["Ackermann steering","car steering","tricycle drive","single steer wheel","steerable wheel drive","rear-wheel drive with steering"],"category":"drive","tags":["non-holonomic","steering"],"entry":"architecture","family":"steered-drive","type":"drive-wheel","short":"A drivetrain that steers by turning one or two wheels like a car or tricycle instead of by skid-steering.","how":"Driven wheels push the robot forward while a steering servo turns the front wheel(s). The robot follows an arc whose radius is wheelbase / tan(steer angle). With Ackermann linkage, the inner wheel turns more than the outer so both follow circles around one center. It cannot turn in place or strafe.","uses":["learning vehicle kinematics","outside FTC: cars and carts"],"pros":["very little wheel scrub","simple drive gearing"],"cons":["large turning radius; cannot spin in place","rarely competitive in FTC field play"],"failures":["steering servo overload","understeer on slippery tiles"],"dof":2,"actuation":"motor+servo","motion":{"input":"drive motor and steering servo","output":"forward travel along an arc"},"params":[{"key":"wheelbase","label":"Wheelbase","unit":"in","min":6,"max":18,"default":12,"estimate":true},{"key":"maxSteer","label":"Max steering angle","unit":"deg","min":15,"max":45,"default":30,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Not checked against the BIOBUZZ manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Ackermann steering geometry - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ackermann_steering_geometry","kind":"engineering"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1,"servos":1},
{"id":"traction-wheel","name":"Traction wheel","aliases":["grip wheel","rubber wheel","high-traction wheel","tread wheel"],"category":"drive","tags":["wheel","tank"],"entry":"component","family":"drive-wheel","type":"drive-wheel","short":"A plain rubber or treaded wheel built for maximum grip, used mostly in tank drivetrains.","how":"The wheel's rubber tread contacts the tile along its full width, so it pushes forward hard but resists sliding sideways. In a tank drive that sideways grip causes scrub during turns, which is why teams mix traction and omni wheels.","uses":["tank and 6WD drivetrains","middle wheels of a drop-center drive"],"pros":["highest pushing force","resists being pushed sideways"],"cons":["scrubs when turning","cannot strafe"],"failures":["tread wearing smooth","turning stalls with four traction wheels"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"axle rotation","output":"wheel rolling"},"params":[{"key":"wheelDiameter","label":"Diameter","unit":"mm","min":60,"max":140,"default":96,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Not checked against specific BIOBUZZ rules."},"refs":[{"title":"Drivetrains - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/drivetrains/index.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Tank (Skid-Steer) Drivetrains - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/drivetrains/tank.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"belt-driven-drivetrain","name":"Belt-driven drivetrain","aliases":["belt drive","timing belt drivetrain","HTD belt drive","GT2 belt drive"],"category":"drive","tags":["power-transmission"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"power-transmission","type":"drive-wheel","short":"Motors turn the drive wheels through timing belts and pulleys, which is quiet, precise and lets motors sit away from the wheels.","how":"A pulley on the motor shaft pulls a toothed belt that turns a pulley on the wheel axle; the pulley tooth ratio sets the gear ratio. The belt needs at least 90 degrees of wrap on each pulley (180 is best) and correct tension, often from idler pulleys.","uses":["mecanum and tank drivetrains with motors inside the frame"],"pros":["almost no slop when tensioned","quiet and more efficient than chain","pulleys can be 3D printed"],"cons":["fixed belt lengths","can be wider than chain","each belt is bought new"],"failures":["belt skipping teeth from low wrap or tension","belt falling off when loose","motor burnout from over-tension"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"wheel rotation"},"params":[{"key":"ratio","label":"Pulley ratio (wheel:motor teeth)","unit":"ratio","min":0.5,"max":3,"default":1,"estimate":true},{"key":"wrap","label":"Belt wrap on smallest pulley","unit":"deg","min":90,"max":200,"default":180,"estimate":false,"note":"Game Manual 0: at least 90, 180 or more best"}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Not checked against specific BIOBUZZ rules."},"refs":[{"title":"Timing Belt - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/power-transmission/belt.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"cad","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/power-transmission/belt.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/8417-dt.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), belt-driven drivetrain CAD by FTC 8417 Lectric Legends, Rover Ruckus"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"chain-driven-drivetrain","name":"Chain-driven drivetrain","aliases":["chain drive","#25 chain drive","roller chain drivetrain","sprocket drive"],"category":"drive","tags":["power-transmission"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"power-transmission","type":"drive-wheel","short":"Motors drive the wheels through roller chain and sprockets; chain is strong and can be cut to any length.","how":"A sprocket on the motor pulls a chain that turns sprockets on the wheel axles, often one or two chain runs per side. The tooth ratio sets the gear ratio. A tensioner keeps the chain from going slack as it stretches.","uses":["tank and 6WD drivetrains","linking wheels on one side"],"pros":["very strong (#25 chain far exceeds FTC loads)","length easy to adjust with a chain breaker"],"cons":["stretches and needs tensioning","large sprockets take space","heavier and noisier than belts"],"failures":["chain derailing or skipping when slack","master links loosening and falling off","over-tension burning out motors"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"wheel rotation"},"params":[{"key":"ratio","label":"Sprocket ratio","unit":"ratio","min":0.5,"max":3,"default":1,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Not checked against specific BIOBUZZ rules."},"refs":[{"title":"Roller Chain - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/power-transmission/chain.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/power-transmission/chain.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/724-dt.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo of FTC 724 Rednek Robotics Wun drivetrain, Relic Recovery"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"direct-drive-drivetrain","name":"Direct-drive drivetrain","aliases":["direct drive","motor on wheel","direct-driven wheels"],"category":"drive","tags":["power-transmission","beginner"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"power-transmission","type":"drive-wheel","short":"Each wheel is mounted straight on its motor's output shaft, the simplest way to power a wheel.","how":"The wheel hub clamps to the gearmotor shaft, so wheel speed equals gearbox output speed. Gear ratio can only be changed by swapping gearboxes. Supporting the shaft with a bearing on the outside (or inside) keeps side loads off the gearbox.","uses":["beginner and kit chassis","X-drive corners"],"pros":["simplest and most compact"],"cons":["side impacts go straight into the gearbox","no extra gearing","faster gearbox wear"],"failures":["bent motor shafts on heavy robots","gearbox damage from hits on walls or robots","unsupported shafts cantilevering"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"wheel rotation"},"params":[{"key":"supported","label":"Outer shaft support","unit":"on/off","min":0,"max":1,"default":1,"estimate":true,"note":"Game Manual 0 does not recommend unsupported direct drive"}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Not checked against specific BIOBUZZ rules."},"refs":[{"title":"Direct Drive - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/power-transmission/direct-drive.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/power-transmission/direct-drive.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/outside-supported-direct-drive.jpg","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), outside-supported direct drive by FTC 11316 Weapons of Mass Construction"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1},
{"id":"suspended-drive-module","name":"Independently suspended drive module","aliases":["suspension","rocker suspension","sprung drive pod","articulating module"],"category":"drive","tags":["suspension","terrain"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"suspension","type":"drive-wheel","short":"A wheel or wheel pod that pivots or slides on a spring so it stays on the floor when the field is uneven.","how":"The wheel module is hinged (rocker) or slides vertically, and a spring or the robot's weight presses it down. This keeps a wheel, such as an H-drive strafer wheel, in contact even when the other wheels hold the frame at a different height. The drive kinematics are unchanged; only contact improves.","uses":["H-drive strafer wheels","drivetrains on uneven fields"],"pros":["keeps traction on uneven ground"],"cons":["extra pivots, springs and weight","can make the robot wobble"],"failures":["spring too weak so the wheel lifts","spring too strong so other wheels unload"],"dof":1,"actuation":"spring","motion":{"input":"floor height change","output":"wheel pivot or vertical travel"},"params":[{"key":"travel","label":"Suspension travel","unit":"in","min":0.1,"max":1,"default":0.25,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Not checked against specific BIOBUZZ rules."},"refs":[{"title":"Holonomic Drivetrains - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/drivetrains/holonomic.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"rocker-bogie","name":"Rocker-bogie suspension","aliases":["rocker bogie","Mars rover suspension","6-wheel rover","Articulated mobile chassis","rocker-bogie","articulated chassis","rover suspension","passive articulated suspension"],"category":"drive","tags":["suspension","terrain","non-ftc"],"entry":"architecture","family":"suspension","type":"drive-wheel","short":"A six-wheel rover suspension, used on NASA Mars rovers, that keeps all wheels on rough ground with no springs.","how":"Each side has a large rocker linkage with a wheel at one end and a smaller bogie with two wheels at the other. A differential links the two rockers so they move in opposite directions and the body sits at their average pitch. All six wheels stay in contact on uneven terrain.","uses":["planetary rovers","outside-FTC terrain robots"],"pros":["climbs obstacles taller than a wheel radius","no springs needed"],"cons":["slow and complex","not designed for flat, fast FTC play"],"failures":["tipping if the center of mass is high"],"dof":3,"actuation":"passive","motion":{"input":"terrain height","output":"rocker and bogie pivots keeping wheels down"},"params":[{"key":"wheelDiameter","label":"Wheel diameter","unit":"mm","min":50,"max":150,"default":80,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["non-ftc"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"A non-FTC design; never assume FTC allows it."},"refs":[{"title":"Rocker-bogie - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rocker-bogie","kind":"engineering"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"high","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":6},
{"id":"two-dead-wheel-odometry","name":"Two-dead-wheel odometry (with IMU heading)","aliases":["2-wheel odometry","two wheel tracking","TwoDeadWheelLocalizer","2 pod odometry","dead wheel odometry"],"category":"drive","tags":["localization","autonomous"],"entry":"software","family":"odometry","type":null,"short":"Two unpowered tracking wheels, one forward and one sideways, measure travel while the IMU supplies heading, giving the robot's field position.","how":"A parallel pod measures forward travel and a perpendicular pod measures sideways travel; the gyro gives heading. Each loop the pod deltas are corrected for the heading change times each pod's offset from the turning center, rotated into field coordinates and added to the pose. Libraries use pose exponentials to handle curved motion between updates.","uses":["autonomous path following","field-centric aiming"],"pros":["cheaper than three pods","good accuracy"],"cons":["drifts more than three pods when heading comes from a slow I2C IMU","needs pod offsets measured"],"failures":["pod lifting or slipping","IMU yaw drift","encoder direction reversed"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"parallelOffset","label":"Parallel pod sideways offset","unit":"in","min":-8,"max":8,"default":0,"estimate":true},{"key":"perpOffset","label":"Perpendicular pod forward offset","unit":"in","min":-8,"max":8,"default":0,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R303"],"note":"A single-DoF COTS part under R303 (not one of its listed exceptions); legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Dead Wheels - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/dead-wheels.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Dead Wheels - Learn Road Runner","url":"https://learnroadrunner.com/dead-wheels.html","kind":"team"},{"title":"Road Runner v1.0 Tuning","url":"https://rr.brott.dev/docs/v1-0/tuning/","kind":"engineering"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["2 dead-wheel encoders","IMU"]},
{"id":"three-dead-wheel-odometry","name":"Three-dead-wheel odometry","aliases":["3-wheel odometry","three wheel tracking","ThreeDeadWheelLocalizer","3 pod odometry"],"category":"drive","tags":["localization","autonomous"],"entry":"software","family":"odometry","type":null,"short":"Two forward-facing tracking wheels and one sideways wheel measure the robot's motion; heading comes from the difference between the two forward wheels.","how":"Heading change is (dxL - dxR)/L, where L is the lateral distance between the parallel pods; forward travel is (dxL + dxR)/2; sideways travel is dxPerp minus forward offset times the heading change. These are rotated by the heading into field X/Y. No IMU is needed. (gm0 sign convention; with counter-clockwise-positive heading and forward-positive encoders the heading change is (dxR - dxL)/L.)","uses":["accurate autonomous paths"],"pros":["generally the most accurate dead-wheel setup","no IMU latency"],"cons":["three pods cost more and take space","lateral distance must be tuned carefully"],"failures":["wrong lateral distance makes turns over- or under-count","one pod slipping corrupts heading"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"lateralDistance","label":"Distance between parallel pods","unit":"in","min":6,"max":16,"default":12,"estimate":true},{"key":"forwardOffset","label":"Perpendicular pod forward offset","unit":"in","min":-8,"max":8,"default":0,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R303"],"note":"A single-DoF COTS part under R303 (not one of its listed exceptions); legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Odometry - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/software/concepts/odometry.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Dead Wheels - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/dead-wheels.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"schematic","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/software/concepts/odometry.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/offsets-and-trackwidth.png","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), sensor offsets on FTC 17508 Rising Tau's 2019/20 Skystone robot"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["3 dead-wheel encoders"]},
{"id":"drive-encoder-odometry","name":"Drive-encoder odometry (powered-wheel, differential)","aliases":["drive encoder localization","powered wheel odometry","differential odometry","tank odometry","wheel odometry","dead reckoning"],"category":"drive","tags":["localization","autonomous"],"entry":"software","family":"odometry","type":null,"short":"Uses the encoders already in the drive motors to estimate where the robot is, with no extra pods.","how":"For a tank (differential) drive, forward travel is (dL + dR)/2 and heading change is (dR - dL)/track width, then rotated into field coordinates. It is Road Runner's default localizer. Because powered wheels slip when accelerating and turning, the estimate drifts more than dead wheels.","uses":["simple autonomous routines","teams without odometry pods"],"pros":["no extra hardware","easy to start"],"cons":["wheel slip causes drift","track width must be tuned (effective value differs from measured)"],"failures":["slip under hard acceleration","encoder on a software-decoded port losing counts at high speed"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"trackWidth","label":"Effective track width","unit":"in","min":8,"max":20,"default":14,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Software only; no specific rule checked."},"refs":[{"title":"Odometry - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/software/concepts/odometry.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Road Runner v1.0 Tuning","url":"https://rr.brott.dev/docs/v1-0/tuning/","kind":"engineering"},{"title":"SDK Motors - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/software/adv-control-system/sdk-motors.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["motor encoders"]},
{"id":"mecanum-drive-odometry","name":"Mecanum drive-encoder odometry","aliases":["mecanum odometry","mecanum localizer","MecanumDrive localizer"],"category":"drive","tags":["localization","mecanum"],"entry":"software","family":"odometry","parent":"drive-encoder-odometry","type":null,"short":"Estimates position from the four mecanum wheel encoders by running the mecanum equations backwards; usually helped by the IMU for heading.","how":"Forward kinematics invert the wheel equations: forward = (fl + fr + bl + br)/4, strafe (right-positive, same convention as mecanum-drive) = (fl - fr - bl + br)/4 times a strafe factor, turn from the side difference. Road Runner uses the IMU for heading on mecanum. Strafing slip means the lateral scale is smaller than the forward scale.","uses":["mecanum robots without dead wheels"],"pros":["no extra hardware"],"cons":["roller slip, especially strafing, causes drift"],"failures":["lateral distance badly off after hard strafes","collisions shifting the robot without wheel rotation"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"lateralScale","label":"Lateral inches per tick vs forward","unit":"ratio","min":0.6,"max":1,"default":0.85,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Software only."},"refs":[{"title":"Road Runner v1.0 Tuning","url":"https://rr.brott.dev/docs/v1-0/tuning/","kind":"engineering"},{"title":"Mecanum TeleOp - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/software/tutorials/mecanum-drive.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["4 motor encoders","IMU"]},
{"id":"swerve-module-odometry","name":"Swerve module odometry","aliases":["swerve odometry","module state odometry"],"category":"drive","tags":["localization","swerve"],"entry":"software","family":"odometry","parent":"drive-encoder-odometry","type":null,"short":"Estimates robot motion from each swerve module's wheel distance and steering angle.","how":"Each module reports a wheel distance and steering angle, giving a velocity vector. A least-squares fit of all module vectors gives the robot's forward, sideways and turn motion, which is integrated into a field pose, usually with IMU heading. Traction wheels slip less than mecanum rollers.","uses":["swerve robots"],"pros":["less slip than mecanum encoder odometry"],"cons":["needs accurate steering angles","few FTC-specific libraries"],"failures":["steering angle offset errors bending the path"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Software only."},"refs":[{"title":"FTC swerve is useless? - Chief Delphi","url":"https://www.chiefdelphi.com/t/ftc-swerve-is-useless/521783","kind":"team"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["drive encoders","steering encoders","IMU"]},
{"id":"imu-assisted-odometry","name":"IMU-assisted odometry (gyro heading)","aliases":["IMU heading odometry","gyro-assisted localization"],"category":"drive","tags":["localization","imu"],"entry":"software","family":"odometry","type":null,"short":"Replaces the heading worked out from wheels with the IMU's yaw, so turning slip does not corrupt position.","how":"Wheel or pod encoders give the translation, and the IMU's yaw gives heading directly. The yaw is read each loop and used to rotate travel into field coordinates. IMU yaw drifts slowly, and on REV hubs it is an I2C read that is slower than encoder reads.","uses":["two-dead-wheel setups","mecanum drive-encoder localizers"],"pros":["heading immune to wheel slip"],"cons":["slow I2C reads add latency","yaw drift over a match"],"failures":["wrong hub-orientation setting giving bad yaw","drift accumulating"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Software using the hub's built-in IMU."},"refs":[{"title":"IMU Programming - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/programming_resources/imu/imu.html","kind":"official"},{"title":"Dead Wheels - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/dead-wheels.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["IMU","encoders"]},
{"id":"encoder-imu-fusion","name":"Encoder and IMU fusion (onboard localizer)","aliases":["sensor fusion odometry","Kalman filter odometry","Pinpoint fusion","OctoQuad localizer"],"category":"drive","tags":["localization","fusion"],"entry":"software","family":"odometry","type":null,"short":"Combines odometry pod counts with gyro readings, often on a dedicated board, to estimate pose quickly and without time skew.","how":"A coprocessor reads both pods and an IMU at a high rate (goBILDA quotes about 1500 Hz for Pinpoint, Digital Chicken Labs about 1.92 kHz for OctoQuad MK2) and integrates the pose itself. The robot controller reads the finished pose over I2C in one transaction. Reading all sensors together removes the lag between IMU and encoder samples.","uses":["two-pod odometry with better heading","freeing the hub's encoder ports"],"pros":["high update rate","one I2C read gives the whole pose"],"cons":["another device to wire and configure","driver versions must match the SDK"],"failures":["pods not detected (status LED shows which)","calibrating while the robot moves"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R702","R613"],"note":"R702 examples list OctoQuad FTC Edition among allowed devices; Pinpoint is not named in the rules I read, so check it. Sensors must be powered by the device they plug into (R613)."},"refs":[{"title":"Pinpoint Odometry Computer - goBILDA","url":"https://www.gobilda.com/pinpoint-odometry-computer-imu-sensor-fusion-for-2-wheel-odometry/","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"OctoQuad FTC Edition MK2 - Tindie","url":"https://www.tindie.com/products/digitalchickenlabs/octoquad-ftc-ed-mk2-8x-encoderpwm-imu/","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"Road Runner v1.0 Tuning","url":"https://rr.brott.dev/docs/v1-0/tuning/","kind":"engineering"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["2 dead wheels","IMU (on the coprocessor)"]},
{"id":"gobilda-pinpoint","name":"goBILDA Pinpoint odometry computer","aliases":["Pinpoint","Pinpoint V2","GoBildaPinpointDriver","odometry computer","PinpointLocalizer"],"category":"drive","tags":["localization","sensor","i2c"],"entry":"sensor","family":"odometry","type":null,"short":"A small I2C board that reads two odometry pods and its own IMU and reports the robot's position and heading.","how":"Two pods plug into its JST PH encoder inputs; its IMU measures yaw and its processor fuses them about every 0.65 ms. The Control Hub reads x, y and heading over I2C. FTC SDK 10.3 added Pinpoint support with the SensorGoBildaPinpoint sample, and 11.1 added Pinpoint V2 support.","uses":["two-pod odometry for autonomous","Road Runner PinpointLocalizer"],"pros":["fast fused updates","frees hub encoder ports","status LED shows missing pods"],"cons":["original version discontinued (V2 replaces it)","must be mounted flat and still while calibrating"],"failures":["pod directions reversed","moving during IMU calibration","offsets entered wrong"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"podCountsPerRev","label":"Pod counts per wheel revolution","unit":"counts/rev","min":2000,"max":8192,"default":2000,"estimate":false,"note":"goBILDA swingarm pod: 2000 counts per revolution of its 48 mm wheel (about 13.26 ticks per mm)"}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R702","R613"],"note":"Pinpoint is not named in the BIOBUZZ rules I read; check R702 and the Q&A before relying on it."},"refs":[{"title":"Pinpoint Odometry Computer - goBILDA","url":"https://www.gobilda.com/pinpoint-odometry-computer-imu-sensor-fusion-for-2-wheel-odometry/","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"FtcRobotController README (release notes)","url":"https://github.com/FIRST-Tech-Challenge/FtcRobotController/blob/master/README.md","kind":"official"}],"image":{"kind":"component-photo","page":"https://www.gobilda.com/pinpoint-odometry-computer-imu-sensor-fusion-for-2-wheel-odometry/","src":"https://cdn11.bigcommerce.com/s-x56mtydx1w/images/stencil/2400x2400/products/2262/13383/Pinpoint_FTC_Price__99578__36598.1726607395.jpg?c=1","credit":"goBILDA store listing","shows":"the Pinpoint odometry computer board"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["2 odometry pods"]},
{"id":"sparkfun-otos","name":"SparkFun Optical Tracking Odometry Sensor (OTOS)","aliases":["OTOS","optical odometry","mouse sensor odometry","optical flow odometry","PAA5160E1","OTOSLocalizer"],"category":"drive","tags":["localization","sensor","i2c"],"entry":"sensor","family":"odometry","type":null,"short":"A one-inch board that watches the floor like an optical mouse and fuses it with a built-in IMU to track x, y and heading.","how":"A PixArt PAA5160E1 optical sensor images the floor at up to 20,000 fps to measure sliding motion, and a 6-axis IMU gives rotation; an onboard microcontroller fuses them. It connects over Qwiic I2C (address 0x17). On foam field tiles SparkFun says it must sit 10 mm above the surface. FTC SDK 9.2 added support.","uses":["odometry without dead wheels","Road Runner OTOSLocalizer"],"pros":["tiny, no wheels to wear or slip","onboard fusion"],"cons":["mounting height is critical","needs per-robot scale calibration (3-5% out of the box)"],"failures":["wrong height so tracking fails","dirt or tile seams confusing the optical sensor"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"mountHeight","label":"Height above tile","unit":"mm","min":10,"max":27,"default":10,"estimate":false,"note":"SparkFun: 10 mm on FTC foam tiles"}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R702","R613"],"note":"R702 Example 2 lists the SparkFun OTOS as allowed; the robot as a whole must still pass inspection."},"refs":[{"title":"SparkFun Optical Tracking Odometry Sensor - SparkFun","url":"https://www.sparkfun.com/sparkfun-optical-tracking-odometry-sensor-paa5160e1-qwiic.html","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML)","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"}],"image":{"kind":"component-photo","page":"https://www.sparkfun.com/sparkfun-optical-tracking-odometry-sensor-paa5160e1-qwiic.html","src":"https://www.sparkfun.com/media/catalog/product/cache/f3020b7489dcfc4d1d147cf4dad07b7f/S/E/SEN-24904-Optical-Tracking-Odometry-Sensor-Feature_1.jpg","credit":"SparkFun store listing","shows":"the OTOS sensor board"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"vision-assisted-localization","name":"Vision-assisted localization (AprilTag correction of odometry)","aliases":["AprilTag relocalization","vision pose correction","MegaTag localization","odometry reset from tags"],"category":"drive","tags":["localization","vision","fusion"],"entry":"software","family":"odometry","type":null,"short":"Odometry tracks motion smoothly, and whenever the camera sees a field AprilTag the robot's pose is corrected toward the camera's estimate.","how":"The FTC AprilTag processor computes the robot's field pose from a tag's known field position and the camera's pose on the robot. Software blends or resets the odometry pose with that estimate when the detection is trustworthy (close, steady, fully in view). Limelight 3A does a similar MegaTag estimate on the camera.","uses":["removing drift during long autonomous runs","re-centering before shooting or scoring"],"pros":["bounds odometry drift"],"cons":["tags must be in view and the camera pose measured exactly","vision updates are slower and noisier than odometry"],"failures":["bad camera pose offsets shifting the robot","motion blur giving false poses"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[],"seasons":["general","biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R708"],"note":"Uses USB vision allowed under R708; BIOBUZZ places 36h11 AprilTags on the CELLs (section 9.9)."},"refs":[{"title":"AprilTag Localization - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/apriltag/vision_portal/apriltag_localization/apriltag-localization.html","kind":"official"},{"title":"Limelight 3A Smart Camera - goBILDA","url":"https://www.gobilda.com/limelight-3a-smart-camera/","kind":"vendor"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["camera","odometry"]},
{"id":"dead-wheel-pod","name":"Dead-wheel odometry pod (spring-loaded)","aliases":["dead wheel","odometry pod","tracking wheel","odo pod","spring-loaded pod","swingarm odometry pod","4-bar odometry pod"],"category":"drive","tags":["localization","odometry"],"entry":"component","family":"odometry","type":"odometry-pod","short":"An unpowered omni wheel with an encoder, pressed onto the floor by a spring, that measures how far the robot really moved.","how":"The wheel rolls only when the chassis moves, so it is not fooled by drive wheel slip. A spring or rubber band pivots the pod (or slides it vertically) to keep the wheel on the tile. Its encoder, such as the REV Through Bore (8192 counts/rev) or the goBILDA pod's 2000 counts/rev, plugs into an encoder port or odometry computer.","uses":["two- or three-wheel odometry"],"pros":["far less slip than drive encoders","COTS options from goBILDA and others"],"cons":["takes space under the robot","spring force must be tuned"],"failures":["too little spring so the wheel slips","too much spring so the drivetrain lifts","encoder cable damage"],"dof":1,"actuation":"spring","motion":{"input":"robot travel over the floor","output":"wheel rotation counted by the encoder"},"params":[{"key":"wheelDiameter","label":"Wheel diameter","unit":"mm","min":32,"max":60,"default":48,"estimate":false,"note":"goBILDA swingarm pod"},{"key":"countsPerRev","label":"Encoder counts per rev","unit":"counts","min":2000,"max":8192,"default":2000,"estimate":false,"note":"goBILDA pod 2000; REV Through Bore 8192"}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R303"],"note":"A single-DoF COTS part under R303 (not one of its listed exceptions); legality depends on the whole robot and the current manual."},"refs":[{"title":"Dead Wheels - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/dead-wheels.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Swingarm Odometry Pod (48mm Wheel) - goBILDA","url":"https://www.gobilda.com/swingarm-odometry-pod-48mm-wheel/","kind":"vendor"}],"image":{"kind":"mechanism-photo","page":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/dead-wheels.html","src":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/_images/14320-pivot-half.jpg","credit":"Game Manual 0 (CC BY-SA 4.0), photo of FTC 14320's pivot-tensioned odometry pod"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["quadrature encoder"]},
{"id":"retractable-dead-wheel-pod","name":"Retractable dead-wheel pod","aliases":["retracting odometry","odo retract","lifting dead wheels"],"category":"drive","tags":["localization","odometry"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"odometry","parent":"dead-wheel-pod","type":"odo-retract","preset":{"states":{"down":0,"up":60},"lower":0,"upper":90,"speed":180},"short":"A dead-wheel pod that a servo can lift off the floor when it is not needed, such as during teleop or over obstacles.","how":"The pod's pivot arm is held down by its spring during autonomous; a servo pulls it up so the wheel clears the floor. This protects the pod during defense or climbing and removes its drag. Pose tracking is lost while it is up.","uses":["protecting pods in teleop","clearing barriers or field elements"],"pros":["less wear and fewer pod hits"],"cons":["one more servo","odometry stops while retracted"],"failures":["pod not fully down so it slips","servo fighting the spring and overheating"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation","output":"pod pivot up or down"},"params":[{"key":"liftAngle","label":"Retract angle","unit":"deg","min":20,"max":90,"default":60,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R503"],"note":"Adds a servo toward the R503 limit; not otherwise checked."},"refs":[{"title":"Dead Wheels - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/dead-wheels.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":1},
{"id":"crservo-swerve-steering","name":"Continuous-servo swerve steering","aliases":["CR servo steering","continuous rotation steering","servo-steered swerve","Axon steering"],"category":"drive","tags":["swerve","steering"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"swerve-steering","parent":"swerve-drive","type":"swerve-steer","preset":{"states":{"forward":0,"left":90},"lower":-180,"upper":180,"speed":360},"short":"A continuous-rotation servo turns a swerve module, and a separate angle sensor tells the code where the wheel points.","how":"The CR servo spins the module's steering gear in either direction without limits. An absolute encoder or analog feedback on the steering axis reports the angle, and a PID loop drives the servo until the wheel faces the target. Teams in the Chief Delphi thread used goBILDA CR servos with analog encoders.","uses":["swerve modules on robots short of motor ports"],"pros":["saves motors for other mechanisms","unlimited rotation"],"cons":["servos may be underpowered for steering","needs a separate angle sensor"],"failures":["steering stalls under load","angle sensor slipping on its gear"],"dof":1,"actuation":"crservo","motion":{"input":"CR servo rotation","output":"module heading"},"params":[{"key":"steerRatio","label":"Servo to module ratio","unit":"ratio","min":1,"max":3,"default":1,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R502","R503"],"note":"Servos must meet R502 limits and count toward R503's 8 servos."},"refs":[{"title":"Angle sensor for FTC Swerve wheel - Chief Delphi","url":"https://www.chiefdelphi.com/t/angle-sensor-for-ftc-swerve-wheel/384085","kind":"team"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":1,"sensors":["absolute steering encoder"]},
{"id":"positional-servo-steering","name":"Positional-servo steering","aliases":["standard servo steering","180-degree servo steering","angle servo steering"],"category":"drive","tags":["swerve","steering"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"swerve-steering","parent":"swerve-drive","type":"swerve-steer","preset":{"states":{"forward":0,"left":90},"lower":-90,"upper":90,"speed":300},"short":"A normal position servo sets the steering angle directly, so no extra sensor is needed, but the range is limited.","how":"The servo is commanded to an angle, and its internal feedback holds it. A limited range (about 180 to 300 degrees, or less if geared) means software flips wheel direction instead of rotating past the stop. Multi-turn sail-winch servos extend range but are slow.","uses":["simple swerve or steered-wheel prototypes"],"pros":["no external encoder","simple programming"],"cons":["limited angle range","gearing for range costs torque"],"failures":["servo overshoot or buzzing under load","module hitting the travel limit"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo position","output":"module heading"},"params":[{"key":"servoRange","label":"Servo travel","unit":"deg","min":90,"max":300,"default":180,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R502","R503"],"note":"Servo limits from R502/R503 apply."},"refs":[{"title":"Angle sensor for FTC Swerve wheel - Chief Delphi","url":"https://www.chiefdelphi.com/t/angle-sensor-for-ftc-swerve-wheel/384085","kind":"team"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":1},
{"id":"motor-encoder-steering","name":"Motor-and-encoder steering","aliases":["motor steering","DC motor swerve steering","encoder steering"],"category":"drive","tags":["swerve","steering"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"swerve-steering","parent":"swerve-drive","type":"swerve-steer","preset":{"states":{"forward":0,"left":90},"lower":-180,"upper":180,"speed":720},"short":"A DC gearmotor turns each swerve module, using its encoder (and often an absolute sensor) to hold the steering angle.","how":"The steering motor drives the module through a reduction; its quadrature encoder measures relative angle once zeroed. An absolute encoder or homing step sets zero at startup. This is strong and fast but costs a motor per module.","uses":["high-torque swerve steering"],"pros":["more torque than servos","precise encoder feedback"],"cons":["four steering motors plus four drive motors uses all 8 motors"],"failures":["losing zero after a power cycle without an absolute sensor"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"motor rotation","output":"module heading"},"params":[{"key":"steerRatio","label":"Motor to module ratio","unit":"ratio","min":1,"max":10,"default":4,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R503"],"note":"R503 caps the robot at 8 motors, which limits this approach."},"refs":[{"title":"FTC swerve is useless? - Chief Delphi","url":"https://www.chiefdelphi.com/t/ftc-swerve-is-useless/521783","kind":"team"},{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML)","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","motors":1},
{"id":"absolute-encoder-steering","name":"Absolute-encoder steering feedback","aliases":["absolute encoder module","steering encoder","analog steering encoder","azimuth encoder"],"category":"drive","tags":["swerve","sensor"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"swerve-steering","parent":"swerve-drive","type":"swerve-steer","preset":{"states":{"forward":0,"left":90},"lower":-180,"upper":180,"speed":360},"short":"An absolute angle sensor on the steering axis tells the robot which way each wheel points as soon as it powers on.","how":"An analog absolute encoder (or a PWM one read through an interface such as OctoQuad) is geared 1:1 to the module so one sensor turn equals one module turn. The voltage or pulse width maps to 0-360 degrees with no homing. REV hub analog inputs read 0-3.3 V, so 5 V sensors need a level shifter or 3.3 V mode.","uses":["zeroing swerve modules at startup"],"pros":["no homing routine","angle known after a power cycle"],"cons":["voltage-level and wiring details","offsets must be calibrated per module"],"failures":["5 V output beyond the 3.3 V input range","encoder gear slipping so the offset changes"],"dof":1,"actuation":"none","motion":{"input":"module rotation","output":"absolute angle reading"},"params":[{"key":"offset","label":"Zero offset","unit":"deg","min":0,"max":360,"default":0,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R613","R607"],"note":"Sensors must be powered by the hub they plug into (R613); custom level shifters count as custom circuits (R607)."},"refs":[{"title":"Angle sensor for FTC Swerve wheel - Chief Delphi","url":"https://www.chiefdelphi.com/t/angle-sensor-for-ftc-swerve-wheel/384085","kind":"team"},{"title":"OctoQuad FTC Edition MK2 - Tindie","url":"https://www.tindie.com/products/digitalchickenlabs/octoquad-ftc-ed-mk2-8x-encoderpwm-imu/","kind":"vendor"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["absolute encoder"]},
{"id":"steering-reduction","name":"Gear- or belt-reduced steering","aliases":["gear-reduced steering","belt-reduced steering","steering gearbox","steering reduction"],"category":"drive","tags":["swerve","power-transmission"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"swerve-steering","parent":"swerve-drive","type":"swerve-steer","preset":{"states":{"forward":0,"left":90},"lower":-180,"upper":180,"speed":360},"short":"Gears or a timing belt between the steering actuator and the module trade speed for torque so steering holds under load.","how":"A small gear or pulley on the actuator drives a larger one on the module, multiplying torque by the ratio and dividing speed. Gears are compact and stiff; belts allow the actuator to sit farther away and run quietly. The steering sensor should be on the module side (or geared 1:1) so backlash does not hide the true angle.","uses":["swerve steering with servos or motors"],"pros":["more holding torque","lets the actuator be placed where it fits"],"cons":["reduces steering speed","backlash (gears) or stretch (belts)"],"failures":["belt skipping","gear backlash causing wobble"],"dof":1,"actuation":"varies","motion":{"input":"actuator rotation","output":"slower, stronger module rotation"},"params":[{"key":"ratio","label":"Reduction ratio","unit":"ratio","min":1,"max":5,"default":2,"estimate":true},{"key":"style","label":"Gear (0) or belt (1)","unit":"choice number","min":0,"max":1,"default":0,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Not checked against specific BIOBUZZ rules."},"refs":[{"title":"Angle sensor for FTC Swerve wheel - Chief Delphi","url":"https://www.chiefdelphi.com/t/angle-sensor-for-ftc-swerve-wheel/384085","kind":"team"},{"title":"Timing Belt - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/common-mechanisms/power-transmission/belt.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"incremental-encoder","name":"Incremental (quadrature) encoder","aliases":["quadrature encoder","motor encoder","relative encoder","built-in motor encoder","REV Through Bore Encoder","ticks","CPR"],"category":"sense","tags":["position","encoder"],"entry":"sensor","family":"encoder","type":null,"short":"Counts ticks as a shaft turns so the robot knows how far a motor, wheel or arm has moved since it powered on.","how":"Two offset signals (A and B) pulse as the shaft turns; their order gives direction. Counts per revolution times the gear ratio converts ticks to turns, angle or distance. Read with motor.getCurrentPosition(); reset with STOP_AND_RESET_ENCODER. On REV hubs, ports 0 and 3 are hardware-decoded and ports 1 and 2 can lose steps with encoders above about 4000 CPR.","uses":["drive distance","arm and slide positions","dead wheels"],"pros":["built into FTC motors","high resolution"],"cons":["position resets at power-up","needs homing for absolute positions"],"failures":["lost counts on software-decoded ports","reversed direction","velocity overflow on 8192 CPR encoders at high speed"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"cpr","label":"Counts per revolution","unit":"counts","min":28,"max":8192,"default":537.7,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R609","R613"],"note":"Encoder wiring is signal-level (R609) and encoders must be powered by the hub they connect to (R613)."},"refs":[{"title":"Encoders - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/software/tutorials/encoders.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"SDK Motors - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/software/adv-control-system/sdk-motors.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Through Bore Encoder - REV Robotics","url":"https://www.revrobotics.com/rev-11-1271/","kind":"vendor"}],"image":{"kind":"component-photo","page":"https://www.revrobotics.com/rev-11-1271/","src":"https://cdn11.bigcommerce.com/s-t3eo8vwp22/images/stencil/1500x1500/products/404/1487/Through_Bore_Encoder_Both_Shots_Sml__30025.1574098624.png?c=2","credit":"REV Robotics store listing","shows":"REV Through Bore Encoder"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"absolute-rotary-encoder","name":"Absolute rotary encoder","aliases":["absolute encoder","analog encoder","MA3","PWM encoder","Through Bore absolute output"],"category":"sense","tags":["position","encoder"],"entry":"sensor","family":"encoder","type":null,"short":"Reports the true shaft angle at any moment, even right after power-up, so no homing is needed.","how":"The sensor outputs an analog voltage or a PWM pulse width proportional to angle (0-360 degrees). Analog types plug into a hub analog port (0-3.3 V on REV hubs). The REV Through Bore's absolute output is PWM, which gm0 notes the hub cannot read directly; interfaces like OctoQuad can read it.","uses":["swerve steering","arm angle at startup","turrets"],"pros":["no homing","survives power cycles"],"cons":["voltage and wiring compatibility","usually lower resolution than quadrature"],"failures":["5 V sensor output exceeding the 3.3 V input","wraparound at 360 not handled in code"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"zeroOffset","label":"Zero offset","unit":"deg","min":0,"max":360,"default":0,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R613"],"note":"Sensors must be powered by the hub or device they connect to (R613); level shifters may be custom circuits."},"refs":[{"title":"Sensor Glossary - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/power-and-electronics/sensor-glossary.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Angle sensor for FTC Swerve wheel - Chief Delphi","url":"https://www.chiefdelphi.com/t/angle-sensor-for-ftc-swerve-wheel/384085","kind":"team"},{"title":"OctoQuad FTC Edition MK2 - Tindie","url":"https://www.tindie.com/products/digitalchickenlabs/octoquad-ftc-ed-mk2-8x-encoderpwm-imu/","kind":"vendor"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"magnetic-rotary-encoder","name":"Magnetic rotary encoder","aliases":["magnetic absolute encoder","hall rotary encoder","AS5600","diametric magnet encoder","Thrifty encoder"],"category":"sense","tags":["position","encoder"],"entry":"sensor","family":"encoder","parent":"absolute-rotary-encoder","type":null,"short":"A chip reads the direction of a small magnet on the end of a shaft to report its absolute angle without touching it.","how":"A diametrically polarized magnet is fixed to the shaft end; a hall-sensor chip facing it measures the field direction and outputs angle as analog voltage or PWM. Because there is no contact there is no wear. Spacing and centering of the magnet affect accuracy.","uses":["swerve steering","arm and turret angle"],"pros":["contactless, no wear","absolute angle"],"cons":["magnet must be centered and close","many boards default to 5 V or PWM output"],"failures":["magnet loosening or off-center","nearby steel or motors disturbing the field"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"magnetGap","label":"Magnet to chip gap","unit":"mm","min":0.5,"max":3,"default":1.5,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R613"],"note":"Power and voltage-level rules apply; specific boards not checked."},"refs":[{"title":"Thrifty Absolute Magnetic Encoder - The Thrifty Bot","url":"https://www.thethriftybot.com/products/thrifty-absolute-magnetic-encoder","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"Angle sensor for FTC Swerve wheel - Chief Delphi","url":"https://www.chiefdelphi.com/t/angle-sensor-for-ftc-swerve-wheel/384085","kind":"team"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"potentiometer","name":"Potentiometer (rotary angle sensor)","aliases":["pot","REV Potentiometer","analog angle sensor","REV-31-1155"],"category":"sense","tags":["position","analog"],"entry":"sensor","family":"potentiometer","type":null,"short":"A variable resistor on a shaft that outputs a voltage proportional to angle, giving absolute position over a limited range.","how":"Turning the shaft moves a wiper along a resistive track, changing the output voltage. The REV Potentiometer is 10 kOhm, linear, 270 degrees of travel, with a 5 mm hex input, and connects to an analog port (n channel only, so ports 0 or 2). Code maps voltage to angle.","uses":["arm angle","wrist or turret position with limited travel"],"pros":["cheap and simple","absolute position"],"cons":["limited travel; forcing past the stop damages it","noisy compared with encoders"],"failures":["shaft over-rotated and broken","two pots on one splitter (not possible)"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"range","label":"Electrical range","unit":"deg","min":180,"max":340,"default":270,"estimate":false,"note":"REV Potentiometer"}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R613"],"note":"Must be powered by the hub port it plugs into (R613); whole-robot legality not implied."},"refs":[{"title":"Potentiometer - REV Robotics","url":"https://www.revrobotics.com/rev-31-1155/","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"Potentiometer Specifications - REV Docs","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/rev-crossover-products/sensors/potentiometer/specifications","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"Sensors - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/control_hard_compon/rc_components/sensors/sensors.html","kind":"official"}],"image":{"kind":"component-photo","page":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/control_hard_compon/rc_components/sensors/sensors.html","src":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/_images/REV-31-1155.png","credit":"FIRST Tech Challenge Docs","shows":"REV Potentiometer (REV-31-1155)"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"magnetic-limit-switch","name":"Magnetic limit switch (Hall-effect sensor)","aliases":["hall effect sensor","hall-effect switch","magnetic limit switch","REV Magnetic Limit Switch","REV-31-1462","magnet sensor"],"category":"sense","tags":["limit-switch","digital","homing"],"entry":"sensor","family":"limit-switch","type":null,"short":"A digital switch that turns on when a magnet comes close, used to detect end-of-travel without touching anything.","how":"Hall-effect elements sense a nearby magnet and pull the digital line low (active-low). The REV switch is omnipolar, triggers at about 10 mm from the top or 5 mm from the side with its magnet, and has about 5 mm hysteresis. Mount the magnet on the moving part and the switch on the frame.","uses":["slide or arm home position","counting turret or spindexer positions"],"pros":["no contact or wear","works at a small distance"],"cons":["only on/off","hysteresis means it releases a few mm later"],"failures":["magnet too far away","switch wired from 3.3 V instead of to ground on a custom sensor"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"triggerDistance","label":"Trigger distance","unit":"mm","min":2,"max":15,"default":10,"estimate":false,"note":"REV, top of sensor, included magnet"}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R613"],"note":"Powered from the hub's digital port as R613 requires; whole-robot legality not implied."},"refs":[{"title":"Magnetic Limit Switch - REV Robotics","url":"https://www.revrobotics.com/rev-31-1462/","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"Magnetic Limit Switch Specs - REV Docs","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/rev-crossover-products/sensors/magnetic-limit-switch/specs","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"Sensors - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/control_hard_compon/rc_components/sensors/sensors.html","kind":"official"}],"image":{"kind":"component-photo","page":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/control_hard_compon/rc_components/sensors/sensors.html","src":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/_images/REV-31-1462.png","credit":"FIRST Tech Challenge Docs","shows":"REV Magnetic Limit Switch (REV-31-1462)"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"touch-limit-switch","name":"Touch sensor or mechanical limit switch","aliases":["touch sensor","limit switch","bump switch","REV Touch Sensor","REV-31-1425","endstop","microswitch"],"category":"sense","tags":["limit-switch","digital","homing"],"entry":"sensor","family":"limit-switch","type":null,"short":"A push button that tells the robot when something is pressed against it, often used as an end-of-travel stop.","how":"Pressing the button pulls the digital line from high to low. The REV Touch Sensor uses the n+1 channel of a digital port; the SDK reads TRUE when not pressed. Generic switches should be wired between ground and the digital pin, not from 3.3 V, according to gm0.","uses":["slide and arm homing","detecting contact with a wall or game element","driver input"],"pros":["very cheap and simple","certain physical contact"],"cons":["needs physical contact","can be crushed if the mechanism over-travels"],"failures":["switch broken by impact","wired to the wrong channel of a splitter"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R613"],"note":"Powered from the hub port per R613; whole-robot legality not implied."},"refs":[{"title":"Touch Sensor - REV Robotics","url":"https://www.revrobotics.com/rev-31-1425/","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"SDK Sensors - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/software/adv-control-system/sdk-sensors.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Sensors - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/control_hard_compon/rc_components/sensors/sensors.html","kind":"official"}],"image":{"kind":"component-photo","page":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/control_hard_compon/rc_components/sensors/sensors.html","src":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/_images/REV-31-1425.png","credit":"FIRST Tech Challenge Docs","shows":"REV Touch Sensor (REV-31-1425)"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"linear-position-sensor","name":"Linear position sensor (slide potentiometer)","aliases":["linear potentiometer","slide pot","string potentiometer","linear encoder"],"category":"sense","tags":["position","analog"],"entry":"sensor","family":"potentiometer","type":null,"short":"Measures how far something has slid in a straight line, giving an absolute extension reading.","how":"A wiper moving along a straight resistive track (or a cable pulled from a spring-loaded spool) changes an analog voltage in proportion to position. It plugs into an analog port and code maps voltage to inches. Most FTC teams use motor encoders on the slide spool instead.","uses":["absolute slide extension"],"pros":["absolute position, no homing"],"cons":["limited stroke and bulky","few FTC-specific products"],"failures":["wiper wear causing jumpy readings"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"stroke","label":"Stroke length","unit":"mm","min":30,"max":300,"default":100,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R613"],"note":"Power rules apply; specific parts not checked."},"refs":[{"title":"Sensor Glossary - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/power-and-electronics/sensor-glossary.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"imu","name":"IMU (inertial measurement unit)","aliases":["gyro","gyroscope","accelerometer","BHI260AP","BNO055","Control Hub IMU","navX2-Micro","9-axis IMU"],"category":"sense","tags":["heading","orientation","i2c"],"entry":"sensor","family":"imu","type":null,"short":"A chip that measures rotation and acceleration, giving the robot's heading, pitch and roll.","how":"Gyros measure turn rate and accelerometers measure gravity; the chip fuses them into an orientation. Control Hubs since September 2022 use a Bosch BHI260AP, older ones a BNO055; the SDK 8.1+ IMU interface works with both. Tell it how the hub is mounted with RevHubOrientationOnRobot, then read getRobotYawPitchRollAngles().","uses":["heading for field-centric drive and turns","odometry heading","tilt detection"],"pros":["built into the Control Hub","no wheels to slip"],"cons":["yaw drifts slowly","I2C reads are slower than encoder reads"],"failures":["wrong mounting orientation giving wrong axes","resetting yaw while the robot is tilted"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"logoDirection","label":"Hub logo direction","unit":"choice number","min":0,"max":5,"default":0,"estimate":true},{"key":"usbDirection","label":"Hub USB direction","unit":"choice number","min":0,"max":5,"default":2,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R702","R613"],"note":"R702 examples list the Adafruit BNO055 as an allowed sensor; the hub's built-in IMU is part of the control system."},"refs":[{"title":"IMU Programming - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/programming_resources/imu/imu.html","kind":"official"},{"title":"Sensors - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/control_hard_compon/rc_components/sensors/sensors.html","kind":"official"}],"image":{"kind":"component-photo","page":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/control_hard_compon/rc_components/sensors/sensors.html","src":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/_images/BNO055.jpg","credit":"FIRST Tech Challenge Docs","shows":"BNO055 IMU board"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"gyro-heading","name":"Gyro heading control (field-centric and heading hold)","aliases":["field centric","field-oriented drive","heading hold","gyro turn","turn to angle"],"category":"sense","tags":["heading","software","drive"],"entry":"software","family":"imu","type":null,"short":"Uses the IMU's yaw to drive relative to the field, turn to exact angles, or keep the robot pointed straight.","how":"Field-centric drive rotates the joystick vector by minus the IMU yaw before applying the mecanum equations, so pushing up always means away from the driver. Turn-to-angle and heading hold run a P or PID loop on the yaw error. A button to reset yaw counters drift.","uses":["teleop field-centric driving","precise autonomous turns","straight strafing"],"pros":["easier driving","accurate turns"],"cons":["depends on correct yaw and its reset"],"failures":["yaw reset at the wrong time","angle wrap at +/-180 not handled"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"kP","label":"Heading gain","unit":"power/deg","min":0.005,"max":0.05,"default":0.02,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Software only."},"refs":[{"title":"Mecanum TeleOp - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/software/tutorials/mecanum-drive.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"IMU Programming - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/programming_resources/imu/imu.html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["IMU"]},
{"id":"accelerometer-tilt","name":"Tilt detection from IMU pitch and roll","aliases":["anti-tip","tip detection","accelerometer orientation","pitch roll check"],"category":"sense","tags":["orientation","software","safety"],"entry":"software","family":"imu","type":null,"short":"Watches the IMU's pitch and roll so the robot can notice it is tipping or climbing and react.","how":"Gravity seen by the accelerometer sets pitch and roll, which (unlike yaw) do not drift. Code compares getRobotYawPitchRollAngles() pitch and roll against thresholds and, for example, slows the drive or lowers a lift when the robot leans too far.","uses":["anti-tip with tall lifts","detecting climbs or ramps (past games; BIOBUZZ has no hang, G415)"],"pros":["pitch and roll are stable over time"],"cons":["needs correct hub orientation","vibration causes false triggers"],"failures":["threshold too low, triggering on bumps"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"tipThreshold","label":"Tip threshold","unit":"deg","min":5,"max":30,"default":12,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Software only."},"refs":[{"title":"IMU Programming - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/programming_resources/imu/imu.html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["IMU"]},
{"id":"color-sensor","name":"Color sensor","aliases":["REV Color Sensor V3","REV-31-1557","RGB sensor","APDS-9151","color/distance sensor"],"category":"sense","tags":["object-detection","i2c","sorting"],"entry":"sensor","family":"color-sensor","type":null,"short":"Shines light on a nearby object and reads its red, green and blue levels so the robot can tell what color it is.","how":"The REV Color Sensor V3 uses a Broadcom APDS-9151 with a white LED; it returns red, green, blue, alpha and an IR proximity value over I2C (address 0x52). Proximity range is 1 to 10 cm, and color readings are best when the object is close. Code compares ratios or converts to HSV to decide color.","uses":["sorting game pieces by color","confirming a piece is held","line detection"],"pros":["cheap and simple","also gives proximity"],"cons":["only works at short range","ambient light and distance change readings"],"failures":["thresholds tuned under different lighting","sensor too far from the piece"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"sensingDistance","label":"Distance to target","unit":"mm","min":5,"max":100,"default":20,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R613"],"note":"An I2C sensor powered from the hub (R613); whole-robot legality not implied."},"refs":[{"title":"Color Sensor V3 - REV Robotics","url":"https://www.revrobotics.com/rev-31-1557/","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"Color Sensor Specs - REV Docs","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/rev-crossover-products/sensors/color-sensor/specs","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"Sensors - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/control_hard_compon/rc_components/sensors/sensors.html","kind":"official"}],"image":{"kind":"component-photo","page":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/control_hard_compon/rc_components/sensors/sensors.html","src":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/_images/REV-31-1557.png","credit":"FIRST Tech Challenge Docs","shows":"REV Color Sensor (REV-31-1557)"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"distance-sensor","name":"Time-of-flight distance sensor","aliases":["distance sensor","ToF","time of flight","REV 2m Distance Sensor","REV-31-1505","VL53L0X","laser distance sensor","lidar"],"category":"sense","tags":["object-detection","i2c","alignment"],"entry":"sensor","family":"distance-sensor","type":null,"short":"Times how long an invisible infrared laser pulse takes to bounce back to measure distance up to about 2 meters.","how":"The REV 2m Distance Sensor uses an ST VL53L0X time-of-flight chip with a 940 nm Class 1 IR laser, 5 to 200 cm range, 1 mm resolution and a 25 degree field of view, over I2C. Because it measures time rather than brightness, target color matters less, but transparent panels can reduce accuracy.","uses":["distance to walls for alignment","detecting game pieces in an intake","measuring lift height"],"pros":["longer range than a color sensor","fairly insensitive to target reflectivity"],"cons":["wide cone can see the wrong object","clear polycarbonate walls read poorly"],"failures":["reading the floor or field wall instead of the target","bad first reading (fixed in SDK 10.3)"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"maxRange","label":"Max range used","unit":"cm","min":5,"max":200,"default":120,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R710","R613"],"note":"R710 allows lasers only inside a sensor, Class I or exempt, and non-visible; REV lists this sensor as 940 nm Class 1."},"refs":[{"title":"2m Distance Sensor - REV Robotics","url":"https://www.revrobotics.com/rev-31-1505/","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"2m Distance Sensor Specs - REV Docs","url":"https://docs.revrobotics.com/rev-crossover-products/sensors/2m-distance/specs","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"Sensors - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/control_hard_compon/rc_components/sensors/sensors.html","kind":"official"}],"image":{"kind":"component-photo","page":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/control_hard_compon/rc_components/sensors/sensors.html","src":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/_images/REV-31-1505.png","credit":"FIRST Tech Challenge Docs","shows":"REV 2m Distance Sensor (REV-31-1505)"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"ultrasonic-distance-sensor","name":"Ultrasonic distance sensor","aliases":["ultrasonic","sonar","MaxBotix MB1242","range finder"],"category":"sense","tags":["object-detection","i2c"],"entry":"sensor","family":"distance-sensor","parent":"distance-sensor","type":null,"short":"Sends out a sound pulse and times the echo to measure distance to walls or large objects.","how":"The sensor emits an ultrasonic chirp and measures the round-trip time of the echo, converting it to distance. The MaxBotix MB1242 listed in FTC Docs is an I2C device. Its wide beam suits walls more than small pieces.","uses":["distance to field walls"],"pros":["works on clear surfaces that confuse optical sensors"],"cons":["wide beam","slower update rate than ToF"],"failures":["echoes from other objects","soft or angled surfaces absorbing the sound"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R613"],"note":"Power rules apply; not otherwise checked."},"refs":[{"title":"Sensors - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/control_hard_compon/rc_components/sensors/sensors.html","kind":"official"}],"image":{"kind":"component-photo","page":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/control_hard_compon/rc_components/sensors/sensors.html","src":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/_images/MB1242.jpg","credit":"FIRST Tech Challenge Docs","shows":"MaxBotix MB1242 ultrasonic sensor"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"optical-proximity-sensor","name":"Optical proximity sensor (reflective IR)","aliases":["proximity sensor","reflective optical sensor","IR proximity","color sensor proximity","object near sensor"],"category":"sense","tags":["object-detection"],"entry":"sensor","family":"color-sensor","parent":"color-sensor","type":null,"short":"Shines infrared light and measures how much reflects back to tell whether something is very close.","how":"An IR emitter lights the area and a detector reads the reflection; closer or brighter objects return more light. The REV Color Sensor V3's proximity channel works this way over 1 to 10 cm. Because the reading depends on surface color and shine, it is best as a present/not-present check.","uses":["detecting a piece in an intake or claw"],"pros":["fast and cheap","no contact"],"cons":["reading depends on material and color","very short range"],"failures":["dark or shiny pieces reading as far away"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"threshold","label":"Presence threshold distance","unit":"mm","min":5,"max":100,"default":30,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R613"],"note":"Power rules apply."},"refs":[{"title":"Color Sensor V3 - REV Robotics","url":"https://www.revrobotics.com/rev-31-1557/","kind":"vendor"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"beam-break-sensor","name":"Beam-break sensor","aliases":["break beam","IR break beam","photogate","light gate","through-beam sensor"],"category":"sense","tags":["object-detection","digital","intake"],"entry":"sensor","family":"beam-break","type":null,"short":"An infrared emitter and receiver face each other across a gap; when a game piece blocks the beam, the robot knows it is there.","how":"The emitter shines IR at the receiver, whose open-collector output switches when the beam is blocked. It reads as a digital input with a pull-up (REV digital inputs have one). The Adafruit 3 mm version works to about 25 cm and runs on 3.3 to 5.5 V.","uses":["counting pieces in an intake or hopper","confirming a piece reached the shooter"],"pros":["very reliable on/off detection","independent of piece color"],"cons":["needs both sides of a gap","alignment matters"],"failures":["emitter and receiver knocked out of line","bright light flooding the receiver"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"gap","label":"Beam gap","unit":"mm","min":10,"max":250,"default":100,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R613","R607"],"note":"Sensors must be powered by the hub port (R613); not checked whether a given module counts as a custom circuit."},"refs":[{"title":"IR Break Beam Sensors - Adafruit","url":"https://www.adafruit.com/product/2167","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"SDK Sensors - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/software/adv-control-system/sdk-sensors.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":{"kind":"component-photo","page":"https://www.adafruit.com/product/2167","src":"https://cdn-shop.adafruit.com/970x728/2167-05.jpg","credit":"Adafruit store listing","shows":"IR break beam emitter and receiver pair"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"contact-bumper","name":"Contact bumper (switch-backed bumper)","aliases":["bump sensor","bumper switch","wall contact sensor"],"category":"sense","tags":["alignment","digital"],"entry":"component","family":"limit-switch","parent":"touch-limit-switch","type":null,"short":"A bumper plate backed by touch switches tells the robot when it has driven into a wall or field element.","how":"A lightly sprung plate or flap sits in front of one or more touch or limit switches. When the robot touches a wall the plate moves and closes the switch, and code can stop or square up (two switches show if both sides are touching).","uses":["squaring against a wall","stopping at a field element"],"pros":["simple and reliable"],"cons":["must physically hit something"],"failures":["switch crushed by a hard hit","plate stuck pressed"],"dof":0,"actuation":"passive","params":[{"key":"switches","label":"Switch count","unit":"count","min":1,"max":2,"default":2,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Not checked; bumpers have their own robot rules in some seasons."},"refs":[{"title":"Touch Sensor - REV Robotics","url":"https://www.revrobotics.com/rev-31-1425/","kind":"vendor"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["touch sensors"],"motion":{"input":"the robot pressing the plate against a wall","output":"the switch behind it closes"}},
{"id":"intake-presence-detector","name":"Intake object-presence detector","aliases":["piece detector","have-piece sensor","intake sensor","ball counter"],"category":"sense","tags":["intake","object-detection","automation"],"entry":"concept","family":"presence-detection","type":null,"short":"A sensor placed in the intake path that tells the code a game piece is held, so it can stop the intake or start a transfer.","how":"A distance, color, proximity or beam-break sensor watches the spot where a held piece sits. Code debounces the reading (requires it for a few loops) and then changes state: stop rollers, close a gate, or start scoring. Color sensors can also reject the wrong-colored piece.","uses":["auto-stop intakes","counting pieces","rejecting wrong colors"],"pros":["faster, more consistent cycles"],"cons":["false triggers from robot parts or partial pieces"],"failures":["sensor bumped out of position","threshold set for one lighting condition"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"debounceMs","label":"Debounce time","unit":"ms","min":0,"max":200,"default":50,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Depends on the sensor chosen."},"refs":[{"title":"Sensors - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/control_hard_compon/rc_components/sensors/sensors.html","kind":"official"},{"title":"2m Distance Sensor - REV Robotics","url":"https://www.revrobotics.com/rev-31-1505/","kind":"vendor"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["distance, color or beam-break sensor"]},
{"id":"uvc-webcam","name":"USB webcam (UVC)","aliases":["webcam","Logitech C270","Logitech C920","Logitech C310","Arducam global shutter","Webcam 1","camera"],"category":"sense","tags":["vision","camera","usb"],"entry":"sensor","family":"camera","type":null,"short":"A standard USB webcam plugged into the Control Hub that feeds images to the FTC VisionPortal for AprilTags and color processing.","how":"The SDK works with UVC webcams configured as a webcam device. VisionPortal handles resolution, previews and camera controls (exposure, gain, focus). Pose estimates need lens calibration for the exact camera and resolution; built-in calibrations cover only some cameras. Global-shutter cameras reduce motion blur.","uses":["AprilTag detection","color locating","driver camera stream"],"pros":["cheap and well supported","works with all FTC vision samples"],"cons":["USB bandwidth and CPU load","calibration needed for pose"],"failures":["motion blur at speed","USB cable disconnects","no calibration for the chosen resolution"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"resolution","label":"Resolution width","unit":"px","min":320,"max":1920,"default":640,"estimate":true},{"key":"fov","label":"Horizontal field of view","unit":"deg","min":50,"max":90,"default":60,"estimate":false,"note":"Logitech C270 per FTC Docs; C920 is 78"}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R707","R708"],"note":"R707/R708 allow single-sensor UVC webcams (example Logitech C270) using only the UVC stream; stereoscopic cameras are not allowed."},"refs":[{"title":"Webcams for Vision Portal - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/apriltag/vision_portal/visionportal_webcams/visionportal-webcams.html","kind":"official"},{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML)","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"}],"image":{"kind":"component-photo","page":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/apriltag/vision_portal/visionportal_webcams/visionportal-webcams.html","src":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/_images/020-C920.png","credit":"FIRST Tech Challenge Docs","shows":"Logitech C920 webcam"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"limelight-3a","name":"Limelight 3A smart camera","aliases":["Limelight","LL3A","Limelight3A","MegaTag","MegaTag2","vision coprocessor"],"category":"sense","tags":["vision","camera","localization"],"entry":"sensor","family":"camera","type":null,"short":"A camera with its own processor that runs AprilTag and color pipelines on board and sends results to the robot over USB.","how":"Pipelines are set up in Limelight's web interface; the camera processes images itself and returns targets and robot pose (MegaTag1 multi-tag, or IMU-fused MegaTag2). It connects by USB-C. FTC SDK 10.0 added Limelight 3A support and 10.2 added pipeline upload. It does not use VisionPortal.","uses":["AprilTag localization","targeting for shooters","color target tracking"],"pros":["offloads vision work from the Control Hub","built-in localization"],"cons":["costs more than a webcam","different API from VisionPortal"],"failures":["wrong pipeline selected","camera pose not set in its configuration"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"pipeline","label":"Active pipeline","unit":"choice number","min":0,"max":9,"default":0,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R702","R708"],"note":"Table 12-9 under R702 lists the Limelight 3A as the allowed programmable vision coprocessor; the Limelight 3G is named as prohibited."},"refs":[{"title":"Limelight 3A Smart Camera - goBILDA","url":"https://www.gobilda.com/limelight-3a-smart-camera/","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"FtcRobotController README (release notes)","url":"https://github.com/FIRST-Tech-Challenge/FtcRobotController/blob/master/README.md","kind":"official"},{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML)","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"}],"image":{"kind":"component-photo","page":"https://www.gobilda.com/limelight-3a-smart-camera/","src":"https://cdn11.bigcommerce.com/s-x56mtydx1w/products/2237/images/13407/Ang1__01989.1727449275.386.513.jpg?c=1","credit":"goBILDA store listing","shows":"Limelight 3A camera"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"huskylens","name":"DFRobot HuskyLens vision sensor","aliases":["HuskyLens","AI camera","I2C vision sensor"],"category":"sense","tags":["vision","camera","i2c"],"entry":"sensor","family":"camera","type":null,"short":"A small AI vision sensor with a screen that learns tags, colors or objects and reports them to the robot over I2C.","how":"Algorithms such as TAG_RECOGNITION (36h11 AprilTags) and COLOR_RECOGNITION run on the device; results come back as blocks with IDs and positions. It plugs into a hub I2C bus with a custom adapter cable and is configured as a HuskyLens device. Basic support arrived in SDK 9.0. It does not use VisionPortal.","uses":["simple color or tag detection without VisionPortal"],"pros":["on-device processing","easy training with buttons"],"cons":["needs an adapter cable","colors sensitive to lighting","FTC Docs recommends a UVC webcam for AprilTag navigation"],"failures":["wrong pin order in the adapter","color IDs depending on training order"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R702","R613"],"note":"R702 examples list the DFRobot HuskyLens among allowed sensors; whole-robot legality not implied."},"refs":[{"title":"HuskyLens Intro - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/devices/huskylens/huskylens.html","kind":"official"}],"image":{"kind":"component-photo","page":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/devices/huskylens/huskylens.html","src":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/_images/020-HuskyLens-dual.png","credit":"FIRST Tech Challenge Docs","shows":"DFRobot HuskyLens"},"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"fixed-camera-mount","name":"Fixed camera mount (front, rear, side or angled)","aliases":["front camera","rear camera","side camera","angled targeting camera","camera bracket","webcam mount","tilted camera"],"category":"sense","tags":["vision","mount"],"entry":"component","family":"camera-mount","type":null,"short":"A bracket that holds a camera at a fixed place and angle on the robot; that position and angle must be entered in code for pose estimates.","how":"The camera is bolted at a chosen x, y, z and yaw/pitch/roll on the robot. For AprilTag localization, those numbers go into setCameraPose(position, orientation); pitch -90 means a horizontal lens in the SDK's convention, yaw 0 forward, +90 left. An angled (tilted) mount points the camera toward high or low targets.","uses":["AprilTag localization","targeting goals","seeing game pieces in front of an intake"],"pros":["simple and rigid","camera pose never changes"],"cons":["field of view fixed; may lose targets while turning"],"failures":["mount flexing so the pose is wrong","camera pose measured incorrectly"],"dof":0,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"x","label":"Camera X (right of center)","unit":"in","min":-9,"max":9,"default":0,"estimate":true},{"key":"y","label":"Camera Y (forward of center)","unit":"in","min":-9,"max":9,"default":7,"estimate":true},{"key":"z","label":"Camera height","unit":"in","min":2,"max":18,"default":8,"estimate":true},{"key":"yaw","label":"Yaw (0 forward, 90 left, 180 rear)","unit":"deg","min":-180,"max":180,"default":0,"estimate":true},{"key":"tilt","label":"Tilt from horizontal","unit":"deg","min":-45,"max":45,"default":0,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R708"],"note":"Camera must meet R708; the mount itself was not checked."},"refs":[{"title":"AprilTag Localization - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/apriltag/vision_portal/apriltag_localization/apriltag-localization.html","kind":"official"},{"title":"Webcams for Vision Portal - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/apriltag/vision_portal/visionportal_webcams/visionportal-webcams.html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["camera"],"motion":{"input":"none: it is bolted in place","output":"the camera held at one known pose"}},
{"id":"turret-mounted-camera","name":"Turret-mounted camera","aliases":["camera on turret","shooter camera","turret vision"],"category":"sense","tags":["vision","scoring","turret"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"camera-mount","type":"turret","preset":{"states":{"front":0,"left":90,"right":-90},"lower":-180,"upper":180,"speed":180},"short":"The camera rides on a turret so it always looks where the shooter or arm is pointing.","how":"The camera is fixed to the turret, so its yaw relative to the robot equals the turret angle. Code adds the turret angle (from its encoder) to the camera pose before computing field pose. Aiming can close the loop directly on the target's bearing in the image.","uses":["aiming a shooter at a goal tag","tracking a target while driving"],"pros":["target stays in view while the base turns"],"cons":["camera pose changes with the turret, so localization math is more complex","cable management through rotation"],"failures":["turret angle not included in pose math","cable wrap pulling the USB connector"],"dof":1,"actuation":"motor","motion":{"input":"turret rotation","output":"camera yaw"},"params":[{"key":"turretRange","label":"Turret range","unit":"deg","min":90,"max":360,"default":270,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R708"],"note":"Camera must meet R708; not otherwise checked."},"refs":[{"title":"AprilTag Localization - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/apriltag/vision_portal/apriltag_localization/apriltag-localization.html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["camera","turret encoder"]},
{"id":"wrist-mounted-camera","name":"Wrist-mounted camera","aliases":["claw camera","end-effector camera","arm camera"],"category":"sense","tags":["vision","intake"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"camera-mount","type":"wrist","preset":{"states":{"up":0,"down":90},"lower":-30,"upper":120,"speed":180},"short":"A small camera on the wrist or claw looks straight at game pieces to line up a pickup.","how":"The camera moves with the end effector, so its view is aligned with the grabber. Color locating finds a piece's position and angle in the image, and code nudges the wrist or drive to center it. Its pose relative to the robot depends on arm and wrist angles.","uses":["picking up pieces lying at odd angles","fine alignment before grabbing"],"pros":["close, clear view of the target"],"cons":["vibration and motion blur","cable runs along a moving arm"],"failures":["USB cable fatigue at the joints","camera blocked by the piece itself"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"wrist servo","output":"camera pitch with the claw"},"params":[],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R708"],"note":"Camera must meet R708."},"refs":[{"title":"Color Processing Overview - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/color_processing/overview/overview.html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":1,"sensors":["camera"]},
{"id":"camera-pan","name":"Camera pan mount","aliases":["pan servo","camera yaw","rotating camera mount"],"category":"sense","tags":["vision","mount"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"camera-mount","type":"camera-mount","preset":{"states":{"level":0,"down":30},"lower":-90,"upper":90,"speed":240},"short":"A servo swings the camera left and right so it can look toward targets the robot is not facing.","how":"The camera sits on a servo horn or small turntable. Code sets the servo to an angle and adds that angle to the camera's yaw when computing poses. It can scan for tags or keep a target centered.","uses":["finding AprilTags to the side","tracking a target"],"pros":["wider coverage with one camera"],"cons":["camera pose changes; must be known exactly","servo backlash adds pose error"],"failures":["servo angle not matching the commanded angle","loose mount wobbling"],"dof":1,"actuation":"servo","motion":{"input":"servo rotation","output":"camera yaw"},"params":[{"key":"panRange","label":"Pan range","unit":"deg","min":60,"max":270,"default":180,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R503","R708"],"note":"Servo counts toward R503; camera must meet R708."},"refs":[{"title":"AprilTag Localization - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/apriltag/vision_portal/apriltag_localization/apriltag-localization.html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":1,"sensors":["camera"]},
{"id":"camera-pan-tilt","name":"Pan-and-tilt camera mount","aliases":["two-axis camera mount"],"category":"sense","tags":["vision","mount"],"entry":"subassembly","family":"camera-mount","parent":"pan-tilt-platform","type":"camera-mount","preset":{"states":{"level":0,"down":30},"lower":-60,"upper":60,"speed":240},"short":"Two servos turn the camera left/right and up/down so it can point almost anywhere.","how":"A pan servo carries a tilt servo, which carries the camera. Both servo angles feed into the camera's yaw and pitch. Useful when targets are at different heights, but each extra joint adds pose uncertainty.","uses":["tracking high and low targets"],"pros":["very wide coverage"],"cons":["two servos","more pose error and more to calibrate"],"failures":["servo jitter blurring images","cable snagging"],"dof":2,"actuation":"servo+servo","motion":{"input":"pan and tilt servos","output":"camera yaw and pitch"},"params":[{"key":"tiltRange","label":"Tilt range","unit":"deg","min":30,"max":180,"default":90,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R503","R708"],"note":"Two servos count toward R503."},"refs":[{"title":"AprilTag Localization - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/apriltag/vision_portal/apriltag_localization/apriltag-localization.html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"low","status":"experimental","verified":"2026-11-06","servos":2,"sensors":["camera"]},
{"id":"apriltag-detection","name":"AprilTag detection (VisionPortal)","aliases":["AprilTag","AprilTagProcessor","ftcPose","fiducial detection","36h11"],"category":"sense","tags":["vision","software","autonomous"],"entry":"software","family":"vision","type":null,"short":"The FTC SDK finds square black-and-white AprilTags in the camera image and reports each tag's ID, distance and angle.","how":"An AprilTagProcessor runs inside VisionPortal. For tags in its library (known size), it returns ftcPose: x, y, z, pitch, roll, yaw plus range, bearing and elevation. The whole tag must be in view and the camera needs calibration for the resolution. BIOBUZZ uses 3.25 in 36h11 tags, IDs 30-45, on the bottoms of the CELLs.","uses":["reading randomization","driving to a scoring location","checking position"],"pros":["fast (about 15-20 detections per second) and robust to lighting","built into the SDK with samples"],"cons":["tag must be fully visible","pose needs calibrated cameras"],"failures":["motion blur","tag partly blocked","wrong tag size in the library"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"decimation","label":"Decimation","unit":"x","min":1,"max":4,"default":2,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R708"],"note":"Uses an R708-compliant USB camera; BIOBUZZ section 9.9 defines the field tags."},"refs":[{"title":"AprilTag Introduction - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/apriltag/vision_portal/apriltag_intro/apriltag-intro.html","kind":"official"},{"title":"AprilTag Pose - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/apriltag/vision_portal/apriltag_pose/apriltag-pose.html","kind":"official"},{"title":"BIOBUZZ Competition Manual (HTML)","url":"https://ftc-resources.firstinspires.org/ftc/game/cm-html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["camera"]},
{"id":"apriltag-localization","name":"AprilTag localization (robot field pose)","aliases":["robotPose","ConceptAprilTagLocalization","tag-based localization","setCameraPose"],"category":"sense","tags":["vision","localization","software"],"entry":"software","family":"vision","parent":"apriltag-detection","type":null,"short":"Turns an AprilTag sighting into the robot's position and heading on the field, using the tag's known location and the camera's spot on the robot.","how":"Since SDK 10.0, the AprilTagProcessor takes setCameraPose(position, orientation). Combining the tag's pose relative to the camera, the tag's stored field pose and the camera's pose on the robot gives detection.robotPose (x, y, z, yaw, pitch, roll). Accuracy depends on measuring the camera pose correctly.","uses":["resetting odometry","autonomous start position check"],"pros":["absolute field position, no drift"],"cons":["needs a tag in view","noisy at long range"],"failures":["camera axes entered wrong","tag library missing the current season's tags"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[],"seasons":["general","biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R708"],"note":"Uses an R708-compliant camera; whole-robot legality not implied."},"refs":[{"title":"AprilTag Localization - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/apriltag/vision_portal/apriltag_localization/apriltag-localization.html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["camera"]},
{"id":"color-recognition","name":"Camera color recognition (Color Sensor and Color Locator processors)","aliases":["color processing","ColorBlobLocatorProcessor","PredominantColorProcessor","blob detection","OpenCV color","EasyOpenCV"],"category":"sense","tags":["vision","software","object-detection"],"entry":"software","family":"vision","type":null,"short":"The FTC SDK can report the main color in part of the camera image, or find blobs of a chosen color and give their size and position.","how":"Since SDK 10.1, VisionPortal includes a Color Sensor processor (predominant color in a region) and a Color Locator processor (blobs of a target color, filtered and sorted by area, density or aspect ratio, with circle fitting). They run alongside AprilTag detection on UVC webcams; HuskyLens and Limelight do not use them.","uses":["finding game pieces","checking piece color before scoring"],"pros":["no tags needed on objects","built-in samples in Blocks and Java"],"cons":["sensitive to lighting","reports where in the image, not field position"],"failures":["thresholds tuned under different lights","background objects of the same color"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[],"seasons":["general","biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R708"],"note":"Runs on R708-compliant webcams."},"refs":[{"title":"Color Processing Overview - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/color_processing/overview/overview.html","kind":"official"},{"title":"Computer Vision Overview - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/programming_resources/vision/vision_overview/vision-overview.html","kind":"official"}],"image":{"kind":"illustration","page":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/programming_resources/vision/vision_overview/vision-overview.html","src":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/_images/33-circleFit-LiveView1.png","credit":"FIRST Tech Challenge Docs (screenshot of a Color Locator circle fit)"},"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"high","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["camera"]},
{"id":"vision-assisted-aiming","name":"Vision-assisted aiming","aliases":["auto aim","drive to AprilTag","RobotAutoDriveToAprilTag","target lock","bearing alignment"],"category":"sense","tags":["vision","scoring","software"],"entry":"software","family":"vision","type":null,"short":"Uses the camera's measured angle and distance to a target to turn, strafe or adjust a shooter automatically.","how":"From an AprilTag's ftcPose, bearing drives turning, yaw drives strafing and range drives forward/back to a standoff distance, each through a proportional gain; the SDK's tank sample uses range and bearing only. For shooters, range can also pick flywheel speed or hood angle.","uses":["lining up with a goal","holding aim while the driver moves"],"pros":["fast and repeatable alignment"],"cons":["needs the target in view","gains need tuning to avoid oscillation"],"failures":["loop oscillation","losing the tag mid-move"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"standoff","label":"Desired range","unit":"in","min":6,"max":72,"default":12,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general","biobuzz"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":["R708"],"note":"Camera rules apply; game rules on automated aiming were not checked."},"refs":[{"title":"AprilTag Pose - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/apriltag/vision_portal/apriltag_pose/apriltag-pose.html","kind":"official"},{"title":"AprilTag Introduction - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/apriltag/vision_portal/apriltag_intro/apriltag-intro.html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["camera"]},
{"id":"octoquad","name":"OctoQuad encoder and PWM interface","aliases":["OctoQuad","OctoQuad FTC Edition","OctoQuad MK2","encoder expander"],"category":"sense","tags":["encoder","i2c","localization"],"entry":"sensor","family":"encoder","type":null,"short":"An I2C board that reads up to eight encoders or PWM absolute encoders at once and returns them in one fast read.","how":"Up to 8 inputs count quadrature encoders or measure pulse widths (1 to 65535 us), including the REV Through Bore's absolute PWM output. The MK2 adds an IMU and an onboard dead-wheel localizer. All data comes back in one I2C bulk read with checksums. SDK 9.2 added OctoQuad support; the MK2 needs its own driver per the seller.","uses":["reading absolute steering encoders","more encoder ports","odometry"],"pros":["reads PWM absolute encoders the hub cannot","one fast bulk read"],"cons":["3.3 V only","driver must match firmware"],"failures":["5 V signals damaging inputs","driver/firmware mismatch"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"rule-checked","rules":["R702","R613"],"note":"R702 examples list the OctoQuad FTC Edition as allowed; whole-robot legality not implied."},"refs":[{"title":"OctoQuad FTC Edition MK2 - Tindie","url":"https://www.tindie.com/products/digitalchickenlabs/octoquad-ftc-ed-mk2-8x-encoderpwm-imu/","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"FtcRobotController README (release notes)","url":"https://github.com/FIRST-Tech-Challenge/FtcRobotController/blob/master/README.md","kind":"official"}],"image":{"kind":"component-photo","page":"https://www.tindie.com/products/digitalchickenlabs/octoquad-ftc-ed-mk2-8x-encoderpwm-imu/","src":"https://img.tindie.com/images/resize/bFPvBQRWnsoWWMAng1wdRAjEBNw=/p/fit-in/1032x688/filters:fill(fff)/i/730853/products/2025-03-08T23:58:39.498Z-MK2_CaseClosed.jpg?1741449693","credit":"Digital Chicken Labs Tindie listing","shows":"OctoQuad MK2 in its case"},"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"sensor-fusion","name":"Sensor fusion","aliases":["fusion","Kalman filter","complementary filter","multi-sensor estimate"],"category":"sense","tags":["software","localization"],"entry":"software","family":"fusion","type":null,"short":"Combining readings from several sensors so each covers the others' weaknesses, such as odometry plus IMU plus camera.","how":"Fast but drifting sensors (encoders, gyro) are blended with slower absolute ones (AprilTags, distance to walls). A complementary filter weights them by trust; a Kalman filter also tracks uncertainty. Devices like Pinpoint, OTOS and Limelight MegaTag2 already fuse IMU data on board.","uses":["localization","reliable game-piece detection using two sensors"],"pros":["more accurate and robust than any single sensor"],"cons":["more tuning and math","timing between sensors matters"],"failures":["trusting a bad sensor too much","time skew between readings"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"visionWeight","label":"Vision correction weight","unit":"0-1","min":0,"max":1,"default":0.2,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Software only."},"refs":[{"title":"Pinpoint Odometry Computer - goBILDA","url":"https://www.gobilda.com/pinpoint-odometry-computer-imu-sensor-fusion-for-2-wheel-odometry/","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"SparkFun Optical Tracking Odometry Sensor - SparkFun","url":"https://www.sparkfun.com/sparkfun-optical-tracking-odometry-sensor-paa5160e1-qwiic.html","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"Limelight 3A Smart Camera - goBILDA","url":"https://www.gobilda.com/limelight-3a-smart-camera/","kind":"vendor"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"advanced","confidence":"medium","status":"current","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"encoder-homing","name":"Encoder homing (hard-stop or stall homing)","aliases":["homing","zeroing","stall homing","current homing","encoder reset"],"category":"sense","tags":["software","calibration","lift"],"entry":"software","family":"homing","type":null,"short":"Drives a slide or arm gently to a known stop at startup and resets its encoder there, so positions are correct even after power cycles.","how":"Relative encoders start at zero wherever the mechanism is at power-up. A homing routine drives slowly toward a hard stop until velocity drops to near zero (or motor current rises), then calls STOP_AND_RESET_ENCODER. After that, encoder targets map to real positions.","uses":["lifts and slides","arms with a resting stop"],"pros":["no extra sensor"],"cons":["hits the stop every time","slow if not tuned"],"failures":["detecting a stall from friction, not the stop","driving too hard into the stop"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"homingPower","label":"Homing power","unit":"0-1","min":0.05,"max":0.5,"default":0.2,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Software only."},"refs":[{"title":"SDK Motors - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/software/adv-control-system/sdk-motors.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"Encoders - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/software/tutorials/encoders.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["motor encoder"]},
{"id":"limit-switch-homing","name":"Limit-switch homing","aliases":["switch homing","home switch","zero on limit"],"category":"sense","tags":["software","calibration","lift"],"entry":"software","family":"homing","parent":"encoder-homing","type":null,"short":"Resets a mechanism's encoder whenever a touch or magnetic limit switch shows it is at home.","how":"A touch or magnetic switch is mounted at the home position. Code drives the mechanism toward it until the digital input changes, stops, and resets the encoder; many teams also reset every time the switch is hit during the match to remove drift. Limit switches should be wired between ground and the digital pin.","uses":["lifts and slides","turrets"],"pros":["repeatable zero without hitting a hard stop"],"cons":["needs a sensor and wiring"],"failures":["switch missed at high speed","magnet moved so home shifts"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Software plus a standard sensor."},"refs":[{"title":"SDK Sensors - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/software/adv-control-system/sdk-sensors.html","kind":"gm0"},{"title":"SDK Motors - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/software/adv-control-system/sdk-motors.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["touch or magnetic limit switch","motor encoder"]},
{"id":"mechanical-calibration-reference","name":"Mechanical calibration reference (hard stop or jig)","aliases":["hard stop","alignment jig","starting jig","index mark","reference stop"],"category":"sense","tags":["calibration","concept"],"entry":"concept","family":"homing","type":null,"short":"A physical stop or jig that puts a mechanism or the whole robot in a known position before the code starts counting.","how":"A hard stop, pin, or printed jig sets a joint or the robot's starting pose exactly. Software assumes that pose at init and resets encoders or odometry there. Repeatable physical setup makes autonomous and presets match every match.","uses":["autonomous start alignment","arm and servo zero"],"pros":["cheap and reliable"],"cons":["relies on the team placing it correctly"],"failures":["robot placed slightly off the jig","stop bending over time"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Game rules on placing robots and using alignment aids were not checked."},"refs":[{"title":"Encoders - Game Manual 0","url":"https://gm0.org/en/latest/docs/software/tutorials/encoders.html","kind":"gm0"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"beginner","confidence":"low","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06"},
{"id":"sensor-auto-alignment","name":"Sensor-based auto alignment","aliases":["auto align","wall alignment","distance alignment","square up","target alignment"],"category":"sense","tags":["software","alignment","drive"],"entry":"software","family":"alignment","type":null,"short":"The robot uses sensors to line itself up with a wall, line or target automatically before scoring.","how":"Two distance sensors on one side give the angle to a wall from their difference and the distance from their average; a P loop drives both errors to zero. Similar loops use color sensors for lines or AprilTag bearing and range for targets.","uses":["squaring to a wall before scoring","approaching a goal at a set distance"],"pros":["consistent positioning","faster than driver adjustment"],"cons":["sensor noise and field variation"],"failures":["sensor seeing another robot","oscillation from high gains"],"dof":null,"actuation":"none","params":[{"key":"sensorSpacing","label":"Spacing between distance sensors","unit":"in","min":4,"max":16,"default":10,"estimate":true}],"seasons":["general"],"legality":{"status":"not-verified","rules":[],"note":"Software using allowed sensors; not otherwise checked."},"refs":[{"title":"2m Distance Sensor - REV Robotics","url":"https://www.revrobotics.com/rev-31-1505/","kind":"vendor"},{"title":"AprilTag Pose - FTC Docs","url":"https://ftc-docs.firstinspires.org/en/latest/apriltag/vision_portal/apriltag_pose/apriltag-pose.html","kind":"official"}],"image":null,"difficulty":"intermediate","confidence":"medium","status":"common","verified":"2026-11-06","sensors":["distance sensors","camera"]}
